Below is a **complete, security‑oriented working example** that adds  

* a **single‑upload queue** – only one user may be uploading a file at any given time; the next uploader must press a “Start upload” button before the server will accept any of his chunks, and the previous uploader must press a “Finish upload” button to release the lock,  
* a **download endpoint** that only serves files that belong to the requesting user, and  
* a short security review of the original prototype plus hardening recommendations.

The solution is deliberately kept **framework‑only** (Flask + the Python standard library) so you can see exactly what is happening.  When you move to production you would normally replace the in‑memory queue with a persistent broker (Redis, RabbitMQ, …) and add proper authentication (Flask‑Login, OAuth, etc.).  All the concepts stay the same.

---

## 1.  High‑level design

| Component | Responsibility |
|-----------|-----------------|
| **Upload lock** (`upload_lock = threading.Lock()`) | Guarantees that at most one upload is being processed. |
| **Queue** (`collections.deque`) | Stores waiting user IDs (or a random token) in FIFO order. |
| **Session‑based token** (`session["upload_token"]`) | Identifies the *owner* of the lock so that only the right user can release it. |
| **Upload metadata DB** (`UPLOADS = {token: {"filename": …, "size": …}}`) | Tracks files that have been successfully assembled – used later for downloading. |
| **Download route** (`/download/<token>`) | Streams the file only after verifying that the current user owns the token. |
| **Front‑end** | Two extra buttons – “Enter queue / Start upload” and “Finish upload” – that drive the queue via Ajax calls. |

