from dotenv import load_dotenv
load_dotenv()
```

In production you would put the key into your server’s secret manager (AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Docker secret, etc.) and **never expose** it to the client.

---

## 4. Security & Hardening Checklist

| Area | Why it matters | How to mitigate |
|------|----------------|-----------------|
| **API Key Leakage** | If the key is sent to the browser, anyone can abuse your OpenAI quota. | Keep the key **only** in the server process (`os.getenv`). Do not render it into HTML or JS. |
| **CORS** | Open endpoints can be called from any site (XSS). | Restrict `CORS(app, origins=[your‑domain])` in production. |
| **Input Validation** | Malicious users could send huge payloads or malicious prompts. | - Enforce max length (e.g., `len(text) < 2000`). <br> - Strip dangerous characters if you ever embed user text into system prompts (use triple‑quoted strings). |
| **Rate Limiting** | Prevent denial‑of‑service or excessive billing. | Use Flask‑Limiter or an API‑gateway that throttles per IP/USER. |
| **CSRF** | A malicious site could POST to `/save_chapter`. | Use CSRF tokens (Flask‑WTF) or require a custom header (`X-Requested-With: XMLHttpRequest`). |
| **Error Handling** | Stack traces may reveal internal details. | Return generic error messages; log the real exception to server logs only. |
| **HTTPS** | Prevent eavesdropping on user data and API key. | Deploy behind a TLS terminator (NGINX, Cloudflare). |
| **Logging & Auditing** | Track usage for cost management. | Log request timestamps, token usage (`response.usage.total_tokens`). Ensure logs do **not** contain the API key. |
| **Data Persistence** | Saved chapters are stored on the server. | Store them outside the web root (e.g., `saved_chapters/`) and serve only via the `/save_chapter` endpoint. Clean up old files regularly. |

---

