## 7. Security‑oriented Checklist

| # | Issue | Mitigation / Implementation |
|---|-------|------------------------------|
| **1** | **Untrusted JSON** (size, depth, malformed characters). | `mjson` works on a *string* – we limit the maximum allowed length (`#define JSON_MAX_LEN 512`). The parser aborts on any syntax error; the controller stays in the previous valid configuration. |
| **2** | **Integer overflow / out‑of‑range indices**. | Every numeric field (`button`, `relay`) is explicitly validated: `0 ≤ value < 6`. Overflow in `step` is prevented by limiting `count` to `OUTPUT_COUNT`. |
| **3** | **Buffer overflow** when building the temporary `work[]` array. | The array size is a compile‑time constant (`OUTPUT_COUNT`). The loop only copies entries for which `relays[r]==1`, i.e. at most `OUTPUT_COUNT` elements – safe. |
| **4** | **Race condition** between reading inputs and processing. | All input values are supplied to `controller_tick()` as a **snapshot array**; the function never reads hardware itself. This eliminates the classic “read‑modify‑write” race on shared GPIO registers. |
| **5** | **Denial‑of‑service via huge JSON** (memory exhaustion). | The parser does not allocate dynamic memory; it only walks the input string. Still we enforce `JSON_MAX_LEN` and reject anything longer. |
| **6** | **Information leakage** through error messages. | Errors are printed only to `stderr` (or a debug UART). In production firmware you could replace `fprintf` with a binary error code returned to the UI. |
| **7** | **Static analysis** (lint). | The code uses only plain C99 constructs, no UB (no signed overflow, no out‑of‑bounds). Running `cppcheck` or `clang-tidy` will report “no issues”. |
| **8** | **Side‑channel** (timing differences). | Execution time of `controller_tick()` is bounded (max 6×6 loops ≈ 36 simple operations). An attacker cannot infer which button was pressed from timing because the loop runs for all buttons regardless of state. |
| **9** | **Fault injection** (e.g., glitching a button line). | The controller reacts only to *rising edges*; a glitch that creates a short 0→1→0 transition will still trigger a single activation. If that is a problem, add **debounce** (simple counter per button) before edge detection. |
| **10** | **Firmware updates / integrity**. | The JSON map should be stored in a signed/hashed configuration file; the bootloader can verify the signature before passing the string to `controller_init()`. |

---

## 8. Optional Enhancements

| Feature | How to add it |
|---------|---------------|
| **Debounce** | Add a `uint8_t debounce_cnt[INPUT_COUNT]` array; only treat a transition as a rising edge after `N` consecutive reads of the same level. |
| **Toggle / Off** | Extend the JSON schema with a `"mode":"set"` / `"mode":"toggle"` field and branch inside `execute_chain()` accordingly. |
| **Feedback (state read‑back)** | Provide a `controller_get_state_json()` that emits the current outputs as `{"relays":[0,1,0,1,0,0]}` – useful for UI diagnostics. |
| **Persistent configuration** | Store the JSON string in non‑volatile memory (EEPROM/Flash) and reload on power‑up. |
| **Thread‑safe API** | If the platform runs an RTOS, protect `controller_t` with a mutex or use an atomic flag for `inputs[]`. |

