Below is a **step‑by‑step security‑oriented review of the program**, followed by a **clean, compile‑ready rewrite** that eliminates the bugs and the obvious security problems.

---

## 1️⃣  High‑level functional description (what the program *should* do)

* Read the number of processes (`n`).
* For each process read its **burst time**.
* Sort the burst times in **descending order** (FCFS with longest‑first, as the original author intended).
* Compute, for each process:
  * **Waiting time** – the sum of burst times of all processes that run before it.
  * **Turn‑around time** – `burst + waiting`.
* Print the per‑process values and the **average waiting time**.

---

## 2️⃣  Security‑ and correctness‑oriented bug list

| # | Bug / Vulnerability | Why it matters | Fix |
|---|---------------------|----------------|-----|
| 1 | **`void main()`** | Non‑standard; on many platforms the return value is ignored and the stack may be corrupted. | Use `int main(void)` and `return 0;`. |
| 2 | **Static array `p[10]` vs user input `n`** | If the user types `n > 10` you write past the end of `p`, corrupting memory → *buffer overflow*. | Either limit `n` (`if (n>10) n=10;`) **or** allocate `p` dynamically (`malloc`). |
| 3 | **Variable‑length array `int ar[n];`** (C99‑only) | Not portable to pre‑C99 compilers; also the same overflow problem as #2. | Dynamically allocate (`int *ar = malloc(n * sizeof *ar);`). |
| 4 | **Sorting loop typo** `for(int h=0;h<n-1;h+1)` | The loop never increments → infinite loop (or the optimizer may remove it). | Change to `h++`. |
| 5 | **Bubble‑sort inner bound** `for(int i=0;i<n-1;i++)` | When `h` runs to `n‑2` you compare `ar[i]` and `ar[i+1]`. This works, but the outer loop should run `n‑1` times (or `n` times for safety). | Keep as‑is after fixing outer increment, but optionally write a clearer sort routine. |
| 6 | **Sorting only `ar`, not the process structs** | After sorting `ar` the `p[i]` entries still contain the original, unsorted burst times. The later test `if(p[i].bursttime==ar[i])` only works **by accident** when burst times are unique and already in the same order. | Keep the whole `struct` together while sorting (swap both `p[i]` and `p[i+1]`). |
| 7 | **No check on `scanf` return value** | If the user types a non‑numeric string `scanf` fails, leaving the variable uninitialized → undefined behaviour. | Test `if (scanf("%d",&n)!=1) exit(EXIT_FAILURE);` (same for burst time). |
| 8 | **Integer division for average** `float average=j/n;` | `j` and `n` are `int`; the division happens in integer arithmetic, then converted → loss of precision. | Cast one operand: `float average = (float)j / n;`. |
| 9 | **Unclear variable `j`** | `j` accumulates waiting times, but the name gives no hint. It also shadows the usual loop index style. | Rename to `total_waiting`. |
|10 | **Potential signed‑overflow** (rare with small inputs) | If `bursttime` can be huge, `waitingtime1 = p[i].totaltime;` may overflow `int`. Use `long long` for safety. | Change all time fields to `long long`. |
|11 | **Missing `#include <stdlib.h>`** (for `malloc`, `exit`) | Compilation warning / error. | Add include. |
|12 | **Missing `#include <stdbool.h>`** (optional) | Not needed now. | — |
|13 | **Use of magic numbers (`10`)** | Hard‑coded limit makes the program fragile. | Define a constant (`#define MAX_PROC 256`) or allocate dynamically. |

---

## 3️⃣  Secure‑by‑design rewrite

Below is a **complete, self‑contained program** that:

* Uses **dynamic allocation** for the process array, so `n` can be any reasonable positive value.
* Sorts **the whole structure**, preserving the association between a process and its burst time.
* Performs **full input validation**.
* Avoids all the issues listed above.
* Uses **`long long`** for all time calculations (preventing overflow for very large burst times).
* Returns an appropriate exit status.

```c
/*  fcfs.c - First‑Come‑First‑Served (Longest‑burst‑first) scheduler
 *  Demonstrates safe, standards‑compliant C code.
 *
 *  Compilation (gcc):
 *      gcc -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -std=c11 fcfs.c -o fcfs
 *
 *  Run:
 *      ./fcfs
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <errno.h>

#define MAX_INPUT_LEN 64      /* safe limit for scanf buffers */

typedef struct {
    long long burst;      /* burst time of the process */
    long long waiting;    /* waiting time (computed) */
    long long turnaround;/* burst + waiting */
} Process;

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Helper: read an integer safely (returns 0 on success, non‑zero on error)
   --------------------------------------------------------------*/
static int read_int(const char *prompt, int *out)
{
    char buf[MAX_INPUT_LEN];
    long val;
    char *endptr;

    printf("%s", prompt);
    if (!fgets(buf, sizeof buf, stdin))
        return -1;                     /* EOF or read error */

    errno = 0;
    val = strtol(buf, &endptr, 10);
    if (errno != 0 || endptr == buf || *endptr != '\n')
        return -1;                     /* not a valid integer */

    if (val < 0 || val > INT_MAX)
        return -1;                     /* out of range for int */

    *out = (int)val;
    return 0;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Swap two Process elements (used by bubble sort)
   --------------------------------------------------------------*/
static void swap_process(Process *a, Process *b)
{
    Process tmp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = tmp;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Simple bubble‑sort in *descending* order of burst time.
   (FCFS with longest burst first – this matches the original intent.)
   --------------------------------------------------------------*/
static void sort_descending(Process *proc, int n)
{
    int swapped;
    do {
        swapped = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i) {
            if (proc[i].burst < proc[i + 1].burst) {
                swap_process(&proc[i], &proc[i + 1]);
                swapped = 1;
            }
        }
    } while (swapped);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Main – clean, portable and secure
   --------------------------------------------------------------*/
int main(void)
{
    int n = 0;

    /* ---------- read number of processes --------------------- */
    if (read_int("Enter the number of processes: ", &n) != 0 || n <= 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid number of processes.\n");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    /* ---------- allocate space for all processes ------------- */
    Process *proc = calloc((size_t)n, sizeof *proc);
    if (!proc) {
        perror("calloc");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    /* ---------- read burst times ------------------------------ */
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
