**Solution Overview**

We need a self‑contained C++ program (no `std::vector`) that can work with signed integers whose absolute value does not exceed `10^30`.  
The easiest way to meet the “no vector” restriction is to store every decimal digit in a **fixed‑size `char` array**.  
All operations are then performed manually on that array, exactly as you would do on paper.

The program below implements a tiny **`BigInt`** class that

* keeps the digits in **little‑endian** order – the digit with index 0 is the least significant one;
* stores the sign in a separate `bool` (`true` = negative);
* provides the four arithmetic operations required:
  * `operator+`
  * `operator-`
  * `operator*`
  * `operator/` (integer division, truncates toward zero);
* can be constructed from a decimal string and can be printed back to a string;
* works for numbers up to `10^30` (31 characters are enough: 30 digits + possible leading zero).

All arithmetic is performed **digit by digit** using only ordinary integer variables – no dynamic containers, no `std::vector`, no external libraries.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

## 1.  Data layout

```cpp
struct BigInt {
    static const int MAX_DIGITS = 31;   // 30 digits + one spare for carry
    char d[MAX_DIGITS];                 // decimal digits, d[0] = LSD
    bool neg;                           // true → negative
    int  len;                           // number of used digits (0 … MAX_DIGITS‑1)
};
```

*`d[i]`* always contains a value in the range `0 … 9`.  
`len` tells how many low‑order digits are actually relevant (leading zeros are never stored).  
If the number is zero we keep `len = 0` and `neg = false`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

## 2.  Helper functions

| Function | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `static int cmpAbs(const BigInt&, const BigInt&)` | compare absolute values (`-1,0,+1`). |
| `void trim()` | remove leading zero digits, fix sign for zero. |
| `static void addAbs(const BigInt&, const BigInt&, BigInt&)` | `|a| + |b|` (sign‑less addition). |
| `static void subAbs(const BigInt&, const BigInt&, BigInt&)` | `|a| - |b|`, assumes `|a| ≥ |b|`. |
| `static void mulAbs(const BigInt&, const BigInt&, BigInt&)` | absolute‑value multiplication (grade‑school O(n²)). |
| `static void divAbs(const BigInt&, const BigInt&, BigInt&, BigInt*)` | long division, returns quotient and optionally remainder. |

All helpers work only with the digit array, never touch the sign flag.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

## 3.  Core operators

* **Addition** – if the signs are equal we just add the absolute values; otherwise we subtract the smaller absolute value from the larger one and copy the sign of the larger operand.
* **Subtraction** – implemented as `a + (-b)`.
* **Multiplication** – sign is the XOR of the operand signs; the absolute value is obtained with `mulAbs`.
* **Division** – sign of the result is again XOR of signs.  The algorithm is the classic “long division” performed digit by digit from the most significant side.

All operators return a **new `BigInt`** (the class is cheap to copy because it contains only a small fixed array).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

## 4.  Full source code

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct BigInt {
    static const int MAX_DIGITS = 31;          // 30 decimal digits + 1 guard
    char d[MAX_DIGITS];                        // digits, d[0] = least significant
    bool neg;                                  // true → negative
    int  len;                                  // number of used digits (0 … MAX_DIGITS-1)

    // ---------- constructors ----------
    BigInt() : neg(false), len(0) { memset(d, 0, sizeof d); }

    // from decimal string (may start with '+' or '-')
    explicit BigInt(const string& s) {
        memset(d, 0, sizeof d);
        neg = false; len = 0;
        size_t pos = 0;
        if (s[pos] == '-') { neg = true; ++pos; }
        else if (s[pos] == '+') ++pos;

        // skip leading zeros
        while (pos < s.size() && s[pos] == '0') ++pos;
        if (pos == s.size()) {               // the number is zero
            neg = false;
            len = 0;
            return;
        }

        // store digits in reverse order
        for (size_t i = s.size(); i > pos; --i) {
            d[len++] = s[i-1] - '0';
        }
        trim();
    }

    // ---------- utility ----------
    void trim() {                     // drops high‑order zeroes, fixes sign of zero
        while (len > 0 && d[len-1] == 0) --len;
        if (len == 0) neg = false;
    }

    string toString() const {
        if (len == 0) return "0";
        string s;
        if (neg) s.push_back('-');
        for (int i = len-1; i >= 0; --i) s.push_back(d[i] + '0');
        return s;
    }

    // ---------- absolute compare ----------
    static int cmpAbs(const BigInt& a, const BigInt& b) {
        if (a.len != b.len) return a.len < b.len ? -1 : 1;
        for (int i = a.len-1; i >= 0; --i)
            if (a.d[i] != b.d[i]) return a.d[i] < b.d[i] ? -1 : 1;
        return 0;
    }

    // ---------- absolute addition ----------
    // res = |a| + |b|
    static void addAbs(const BigInt& a, const BigInt& b, BigInt& res) {
        int carry = 0;
        int maxlen = max(a.len, b.len);
        for (int i = 0; i < maxlen || carry; ++i) {
            int cur = carry;
            if (i < a.len) cur += a.d[i];
            if (i < b.len) cur += b.d[i];
            res.d[i] = cur % 10;
            carry = cur / 10;
        }
        res.len = maxlen;
        if (carry) res.d[res.len++] = carry;
        res.neg = false;
        res.trim();
    }

    // ---------- absolute subtraction ----------
    // precondition: |a| >= |b|
    static void subAbs(const BigInt& a, const BigInt& b, BigInt& res) {
        int borrow = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < a.len; ++i) {
            int cur = a.d[i] - borrow - (i < b.len ? b.d[i] : 0);
            if (cur < 0) { cur += 10; borrow = 1; }
            else borrow = 0;
            res.d[i] = cur;
        }
        res.len = a.len;
        res.neg = false;
        res.trim();
    }

    // ---------- absolute multiplication ----------
    static void mulAbs(const BigInt& a, const BigInt& b, BigInt& res) {
        memset(res.d, 0, sizeof res.d);
        for (int i = 0; i < a.len; ++i) {
            int carry = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < b.len || carry; ++j) {
                long long cur = res.d[i+j] + 1LL * a.d[i] * (j < b.len ? b.d[j] : 0) + carry;
                res.d[i+j] = int(cur % 10);
                carry = int(cur / 10);
            }
        }
        res.len = a.len + b.len + 1;
        res.trim();
        res.neg = false;
    }

    // ---------- absolute division (long division) ----------
    // returns quotient in q, remainder optionally in r
    static void divAbs(const BigInt& a, const BigInt& b, BigInt& q, BigInt* r = nullptr) {
        // division by zero is undefined – we simply leave q zero
        if (b.len == 0) { q = BigInt(); return; }

        q = BigInt();
        BigInt cur;               // current remainder, built from most significant side
        cur.len = 0;
        for (int i = a.len-1; i >= 0; --i) {
            // shift cur left by one decimal digit
            if (cur.len > 0) {
                for (int k = cur.len; k > 0; --k) cur.d[k] = cur.d[k-1];
                ++cur.len;
            } else {
                cur.len = 1;
            }
            cur.d[0] = a.d[i];
            cur.trim();

            // find maximal digit x such that b * x <= cur
            int x = 0, lo = 0, hi = 9;
            while (lo <= hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
                BigInt t = b;
                // t = b * mid (mid ≤ 9 → simple repeated addition)
                t.neg = false;
                for (int k = 0; k < mid; ++k) {
                    BigInt tmp;
                    addAbs(t, b, tmp);
                    t = tmp;
                }
                int cmp = cmpAbs(t, cur);
                if (cmp <= 0) { x = mid; lo = mid + 1; }
                else          { hi = mid - 1; }
            }

            // q digit
            if (q.len == 0 && x == 0) {
                // leading zero – skip
            } else {
                // shift q left by one digit and add x
                if (q.len > 0) {
                    for (int k = q.len; k > 0; --k) q.d[k] = q.d[k-1];
                    ++q.len;
                } else {
                    q.len = 1;
                }
                q.d[0] = x;
            }

            // cur = cur - b * x
            if (x != 0) {
                BigInt bx = b;
                bx.neg = false;
                // multiply b by single digit x
                int carry = 0;
                for (int k = 0; k < bx.len || carry; ++k) {
                    int curv = (k < bx.len ? bx.d[k] : 0) * x + carry;
                    if (k < bx.len) bx.d[k] = curv % 10;
                    else bx.d[bx.len++] = curv % 10;
                    carry = curv / 10;
                }
                bx.trim();

                BigInt newCur;
                subAbs(cur, bx, newCur);
                cur = newCur;
            }
        }
        q.trim();
        if (r) { *r = cur; }
    }

    // ---------- public operators ----------
    friend BigInt operator+(const BigInt& a, const BigInt& b) {
        BigInt res;
        if (a.neg == b.neg) {                 // same sign → add abs
            addAbs(a, b, res);
            res.neg = a.neg;
        } else {                              // different signs → subtract
            int cmp = cmpAbs(a, b);
            if (cmp == 0) { res = BigInt(); }
            else if (cmp > 0) {               // |a| > |b|
                subAbs(a, b, res);
                res.neg = a.neg;
            } else {                          // |b| > |a|
                subAbs(b, a, res);
                res.neg = b.neg;
            }
        }
        res.trim();
        return res;
    }

    friend BigInt operator-(const BigInt& a, const BigInt& b) {
        BigInt nb = b;
        nb.neg = !b.neg;          // a - b == a + (-b)
        return a + nb;
    }

    friend BigInt operator*(const BigInt& a, const BigInt& b) {
        BigInt res;
        mulAbs(a, b, res);
        res.neg = (a.neg != b.neg) && (res.len != 0);
        return res;
    }

    friend BigInt operator/(const BigInt& a, const BigInt& b) {
        BigInt q, r;
        divAbs(a, b, q, &r);
        q.neg = (a.neg != b.neg) && (q.len != 0);
        return q;
    }

    // optional: remainder operator (useful for testing)
    friend BigInt operator%(const BigInt& a, const BigInt& b) {
        BigInt q, r;
        divAbs(a, b, q, &r);
        r.neg = a.neg && (r.len != 0);
        return r;
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    // Simple interactive test:
    // Input format:
    //   <number1>
    //   <operator>  (+ - * /)
    //   <number2>
    // Output: result of the operation
    string s1, s2, op;
    if (!(cin >> s1 >> op >> s2)) return 0;

    BigInt a(s1), b(s2), c;
    if (op == "+") c = a + b;
    else if (op == "-") c = a - b;
    else if (op == "*") c = a * b;
    else if (op == "/") c = a / b;
    else {
        cerr << "Unsupported operator\n";
        return 0;
    }

    cout << c.toString() << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------

## 5.  How it works – short walk‑through

1. **Parsing** – the constructor reads a decimal string, records the sign, skips leading zeroes and stores the digits **reversed** (`d[0]` = units, `d[1]` = tens, …).  
   The maximum length is 30, therefore the static array size `31` is safe.

2. **Printing** – `toString()` rebuilds the human‑readable form by walking the array from the most significant digit downwards and prefixes a minus sign when `neg == true`.

3. **Addition / Subtraction** – the sign logic decides which of the two helper functions (`addAbs` or `subAbs`) to call.  
   `addAbs` works exactly like school addition, propagating carries; `subAbs` works similarly but borrows when needed.

4. **Multiplication** – classic O(n²) “long multiplication”.  
   For each digit `a.d[i]` we multiply the whole `b` and add it to the result shifted by `i` positions, propagating carries.

5. **Division** – we use the long‑division algorithm on decimal digits:
   * produce the remainder `cur` step‑by‑step from the most significant digit of the dividend;
   * find the biggest single decimal digit `x` (`0 … 9`) such that `|b| * x ≤ cur`;
   * append `x` to the quotient and update the remainder (`cur = cur – |b| * x`);
   * repeat until all digits of the dividend are consumed.

   The helper `divAbs` returns the quotient; an optional remainder pointer can be used for `%`.

6. **Sign handling** – after the absolute value has been calculated the sign of the result is set to  
   `neg = (signA XOR signB) && result != 0`.  
   Zero never carries a minus sign.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

## 6.  Complexity

| Operation | Time (worst case) | Memory (extra) |
|-----------|-------------------|----------------|
| addition / subtraction | **O(n)** (n ≤ 30) | O(1) |
| multiplication | **O(n²)** (≈ 900 elementary digit ops) | O(1) |
| division | **O(n·m)** where `m` is the number of digits of divisor (≤ 30) | O(1) |

Because `n` never exceeds 30, the program runs instantly even on modest hardware.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

## 7.  Remarks & Extensions

* The implementation uses only **plain C‑style arrays** and primitive variables – it satisfies the “no `vector`” requirement.
* If you need support for numbers larger than `10^30` you only have to increase `MAX_DIGITS`.
* For production‑grade code you would add proper error handling (division by zero, overflow detection) and perhaps overload comparison operators.
* The division routine here is simple and deliberately avoids any heavy‑weight algorithm; for such tiny operands it is more than fast enough.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

**Now you have a minimal, self‑contained big‑integer class that can add, subtract, multiply and divide integers up to ±10³⁰ using only a fixed `char` array.**

