### Why the client works securely

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Connect to server IPC objects** | Uses the **exact same ftok‑derived key** as the server, therefore it can only attach to the *intended* objects. |
| **Validate user‑supplied arguments** | `is_valid_filename()` refuses `../`, absolute paths, or overly long names. |
| **Exclusive mutex (`SEM_MUTEX`)** | Guarantees *no other writer* is inside the command buffer when we copy our request. The client **holds** the mutex only for the tiny critical section (clear + copy), then releases it immediately – this makes the system scalable. |
| **Response handling** | For a `read`, the client *waits* on `SEM_RESPONSE`. The server writes the reply key back into the same command buffer (which the client clears before writing) – this is safe because the critical section is protected by the mutex and the server never overwrites it while `SEM_RESPONSE` is low. |
| **Reply shared memory is private & short‑lived** | The client **removes** the reply segment (`shmctl(..., IPC_RMID)`) right after reading, so no stale segment can be reused by an attacker. The segment is created with mode `0600`. |
| **Signal handling** | The client does **not** install special handlers because it only holds resources for a few seconds – any premature termination will simply leave the server’s command segment empty (the server will clean it up on the next request). In a more robust implementation you would add the same `cleanup()` in a signal handler. |

