## 1️⃣  Quick run‑through of what the original program tries to do

1. Read the number of processes **n**.
2. Read a burst‑time for each process (`p[i].bursttime`).
3. Copy the burst‑times into a temporary array `ar[]` and **sort `ar` in descending order** (the code actually tries to sort *ascending* but the comparison is reversed).
4. Walk through the sorted list and try to map the sorted times back to the original `p[]` elements, compute waiting‑time, turnaround‑time and the average waiting‑time.

The goal is correct, but the implementation is riddled with **logic errors, undefined behaviour, and classic C security pitfalls**.

---

## 2️⃣  Functional / Logical Bugs

| # | Location | Problem | Why it matters |
|---|----------|----------|----------------|
| 1 | `void main()` | `main` must return `int` (C‑standard) | Some compilers will warn/error; non‑zero exit status can be mis‑interpreted. |
| 2 | `int ar[n];` | Variable‑Length Array (VLA) **size is user‑controlled**. If `n` is huge (or negative) you get stack overflow or undefined behaviour. | Stack‑based buffer overflow → crash, possible privilege escalation. |
| 3 | `for(int h=0;h<n-1;h+1)` | `h+1` **does not modify `h`**, infinite loop (or dead code). | The sorting never runs, or the compiler may optimise it away. |
| 4 | `for(int i=0;i<n-1;i++)` inside the sort | The inner loop only goes to `n‑2`, leaving the last element unsorted. | The array isn’t fully sorted → wrong waiting times. |
| 5 | Sorting direction | `if(ar[i] < ar[i+1])` swaps when **left < right**, i.e. it sorts **descending** while SJF needs **ascending** (shortest first). | You compute waiting time as if longest jobs were first → completely wrong schedule. |
| 6 | Mapping sorted times back to original processes | `if(p[i].bursttime == ar[i])` only works when *no two processes share the same burst time*. If duplicate burst times exist, many processes will be skipped, and the `waitingtime` field of some processes stays **uninitialised**. | Uninitialised reads → undefined behaviour, wrong results, possible information leakage. |
| 7 | `float average=j/n;` | `j` is an `int`, `n` is an `int`. Integer division is performed first, then converted to `float`. Also `j` accumulates **waiting time**, not **total waiting time** (you add `p[i].waitingtime` each iteration, but you also later add `p[i].waitingtime` to `j` again via `j=p[i].waitingtime+j;` – double‑counting). | Average is wrong (usually 0) and you lose precision. |
| 8 | No **bounds checking** on `scanf("%d",&n)` or the burst‑time reads. | If a user enters a non‑numeric string, `scanf` fails and leaves `n` uninitialised → huge VLA allocation. | Stack overflow, denial‑of‑service. |
| 9 | No **input size limit** on the `scanf("%d",&p[i].bursttime)`. | Users can input negative numbers or values that overflow `int`. | Negative burst‑times break the algorithm; overflow could be exploited in later arithmetic. |

---

## 3️⃣  Security‑oriented Issues

| # | Issue | Impact |
|---|-------|--------|
| A | **Unbounded VLAs** (`int ar[n];`) – user controlled size on the stack. | Stack‑based buffer overflow → process crash, possible arbitrary code execution on the same privilege level. |
| B | **Unchecked return values** of `scanf`. | If input is malformed, variables keep previous (possibly attacker‑controlled) values → undefined behaviour. |
| C | **Duplicate burst‑times** cause ambiguous mapping → some `waitingtime` fields remain uninitialised. Using those values later is **use‑after‑uninitialised** and may leak stack contents. |
| D | **Signed‑integer overflow** (e.g., `waitingtime1 = p[i].totaltime;` when `totaltime` becomes > INT_MAX). | Undefined behaviour, could be leveraged to bypass checks in a larger system. |
| E | **Missing `return`** from `main`. | Some runtime environments treat non‑zero exit status as error; although not a direct exploit, it indicates sloppy programming which often correlates with other vulnerabilities. |
| F | **No `const` qualifiers** on data that never changes. | Not a direct exploit but a missed opportunity for compiler‑enforced safety. |

---

## 4️⃣  How to Fix It (Secure‑by‑Design)

Below is a **complete rewrite** that:

* Uses **dynamic memory** (`malloc`) with error checking (no VLA on the stack).
* Implements a **proper ascending bubble‑sort** (or a safer `qsort`).
* Keeps **process IDs** so that after sorting we can still report the original order.
* Handles **duplicate burst times** correctly.
* Uses **`size_t`** for array indices, checks all `scanf` return values, and limits the number of processes to a safe constant (`MAX_PROC = 1024`).
* Computes the average waiting time with **floating‑point arithmetic**.
* Returns an **`int`** from `main`.
* Includes basic input sanitisation (rejects negative or zero burst times).

```c
/********************************************************************
 *  Shortest‑Job‑First (SJF) Scheduler – safe, deterministic version *
 *  Author: (your name)                                             *
 *  Updated: 2024‑06‑xx                                               *
 ********************************************************************/

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <limits.h>   // for INT_MAX
#include <errno.h>

#define MAX_PROC 1024          /* hard limit to avoid stack explosion   */
#define MIN_BURST 1            /* burst time must be >0                */

typedef struct {
    int pid;          /* original process number (1‑based)      */
    int burst;        /* burst time (CPU time needed)           */
    int waiting;      /* waiting time (computed)                */
    int turnaround;   /* waiting + burst                        */
} Process;

/* ---------- helper: safe integer read -------------------------------- */
static int safe_read_int(const char *prompt, int *out, int min, int max)
{
    int n;
    char term;
    printf("%s", prompt);
    if (scanf("%d%c", &n, &term) != 2 || term != '\n') {
        fprintf(stderr, "[-] Invalid input – expected an integer.\n");
        return 0;
    }
    if (n < min || n > max) {
        fprintf(stderr, "[-] Value out of range (%d … %d).\n", min, max);
        return 0;
    }
    *out = n;
    return 1;
}

/* ---------- comparator for qsort (ascending burst) ------------------- */
static int cmp_burst_asc(const void *a, const void *b)
{
    const Process *pa = (const Process *)a;
    const Process *pb = (const Process *)b;
    if (pa->burst < pb->burst) return -1;
    if (pa->burst > pb->burst) return  1;
    return 0;   /* equal burst times – keep original order (stable) */
}

/* ---------- main ------------------------------------------------------ */
int main(void)
{
    int n;                      /* number of processes */
    Process *p = NULL;          /* dynamically allocated array */
    long long total_wait = 0;   /* accumulate waiting times (avoid overflow) */
    double avg_wait = 0.0;

    /* ---- 1. read number of processes --------------------------------- */
    if (!safe_read_int("Enter the number of processes (1‑" STR(MAX_PROC) "): ",
                       &n, 1, MAX_PROC)) {
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    /* ---- 2. allocate array ------------------------------------------- */
    p = calloc((size_t)n, sizeof(Process));
    if (!p) {
        perror("calloc");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    /* ---- 3. read burst times ----------------------------------------- */
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        char prompt[64];
        snprintf(prompt, sizeof(prompt),
                 "Enter burst time for process %d (>= %d): ", i + 1, MIN_BURST);
        if (!safe_read_int(prompt, &p[i].burst, MIN_BURST, INT_MAX)) {
            free(p);
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }
        p[i].pid = i + 1;   /* store original index (1‑based) */
    }

    /* ---- 4. sort by burst time (shortest first) ---------------------- */
    qsort(p, (size_t)n, sizeof(Process), cmp_burst_asc);

    /* ---- 5. compute waiting / turnaround times ----------------------- */
    int elapsed = 0;   /* total CPU time consumed so far */
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        p[i].waiting   = elapsed;
        p[i].turnaround = elapsed + p[i].burst;
        elapsed += p[i].burst;
        total_wait += p[i].waiting;
    }

    avg_wait = (double)total_wait / n;

    /* ---- 6. display results ------------------------------------------ */
    puts("\n=== SJF Schedule ===");
    printf("%-8s %-8s %-12s %-12s\n", "PID", "Burst", "Waiting", "Turnaround");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        printf("%-8d %-8d %-12d %-12d\n",
               p[i].pid, p[i].burst, p[i].waiting, p[i].turnaround);
    }
    printf("\nAverage waiting time: %.2f units\n", avg_wait);

    /* ---- 7. cleanup --------------------------------------------------- */
    free(p);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

### What changed & why it matters

| Change | Security/Correctness benefit |
|--------|-------------------------------|
| `int main(void)` → returns `int` | Conforms to the C standard; OS can interpret exit status. |
| Hard‑coded `MAX_PROC` and `calloc` | No VLAs → stack size is bounded; allocation failures checked. |
| `safe_read_int()` validates **both** conversion and range | Prevents malformed input, negative/over‑large numbers, and stray characters that would otherwise leave variables uninitialised. |
