## TL;DR  

**Error**  

```
SSL routines:tls_construct_client_hello:no protocols available
   ssl\statem\statem_clnt.c:1114
```

means **the OpenSSL client library could not find a single TLS/SSL protocol version that both it and the library’s configuration consider “usable”.** In practice this is almost always caused by one (or a combination) of the following:

| # | Root cause | Typical symptom |
|---|------------|-----------------|
| 1 | **All TLS protocol versions have been disabled** (e.g. `SSL_OP_NO_TLSv1`, `SSL_OP_NO_TLSv1_1`, `SSL_OP_NO_TLSv1_2`, `SSL_OP_NO_TLSv1_3`) **or the library is built without support for any of them** | Even a simple `openssl s_client -connect host:443` aborts with the same error. |
| 2 | **FIPS or other “strict mode” is enabled** and the only available algorithms are filtered out (e.g. only RSA‑2048 + SHA‑256 is allowed but the server only offers ECDSA). | The same error is produced even when the server does support TLS‑1.2/1.3. |
| 3 | **The default cipher‑suite list is empty** (e.g. `SSL_CTX_set_cipher_list(ctx, "")` or `SSL_CTX_set_ciphersuites(ctx, "")`). | OpenSSL can negotiate a protocol version but refuses to send a `ClientHello` because it would have no ciphers. |
| 4 | **Compilation flags**: the library was built with `no-tls1_2`/`no-tls1_3` (or even `no-ssl3`) **and the runtime config tries to use those versions**. | The binary reports “no protocols available” regardless of the user‑space configuration. |
| 5 | **Version‑specific bugs** in older OpenSSL releases (≤ 1.0.2) where the “protocol selection” code mis‑detects the system’s crypto provider (Windows CSP/BCrypt). | Updating OpenSSL fixes the problem. |

Below you will find a **step‑by‑step diagnostic checklist**, **remediation actions**, and **security considerations** for each scenario, followed by a **minimal reproducible example** and **hardening suggestions** for production deployments.

---

## 1️⃣ Deep‑Dive into the OpenSSL Code Path

The error originates from `ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c:1114` in the function `tls_construct_client_hello()`. The relevant excerpt (OpenSSL 1.1.1‑master) looks like this:

```c
int tls_construct_client_hello(SSL *s, WPACKET *pkt)
{
    int ret = 0;
    const SSL_METHOD *method = SSL_IS_DTLS(s) ? &DTLS_method : &TLS_method;
    size_t version = method->ssl_version;

    /* 1. Build the protocol version we want to advertise */
    if (!tls_client_version(s, &protocol_version)) {
        SSLfatal(s, SSL_AD_INTERNAL_ERROR,
                 SSL_F_TLS_CONSTRUCT_CLIENT_HELLO,
                 ERR_R_INTERNAL_ERROR);
        return 0;
    }

    /* 2. Pick a cipher list (may be empty) */
    if (!tls1_choose_cipher(s)) {
        /* No cipher suites left => no protocol can be used */
        SSLfatal(s, SSL_AD_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE,
                 SSL_F_TLS_CONSTRUCT_CLIENT_HELLO,
                 SSL_R_NO_PROTOCOLS_AVAILABLE);
        return 0;
    }

    /* ... Normal ClientHello construction ... */
}
```

The `tls1_choose_cipher(s)` routine walks the *available cipher list* and discards any cipher that is:

* Not permitted by the **protocol version** currently selected.
* Disallowed by **policy** (e.g. `SSL_OP_NO_TLSv1_3`).
* Incompatible with the **key‑exchange**/certificate constraints or **FIPS mode**.

If the walk ends with **zero candidates**, `SSL_R_NO_PROTOCOLS_AVAILABLE` is raised – exactly the error you see.

Thus the error is **not** “the server refused the handshake”; it is the **client** refusing to send any `ClientHello` because it has no protocol/cipher combination that satisfies both its own policy and the compiled capabilities.

---

## 2️⃣ Diagnostic Checklist

Run these checks in *exactly the environment* where the error occurs (same binary, same user, same TLS‑library instance).

### 2.1 Verify OpenSSL Build Information

```bash
openssl version -a
```

Look for:

* `built on` and `platform` – ensure you are using the expected binary (system vs bundled library).
* `options:` – especially `no-ssl3`, `no-tls1`, `no-tls1_1`, `no-tls1_2`, `no-tls1_3`.
* `FIPS: enabled` – indicates strict mode.

> **Why:** If the build itself lacks the protocols you expect, no amount of runtime config can resurrect them.

### 2.2 List Enabled Protocols at Runtime

```c
/* Minimal C snippet (compile with -lssl -lcrypto) */
#include <openssl/ssl.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    SSL_library_init();
    const SSL_METHOD *method = TLS_client_method();   // auto‑negotiates
    SSL_CTX *ctx = SSL_CTX_new(method);
    long opts = SSL_CTX_get_options(ctx);
    printf("Options mask: 0x%08lx\n", opts);
    /* Print which protocols are explicitly disabled */
    #define FLAG(name) if (opts & SSL_OP_NO_##name) \
        printf("  %s disabled\n", #name);
    FLAG(SSLv2); FLAG(SSLv3); FLAG(TLSv1); FLAG(TLSv1_1); \
    FLAG(TLSv1_2); FLAG(TLSv1_3);
    SSL_CTX_free(ctx);
    return 0;
