Below is a **self‑contained C program** that implements the `prcinfo` utility described in the question.  
It works on any modern Linux system that exposes the **/proc** pseudo‑filesystem.  
The code is deliberately kept simple (no external libraries) so it can be compiled with a plain `gcc` command.

---

## 1.  What the program does

| Step | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **Argument parsing** | `prcinfo  <root>  <pid1> [pid2 … pid5]  <option>`  – the last argument that starts with ‘‑’ is treated as the option. |
| **Build a view of the whole process table** | Scans every numeric entry under `/proc`, reads **PPid** and **State** (the single‑letter code from `/proc/[pid]/stat`). |
| **Create a parent → children map** | For every process we store its parent‑PID (`ppid`) and a linked list of its direct children. |
| **Check the subtree** | Starting from the *root* PID we walk the children recursively and collect **all** PIDs that belong to that tree. Every `<pidX>` that is found in that set is printed as `PID: <pid>  PPID: <ppid>`. |
| **Option handling** | After the basic list the program can additionally print one of the following (only the first *process_id* in the list – i.e. `pid1` – is examined): <br> * `-nd` – all *non‑direct* descendants of `pid1` (descendants except its immediate children). <br> * `-dd` – immediate children of `pid1`. <br> * `-sb` – **s**ibling processes (same parent) of `pid1`. <br> * `-sz` – sibling processes that are **defunct** (`Z` state). <br> * `-gc` – grandchildren (children of children) of `pid1`. <br> * `-zz` – prints “Defunct” or “Not Defunct” for `pid1`. <br> * `-zc` – direct children of `pid1` that are currently defunct. |
| **Safety notes** | The program never writes to any file, never sends signals, and only reads the read‑only `/proc` hierarchy. It therefore respects the privilege model of Linux – you can only see information about processes that your user is allowed to see. |

