## 10️⃣ Full Flow Walk‑through (Code‑Centric)

| Step | What happens (code) |
|------|----------------------|
| **A. Login** | `POST /auth/login` → `AuthController.login()` → `AuthService.checkCredentials()` → `AuthService.createSession()` → <br>• Generates a **UUID** `sessionId` <br>• Persists a `UserSession` in Redis (`session:{userId}:{sessionId}`) with the *requested scopes* and *expiry* <br>• Calls `JwtTokenProvider.generateToken(session)` → JWT contains `sid`, `scopes`, `uid` claims |
| **B. Client stores JWT** | The token is sent back; the client adds `Authorization: Bearer <jwt>` to every request. |
| **C. Request → JWT Validation** | `JwtAuthenticationFilter.doFilterInternal()` <br>1️⃣ Extract Bearer token <br>2️⃣ `JwtTokenProvider.validateToken()` → signature & expiry check <br>3️⃣ Pull `sid` & `sub` (userId) <br>4️⃣ Query Redis `sessionRepo.findById("session:{userId}:{sid}")` to guarantee the session is still alive (revocation possible) <br>5️⃣ Build authorities from the `scopes` claim (`SCOPE_*`) <br>6️⃣ Populate `SecurityContext` with `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken` |
| **D. Authorization** | `@PreAuthorize("hasAuthority('SCOPE_PAYMENTS')")` (or `hasAuthority('SCOPE_REPORTING')`) checks the authorities that the filter just added. If the scope is missing → `403 Forbidden`. |
| **E. Logout / Revocation** | `POST /auth/logout` extracts the same `sid` from the incoming token, then `sessionRepo.deleteById(...)`. Subsequent requests with that token will fail at step **C** because the Redis entry is missing. |
| **F. Multiple Sessions** | Because each login creates a **new UUID** `sessionId`, a user can hold many distinct entries in Redis (e.g. one per device, per functionality). Revoking one does **not** affect the others. |

