## Overview  

Below is a **complete, security‑oriented guide** for building a Java‑based system that continuously **sends and receives image data over sockets**.  
The guide is split into three parts:

| Part | Goal |
|------|------|
| **1️⃣ Architecture & Protocol** | How to structure the data stream so you can reliably send/receive images indefinitely. |
| **2️⃣ Secure Implementation** | Working code (client / server) that uses TLS, authentication, integrity checks, and defensive‑coding techniques. |
| **3️⃣ Threat Modeling & Hardening** | Common attack surfaces, test vectors, and concrete mitigations you should add before going to production. |

> **Note:**  The code examples use the **Java Standard Library** only (no external libs). If you prefer higher‑level frameworks (Netty, gRPC, etc.) you can adapt the same principles.

---

## 1️⃣ Architecture & Protocol

### 1.1 Why a custom “image‑stream” protocol?
- Raw `InputStream`/`OutputStream` are **byte‑oriented**: you must tell the receiver where an image starts and ends.  
- A **length‑prefixed** frame is the simplest, reliable way.  
- We combine that with a **message header** that includes a **type**, **timestamp**, **hash**, and **optional MAC**.

```
+----------------+----------------+----------------+----------------+------------------+
| 4‑byte length  | 1‑byte type    | 8‑byte ts (ms) | 32‑byte SHA‑256 | N‑byte payload   |
+----------------+----------------+----------------+----------------+------------------+
   total bytes = 4 + 1 + 8 + 32 + N
```

| Field | Size | Meaning |
|-------|------|----------|
| **Length** | 4 bytes (big‑endian) | `N = payload length` (image bytes). |
| **Type**   | 1 byte | `0x01 = IMAGE`, `0x02 = HEARTBEAT`, `0xFF = CLOSE`. |
| **Timestamp** | 8 bytes (long) | Epoch‑ms when the frame was generated – useful for replay detection. |
| **Hash** | 32 bytes | SHA‑256 of the **payload only** (image bytes). |
| **Payload** | `N` bytes | Raw JPEG/PNG/WEBP bytes (no additional encoding). |

> **Why a SHA‑256 hash?**  
> - Detect corruption (network errors).  
> - When coupled with a **secret HMAC key**, it becomes an integrity check that also authenticates the sender (see §2.4).

### 1.2 Continuous “keep‑alive”  

A lightweight **heartbeat** (type `0x02`) with **zero payload** is exchanged every **X seconds** (e.g., 30 s).  
If either side does not receive a heartbeat within a timeout (e.g., 2× interval), it closes the socket. This prevents idle‑connection hangs and gives you a clean point to detect DoS or network loss.

### 1.3 Message Flow  

```
Client                         Server
   |  <---  TLS handshake  ---> |
   |  <--  HEARTBEAT (30s) --> |
   |   IMAGE frame           -->|   (receive, verify hash, store)
   |  <--  ACK (optional) ----|   (optional "image received")
   |  ... repeat forever ...  |
   |  <--  CLOSE (0xFF) ----  |
   |  close socket             |
```

---

## 2️⃣ Secure Implementation  

We'll present **two runnable classes**: `ImageServer` and `ImageClient`.  
Both use **`SSLServerSocket` / `SSLSocket`** (TLS 1.2+), **mutual authentication** (client certs), and **HMAC‑SHA256** for per‑message authentication.

### 2.1 Prerequisites – Keystores  

Generate a self‑signed CA, server cert, and client cert (for a quick demo):

```bash
# 1) CA
openssl req -new -x509 -keyout ca.key -out ca.crt -days 3650 -nodes -subj "/CN=Test CA"

# 2) Server keystore (PKCS12)
openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout server.key -out server.csr -subj "/CN=server"
openssl x509 -req -in server.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial -out server.crt -days 365
openssl pkcs12 -export -in server.crt -inkey server.key -name server -out server.p12 -CAfile ca.crt -caname root

# 3) Client keystore
openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout client.key -out client.csr -subj "/CN=client"
openssl x509 -req -in client.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial -out client.crt -days 365
openssl pkcs12 -export -in client.crt -inkey client.key -name client -out client.p12 -CAfile ca.crt -caname root
```

- **Server truststore** (`server-truststore.p12`) must contain the CA (`ca.crt`).  
- **Client truststore** (`client-truststore.p12`) likewise contains the same CA.

