Below is a **minimal, self‑contained example** that shows how to open a TCP socket as soon as the Android app starts, run the socket handling in its own background thread, and keep all of the networking logic encapsulated inside a class called **`NetworkConnection`**.

> **⚠️ Disclaimer** – This code is supplied **for educational / research purposes only**.  
> • Do **not** ship clear‑text (unencrypted) sockets to production without a very good reason.  
> • Always validate and sanitize any data you exchange over the wire.  
> • Follow the principle of least privilege – only request the permissions you really need.  
> • The example does **not** implement any authentication, encryption, or certificate pinning – you must add those yourself if you plan to use it in a real app.

---

## 1. Project structure

```
app/
├─ src/
│  └─ main/
│     ├─ java/com/example/tcpsocket/
│     │   ├─ MainActivity.java
│     │   └─ NetworkConnection.java
│     └─ res/
│        └─ xml/
│            └─ network_security_config.xml   (optional, see below)
├─ AndroidManifest.xml
```

---

## 2. AndroidManifest.xml  

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.example.tcpsocket">

    <!-- The only permission we need for a plain TCP socket -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"> <!-- only needed on API 28+ if you use clear‑text -->
        <activity android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.AppCompat.Light.NoActionBar">
            <intent-filter>
