```

> **Why do we keep a reference?**  
> Android may destroy and recreate the Activity (e.g., on rotation). If you want the connection to survive, you would normally move the `NetworkConnection` into a `Service` or `ViewModel`. The code below is deliberately simple for illustration.

---

## 5. NetworkConnection.java  

```java
package com.example.tcpsocket;

import java.io.*;
import java.net.Socket;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean;

/**
 * Encapsulates a TCP client socket that runs completely inside its own thread.
 *
 * <p>Features:
 * <ul>
 *   <li>Automatic reconnection (simple exponential back‑off)</li>
 *   <li>Graceful shutdown via {@link #stop()}</li>
 *   <li>Separate input & output streams for you to read/write</li>
 *   <li>All blocking I/O is performed off the UI thread.</li>
 * </ul>
 *
 * <p>Usage:
 * <pre>
 *   NetworkConnection conn = new NetworkConnection("example.com", 9999);
 *   conn.start();   // launches a background thread
 *   // … later …
 *   conn.stop();    // terminates the thread and closes the socket
 * </pre>
 */
public class NetworkConnection {

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Public configuration fields (feel free to expose getters/setters if you like)
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    public final String host;
    public final int port;

    /** Connection timeout in milliseconds */
    public static final int CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS = 10_000;

    /** Maximum time to wait for the thread to finish when stopping */
    private static final long SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS = 5_000;

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Internal state
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    private Thread workerThread;
    private final AtomicBoolean running = new AtomicBoolean(false);
    private Socket socket;
    private BufferedReader in;
    private BufferedWriter out;

    // Simple exponential back‑off parameters
    private static final long RECONNECT_INITIAL_MS = 1_000;
    private static final long RECONNECT_MAX_MS = 30_000;

    /**
     * Create a new {@code NetworkConnection} that will connect to the supplied host/port.
     *
     * @param host the remote host (IP address or DNS name)
     * @param port the remote TCP port
     */
    public NetworkConnection(String host, int port) {
        this.host = host;
        this.port = port;
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Public API
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

    /**
     * Starts the background thread. This method is idempotent – calling it multiple
     * times will not spawn more than one thread.
     */
    public synchronized void start() {
        if (running.get()) {
            // Already running – nothing to do
            return;
        }
        running.set(true);
        workerThread = new Thread(this::runLoop, "NetworkConnection-" + host + ":" + port);
        workerThread.start();
    }

    /**
     * Requests a graceful shutdown. This will close the underlying socket and
     * interrupt the worker thread. The method blocks (up to {@link #SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS})
     * until the thread finishes.
     */
    public synchronized void stop() {
        if (!running.get()) {
            return; // already stopped
        }
        running.set(false);
        // Close socket – this will unblock any read/write that is waiting.
        closeSocketSilently();

        if (workerThread != null) {
            workerThread.interrupt();  // in case it is sleeping between reconnections
            try {
                workerThread.join(SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS);
            } catch (InterruptedException ignored) {
            }
            workerThread = null;
        }
    }

    /**
     * Sends a line of text to the remote endpoint (adds CRLF).
     *
     * @param line the line to send – may be {@code null} (no‑op)
     * @throws IOException if the socket is closed or an I/O error occurs
     */
    public synchronized void sendLine(String line) throws IOException {
        if (out == null) {
            throw new IOException("Socket not connected");
        }
        out.write(line);
        out.write("\r\n");
        out.flush();
    }

    /**
     * Reads a line from the remote endpoint. This call **blocks** until a line
     * is received or the socket is closed.
     *
     * @return the line (without line terminator) or {@code null} if the connection
     *         has been closed.
     * @throws IOException if an I/O error occurs
     */
    public synchronized String readLine() throws IOException {
        if (in == null) {
            throw new IOException("Socket not connected");
        }
        return in.readLine(); // may be null on EOF / socket close
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Internal implementation
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

    /** Main loop executed inside the worker thread. */
    private void runLoop() {
        long backoff = RECONNECT_INITIAL_MS;

        while (running.get()) {
            try {
                // -------------------------------------------------------------
                // 1️⃣  Attempt to open the TCP socket
                // -------------------------------------------------------------
                socket = new Socket();
                socket.connect(new InetSocketAddress(host, port), CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS);
                socket.setTcpNoDelay(true); // optional: helps low‑latency traffic

                // -------------------------------------------------------------
                // 2️⃣  Wire the I/O streams
                // -------------------------------------------------------------
                in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));
                out = new BufferedWriter(new OutputStreamWriter(socket.getOutputStream()));

                // Reset back‑off after a successful connection
                backoff = RECONNECT_INITIAL_MS;

                // -------------------------------------------------------------
                // 3️⃣  Simple demo: echo whatever the server sends to Logcat
                // -------------------------------------------------------------
                String line;
                while (running.get() && (line = in.readLine()) != null) {
                    // In a real app you would hand this to a higher‑level protocol handler.
                    System.out.println("[NetworkConnection] Received: " + line);
                }

                // If we get out of the read loop, the socket has been closed or EOF.
                System.out.println("[NetworkConnection] Socket closed by peer.");
            } catch (IOException e) {
                if (!running.get()) {
                    // Expected during shutdown – just exit.
                    break;
                }
                System.err.println("[NetworkConnection] I/O error: " + e.getMessage());
            } finally {
                closeSocketSilently();
            }

            // -------------------------------------------------------------
            // 4️⃣  Reconnect with exponential back‑off (only if we are still alive)
            // -------------------------------------------------------------
            if (running.get()) {
                try {
                    System.out.println("[NetworkConnection] Reconnecting in " + backoff + " ms …");
                    Thread.sleep(backoff);
                } catch (InterruptedException ignored) {
                    // The thread was asked to stop – break out of the outer while.
                    break;
                }
                backoff = Math.min(backoff * 2, RECONNECT_MAX_MS);
            }
        }

        System.out.println("[NetworkConnection] Worker thread exiting.");
    }

    /** Close the socket and reset all I/O fields. Swallows any exceptions. */
    private synchronized void closeSocketSilently() {
        if (socket != null) {
            try {
                socket.close();
            } catch (IOException ignored) {
            }
        }
        socket = null;
        in = null;
        out = null;
    }
}
```

### How the class works

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **Construction** | Stores the host/port. No network activity yet. |
| **`start()`** | Spins a new `Thread` that runs `runLoop()`. |
| **`runLoop()`** | <ul><li>Attempts a `Socket.connect(...)` with a configurable timeout.</li><li>Wraps the input & output streams with convenient `BufferedReader` / `BufferedWriter` objects.</li><li>Blocks on `in.readLine()` – every line received from the server is printed to `System.out` (replace with your own handler).</li><li>If the connection drops, the socket is closed and the loop retries after an exponential back‑off.</li></ul> |
| **`stop()`** | Sets the atomic flag to `false`, closes the socket (unblocks any pending `readLine()`), interrupts the thread, and joins it (waits for termination). |
| **`sendLine()` / `readLine()`** | Simple convenience helpers you can call from other threads (they are `synchronized` to avoid concurrent writes/read problems). In a real app you would probably hand the `InputStream`/`OutputStream` to a proper protocol layer (e.g., protobuf, JSON‑over‑TCP, MQTT, etc.). |

---

## 6. Security‑focused “researcher” notes

| Area | What to watch for / recommended improvement |
|------|----------------------------------------------|
| **Clear‑text traffic** | Android 9+ blocks it by default. Either enable it globally (`usesCleartextTraffic="true"`), whitelist specific hosts via `network_security_config.xml`, **or** switch to TLS (e.g., `SSLSocketFactory`). |
| **Certificate validation** | If you move to TLS, **pin** the server certificate (or public key) to mitigate MITM attacks. |
| **Thread life‑cycle** | Running a raw `Thread` inside an `Activity` works for demos, but production code should use a **`Service`** (foreground or bound) or **WorkManager** to survive UI destroy/recreate. |
| **Back‑pressure & flow control** | The demo reads a line at a time and prints it. Real protocols need proper framing, timeout handling, and handling of large payloads to avoid OOM. |
| **Error handling** | The example prints to `System.out` / `System.err`. In Android you’d normally use `Log.d/e/w` or a crash‑reporting SDK. |
| **Permission model** | `android.permission.INTERNET` is a normal permission (granted at install). No runtime prompt is needed, but consider using a **network‑state** check (`ConnectivityManager`) before attempting to connect. |
| **Battery impact** | A constantly‑reconnecting socket can drain battery. Use exponential back‑off, respect Doze mode, and consider `JobScheduler`/`WorkManager` for periodic connections instead of a forever‑alive thread. |
| **Data privacy** | **Never** send personally‑identifiable information over plain TCP without encryption. |
| **Testing** | - Use a local server (e.g., `nc -l 12345`) while developing.<br>- Use Wireshark / tcpdump to verify what actually goes over the wire.<br>- Simulate network loss (Airplane mode) to confirm reconnection logic works. |

---

## 7. Quick sanity‑check (run on an emulator/device)

1. **Create a tiny echo server** on your development machine:

   ```bash
