## 2️⃣ Full Java source (`H264SocketPlayer.java`)

```java
/**
 * H264SocketPlayer
 *
 * A minimal demo that:
 *   • Listens on a TCP port for an incoming raw H.264 bit‑stream.
 *   • Pipes the received bytes directly into FFmpeg (via FFmpegFrameGrabber).
 *   • Decodes and shows the video in a CanvasFrame.
 *
 *  Build & run:
 *      javac -cp ".:path/to/javacv-platform-1.5.9.jar" H264SocketPlayer.java
 *      java  -cp ".:path/to/javacv-platform-1.5.9.jar" H264SocketPlayer <port>
 *
 *  The client can be any tool that streams H.264, e.g. FFmpeg:
 *      ffmpeg -re -i input.mp4 -c:v libx264 -f h264 tcp://<host>:<port>
 *
 *  Security notes are at the end of this file.
 */

import org.bytedeco.javacv.*;

import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class H264SocketPlayer {

    /** Size of the internal pipe buffer (in bytes). */
    private static final int PIPE_BUF_SIZE = 2 * 1024 * 1024; // 2 MiB

    /** Timeout for socket read in milliseconds (0 = infinite). */
    private static final int SOCKET_READ_TIMEOUT_MS = 0;

    /** Main entry point. */
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length != 1) {
            System.err.println("Usage: java H264SocketPlayer <listen-port>");
            System.exit(1);
        }

        int port = Integer.parseInt(args[0]);

        // --------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1️⃣ Set up a pipe that will connect the socket reader thread to
        //    FFmpegFrameGrabber (which reads from an InputStream).
        // --------------------------------------------------------------------
        try (PipedOutputStream pipeOut = new PipedOutputStream();
             PipedInputStream pipeIn  = new PipedInputStream(pipeOut, PIPE_BUF_SIZE)) {

            // ----------------------------------------------------------------
            // 2️⃣ Launch the socket‑receiver thread.
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------
            ExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();
            Future<?> receiverFuture = executor.submit(() -> socketReceiverTask(port, pipeOut));

            // ----------------------------------------------------------------
            // 3️⃣ Start FFmpegFrameGrabber on the *reading* side of the pipe.
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------
            try (FFmpegFrameGrabber grabber = new FFmpegFrameGrabber(pipeIn)) {
                // We are feeding raw H.264; tell FFmpeg about the format.
                grabber.setFormat("h264");
                // Set a reasonable timeout for the network source (optional).
                grabber.setOption("rw_timeout", "5000000"); // microseconds

                grabber.start();

                // ----------------------------------------------------------------
                // 4️⃣ Show frames with CanvasFrame (a lightweight Swing window).
                // ----------------------------------------------------------------
                CanvasFrame canvas = new CanvasFrame("H.264 Socket Viewer", CanvasFrame.getDefaultGamma() / grabber.getGamma());
                canvas.setDefaultCloseOperation(javax.swing.JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
                canvas.setCanvasSize(grabber.getImageWidth(), grabber.getImageHeight());

                Frame frame;
                while ((frame = grabber.grabImage()) != null && canvas.isVisible()) {
                    canvas.showImage(frame);
                }

                // Cleanup
                canvas.dispose();
                grabber.stop();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.err.println("FFmpeg error: " + e.getMessage());
                e.printStackTrace();
            } finally {
                // Tell the receiver thread to stop (close the pipe → EOF)
                try { pipeOut.close(); } catch (IOException ignored) {}
                executor.shutdownNow();
            }

            // Make sure the receiver thread has exited cleanly (or log the problem)
            try {
                receiverFuture.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.err.println("Receiver thread did not finish cleanly: " + e);
            }

        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Pipe creation failed: " + e);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Thread that accepts exactly **one** client connection, reads the raw
     * H.264 bytes from it and writes them into the supplied {@link OutputStream}
     * (the pipe’s write‑end). When the client disconnects the method returns.
     *
     * The method is deliberately simple – it does **no protocol handling**, it
     * just streams bytes. This is the most common way to forward a pure H.264
     * elementary stream generated by tools like `ffmpeg -f h264`.
     */
    private static void socketReceiverTask(int listenPort, OutputStream out) {
        ServerSocket serverSocket = null;
        Socket client = null;
        try {
            serverSocket = new ServerSocket();
            // Optional hardening: bind to a specific interface only
            // serverSocket.bind(new InetSocketAddress("127.0.0.1", listenPort));
            serverSocket.bind(new InetSocketAddress(listenPort));
            System.out.println("[+] Listening on port " + listenPort + " …");

            // ----------------------------------------------------------------
            // Accept *one* connection. If you need many simultaneous streams,
            // spin up a new thread per client (be careful with resource limits!).
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------
            client = serverSocket.accept();
            System.out.println("[+] Connection accepted from " + client.getRemoteSocketAddress());

            // Optionally configure socket time‑outs, TCP_NODELAY, etc.
            client.setTcpNoDelay(true); // lower latency
            if (SOCKET_READ_TIMEOUT_MS > 0) {
                client.setSoTimeout(SOCKET_READ_TIMEOUT_MS);
            }

            // ----------------------------------------------------------------
            // Copy bytes from the socket to the pipe (no buffering on the socket
            // side – we rely on the pipe’s internal buffer).
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------
            try (InputStream socketIn = client.getInputStream()) {
                byte[] buffer = new byte[64 * 1024]; // 64 KB chunks
                int read;
                while ((read = socketIn.read(buffer)) != -1) {
                    out.write(buffer, 0, read);
                }
                System.out.println("[*] End‑of‑stream reached, client closed connection.");
            }

        } catch (SocketException se) {
            System.err.println("[!] Socket error: " + se.getMessage());
        } catch (IOException ioe) {
            System.err.println("[!] I/O error: " + ioe.getMessage());
        } finally {
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------
            // Clean up all resources, making sure the pipe gets closed.
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------
            try { if (client != null) client.close(); } catch (IOException ignored) {}
            try { if (serverSocket != null) serverSocket.close(); } catch (IOException ignored) {}
            try { out.close(); } catch (IOException ignored) {}
            System.out.println("[+] Receiver thread terminated.");
        }
    }
}
```

### How it works, step‑by‑step

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Pipe creation** (`PipedInputStream` / `PipedOutputStream`) | Acts as an **in‑memory FIFO** connecting the socket reader (producer) to the FFmpeg decoder (consumer). | No temporary files → no filesystem exposure, minimal I/O latency. |
| **Receiver thread** (`socketReceiverTask`) | Accepts ONE TCP connection, reads raw bytes and pushes them into the pipe. | Simplicity reduces attack surface. If you need multiple streams, spawn a *new* thread per client, but enforce a strict upper bound. |
| **FFmpegFrameGrabber** (`grabber.setFormat("h264")`) | Tells FFmpeg that the incoming byte‑stream is an elementary H.264 stream. | FFmpeg does all heavy‑lifting (parsing, NAL unit extraction, de‑blocking). |
| **CanvasFrame** | Displays `Frame` objects in a Swing window. | Pure Java UI; no external native windowing libs beyond JavaCV’s bundled FFmpeg. |
| **Graceful shutdown** | When the sender closes the socket, the pipe receives EOF → `grabber.grabImage()` returns `null` → loop exits. | Prevents “stuck” threads and resource leakage. |

---

## 3️⃣ Security‑hardening checklist (for the “security researcher” mindset)

| Area | Typical threat | Mitigation in the example (or recommendation) |
|------|----------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| **Network exposure** | Unauthenticated remote client can flood the server with malformed data → crashes, CPU exhaustion, memory‑DoS. | • Bind the `ServerSocket` to a **specific interface** (e.g., `127.0.0.1` for local testing). <br>• Use a **firewall** (iptables, Windows Defender) to restrict which IPs can connect. |
| **Input validation** | Malformed H.264 NAL units might trigger bugs in FFmpeg (historically CVE‑xxxx). | • Keep the **FFmpeg binaries up‑to‑date** (JavaCV ships with a specific version). <br>• Run the process with **limited privileges** (non‑root, chroot if needed). |
| **Resource limits** | Unlimited pipe buffer could be exhausted by a very high‑bit‑rate stream. | • The `PipedInputStream` size (`PIPE_BUF_SIZE`) caps memory usage. <br>• Add a **rate‑limiter** on the socket (`Socket.setReceiveBufferSize`) or monitor `read()` loops for abnormal rates. |
| **Denial‑of‑service** | A client that never sends data (or sends at a glacial pace) holds the connection forever. | • Set a **read timeout** (`SOCKET_READ_TIMEOUT_MS`) to break idle connections. <br>• Reject connections after a maximum **idle period** (close socket). |
| **Thread safety & clean‑up** | Leaky threads cause resource exhaustion over time. | • The `ExecutorService` is shut down with `shutdownNow()` after EOF. <br>• `Future.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)` ensures the receiver thread finishes. |
| **Data confidentiality / integrity** | Man‑in‑the‑middle could tamper with the video or inject malicious payload. | • Run over **TLS** (wrap the raw socket in `SSLSocket`). <br>• Use a **pre‑shared key** or client‑certificate authentication if you need guarantee of source. |
| **Process isolation** | If an attacker exploits an FFmpeg bug, they could escape the JVM. | • Run the whole JVM inside a **container** (Docker) or a **sandbox** (firejail, gvisor). <br>• Use `seccomp`/`AppArmor` profiles to deny execve, fork, etc., beyond what is needed for decoding. |
| **Logging & monitoring** | Blindness to attacks. | Add proper logging (e.g., `java.util.logging` or SLF4J) for connection attempts, byte‑rates, exceptions. Feed logs into a SIEM. |
| **Supply‑chain trust** | Malicious version of `javacv-platform` could contain a backdoor. | Verify the Maven SHA‑256 checksum (or use a reproducible‐build registry). Pin the version, avoid `+` ranges. |

