## 8️⃣ Security‑Researcher Checklist (what to verify / harden)

| # | Aspect | Why it matters | Recommended actions |
|---|--------|----------------|---------------------|
| 1 | **Password storage** | Storing plain text or weak hashes is trivial to crack. | Use **BCrypt**, Argon2, or PBKDF2 with a unique per‑user salt (BCrypt already does this). Enforce a minimum password length + complexity policy at registration. |
| 2 | **Transport security** | Credentials sent over HTTP can be sniffed. | Deploy the whole site under **TLS (HTTPS)**. Set `Secure` and `HttpOnly` flags on cookies. |
| 3 | **CSRF protection** | An attacker could forge a POST to `/admin/login` from another site. | Enable Spring Security’s CSRF token or embed a hidden token in the login form and verify on the server side. |
| 4 | **Brute‑force / credential stuffing** | Unlimited login attempts let attackers for‑ward a dictionary attack. | Implement **rate‑limiting** per IP (e.g., via a filter or a gateway), account lockout (as shown), CAPTCHA after repeated failures, and logging of failed attempts. |
| 5 | **Session fixation** | If the session ID is not regenerated after login, an attacker could hijack the session. | After a successful login, **invalidate the existing session** (`session.invalidate()`) and create a new one, then store the admin principal. |
| 6 | **Error messages** | Detailed errors can reveal whether a username exists. | Always return a **generic error** like “用户名或密码错误”. |
| 7 | **Logging & audit** | Lack of logs makes incident response hard. | Log successful and failed authentication attempts (including timestamp, IP, user‑agent) to a tamper‑evident store. Do **not** log passwords. |
| 8 | **Principle of least privilege** | Granting the admin too many rights may expose the whole system if compromised. | Separate **admin roles** (e.g., `SUPER_ADMIN`, `CONTENT_ADMIN`) and enforce authorization checks for every protected endpoint (use Spring Security’s `@PreAuthorize`). |
| 9 | **Input validation / XSS** | Unescaped data reflected in the UI can lead to XSS. | Encode all output (e.g., JSTL `<c:out>` or Thymeleaf `${...}` automatically escapes). Validate inputs on the server side (as shown with `@Valid`). |
|10| **Dependency management** | Out‑of‑date libraries contain known vulnerabilities. | Keep Spring, MyBatis, BCrypt, etc., up‑to‑date. Scan dependencies with tools like **OWASP Dependency‑Check** or **Snyk**. |
|11| **Database protection** | SQL injection could bypass authentication. | Use **parameterized queries** (MyBatis does this automatically with `#{}` placeholders). Never concatenate raw user input into SQL. |
|12| **Password reset / recovery** | Poor implementation can be abused for account takeover. | Use token‑based, time‑limited, single‑use reset links sent to a verified email; never disclose whether an email exists. |
|13| **Two‑factor authentication (2FA)** | Adds a second barrier beyond password. | Consider integrating TOTP (Google Authenticator) or push‑based 2FA for high‑privilege admin accounts. |

