## SecurityFilterChain in Spring 6 – A “Security‑Researcher” Deep‑Dive  

Spring 6 (and Spring Boot 3) bring a **lambda‑based DSL** that replaces the now‑deprecated `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter`. The central piece is a `SecurityFilterChain` bean that wires the whole filter pipeline. Below you’ll find a **complete walk‑through**:

| What you need to know | Why it matters for security research |
|-----------------------|---------------------------------------|
| **Filter order & the DelegatingFilterProxy** | Guarantees the chain runs **before** any servlet components. Mis‑ordering can expose endpoints to unauthenticated traffic. |
| **Stateless vs. stateful setups** | A stateless JWT‑only API has a **different CSRF** surface than a session‑based UI. |
| **Method‑level security** | `@EnableMethodSecurity` still works, but its semantics changed (e.g., `prePostEnabled` is now the default). |
| **Migration pain points** | `AuthenticationManager` is no longer auto‑exposed; you must declare it as a bean if you need to inject it elsewhere. |
| **Testing hooks** | The new DSL plays nicely with `@Import(SecurityConfiguration.class)` and `MockMvc` + `@WithMockUser`. |
| **Common pitfalls & CVEs** | Over‑permissive `requestMatchers("/**")` or disabling CSRF on UI pages can re‑introduce the *Spring4Shell*‑style vulnerability. |

Below is a **research‑oriented, production‑ready template** plus a **list of gotchas** you should keep an eye on when you audit or harden a Spring 6 app.

---

## 1️⃣ Minimal “Hello‑World” `SecurityFilterChain`

```java
@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity // Enables @PreAuthorize, @Secured, @RolesAllowed
class SecurityConfig {

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣  Authentication (UserDetailsService + PasswordEncoder)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    UserDetailsService userDetailsService() {
        // In real life, replace with a JDBC / LDAP / JPA impl
        return username -> User.withUsername(username)
                .passwordEncoder(password -> passwordEncoder().encode(password))
                .password("secret")      // plain for demo only
                .roles("USER")
                .build();
    }

    @Bean
    PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
        // BCrypt is the de‑facto standard; SCrypt/Bcrypt+Pepper also OK
        return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  AuthenticationManager – needed by custom filters (e.g. JWT)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    AuthenticationManager authenticationManager(
            AuthenticationConfiguration cfg) throws Exception {
        // Delegates to the AuthenticationProvider chain built by Spring
        return cfg.getAuthenticationManager();
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  The SecurityFilterChain bean (the new entry point)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        // ---- 3.1 Core HTTP security defaults ----
        http
            // CSRF: keep enabled for browser UI; disable for pure API
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf
                .ignoringRequestMatchers(
                    // For stateless API endpoints that use JWT
                    request -> "/api/**".equals(request.getServletPath())
                )
            )
