Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** of a minimal HTTPS server written in plain Java 11+ that you can spin up in a few minutes.  
It uses the standard JDK class **`com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpsServer`**, so no external libraries are required.  

---

## 1. What the example does

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **Generate a self‑signed certificate** | A PKCS#12 keystore (`server.p12`) is created with a single RSA key/certificate pair. |
| **Start the server** | The server binds to `https://localhost:8443/` and serves a single endpoint (`/`) that returns a short “Hello, HTTPS!” message. |
| **TLS configuration** | Uses TLS 1.2/1.3, a modern cipher suite list, and requests client authentication (optional). |
| **Graceful shutdown** | Press `Enter` in the console to stop the server cleanly. |

> **⚠️ Security note** – This code is deliberately simple and intended for testing, learning, or internal tooling. For production you should:
> * Use a certificate signed by a trusted CA (or an internal PKI).  
> * Store the keystore in a protected location with a strong password.  
> * Harden the TLS configuration (e.g., disable weak ciphers, enable OCSP stapling, use HSTS headers, etc.).  
> * Run behind a reverse‑proxy or a dedicated HTTP server for logging, rate‑limiting, etc.

---

## 2. Create a keystore (once)

Open a terminal and run:

```bash
# Java 11+ (keytool ships with the JDK)
# Replace "changeit" with a strong password of your own choice.
keytool -genkeypair \
  -alias https-server \
  -keyalg RSA \
  -keysize 2048 \
  -sigalg SHA256withRSA \
  -validity 365 \
  -storetype PKCS12 \
  -keystore server.p12 \
  -storepass changeit \
  -dname "CN=localhost, OU=Demo, O=Acme, L=City, S=State, C=US"
```

*The generated file `server.p12` must be placed in the same directory as the Java source (or you can point to another location via `System.setProperty` in the code).*  

If you need **mutual TLS** (client certificates), generate a separate truststore containing the client CA certificates and point `HttpsConfigurator` to it (see the comments in the code).

---

## 3. Java source – `SimpleHttpsServer.java`

```java
import com.sun.net.httpserver.*;

import javax.net.ssl.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.security.*;
import java.security.cert.CertificateException;
import java.util.*;

public class SimpleHttpsServer {

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Configuration constants – adjust for your environment
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    private static final int    HTTPS_PORT = 8443;
    private static final String KEYSTORE_PATH = "server.p12";      // relative to working dir
    private static final String KEYSTORE_PASSWORD = "changeit";   // same as used in keytool
    private static final String KEYSTORE_TYPE = "PKCS12";

    // Optional: enable client‑auth (set to true if you have a trust store)
    private static final boolean REQUIRE_CLIENT_AUTH = false;
    private static final String TRUSTSTORE_PATH = "truststore.p12";
    private static final String TRUSTSTORE_PASSWORD = "changeit";

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        // 1️⃣ Load the server keystore (private key + cert)
        KeyStore ks = KeyStore.getInstance(KEYSTORE_TYPE);
        try (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(KEYSTORE_PATH)) {
            ks.load(fis, KEYSTORE_PASSWORD.toCharArray());
        }

        // 2️⃣ Initialise a KeyManagerFactory with the keystore
        KeyManagerFactory kmf = KeyManagerFactory.getInstance(
                KeyManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm());
        kmf.init(ks, KEYSTORE_PASSWORD.toCharArray());

        // 3️⃣ (Optional) Load a truststore for client‑auth
        TrustManagerFactory tmf = null;
        if (REQUIRE_CLIENT_AUTH) {
            KeyStore ts = KeyStore.getInstance(KEYSTORE_TYPE);
            try (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(TRUSTSTORE_PATH)) {
                ts.load(fis, TRUSTSTORE_PASSWORD.toCharArray());
            }
            tmf = TrustManagerFactory.getInstance(
                    TrustManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm());
            tmf.init(ts);
        }

        // 4️⃣ Create and initialise the SSLContext
        SSLContext sslContext = SSLContext.getInstance("TLS");
        sslContext.init(
                kmf.getKeyManagers(),
                (tmf != null) ? tmf.getTrustManagers() : null,
                new SecureRandom()
        );

        // 5️⃣ Build the HTTPS server
        HttpsServer httpsServer = HttpsServer.create(
                new InetSocketAddress(HTTPS_PORT), 0);

        // 6️⃣ Configure TLS parameters (cipher suites, protocols, client auth)
        httpsServer.setHttpsConfigurator(new HttpsConfigurator(sslContext) {
            public void configure(HttpsParameters params) {
                // Get the default SSL parameters
                SSLParameters sslParams = getSSLContext().getDefaultSSLParameters();

                // ---- TLS version list (modern defaults) ----
                sslParams.setProtocols(new String[] { "TLSv1.3", "TLSv1.2" });

                // ---- Strong cipher suites (filter out any RSA key‑exchange) ----
                // You can also rely on the JVM defaults – they already exclude
                // weak suites. Below is an example whitelist.
                List<String> strongCiphers = new ArrayList<>();
                for (String cs : sslParams.getCipherSuites()) {
                    // keep only AEAD + forward‑secrecy suites
                    if (cs.contains("_AES_") && cs.contains("_GCM_")
                            && (cs.contains("_ECDHE_") || cs.contains("_DHE_"))) {
                        strongCiphers.add(cs);
                    }
                }
                sslParams.setCipherSuites(strongCiphers.toArray(new String[0]));

                // ---- Client authentication (optional) ----
                if (REQUIRE_CLIENT_AUTH) {
                    sslParams.setNeedClientAuth(true);   // or setWantClientAuth(true)
                }

                // Apply the parameters to the connection
                params.setSSLParameters(sslParams);
            }
        });

        // 7️⃣ Register a simple handler
        httpsServer.createContext("/", new RootHandler());

        // 8️⃣ (Optional) Add a filter to log request details
        httpsServer.createContext("/debug", new DebugHandler())
                   .getFilters().add(new LoggingFilter());

        // 9️⃣ Start the server
        httpsServer.setExecutor(null); // default single‑threaded executor; replace with a thread pool if needed
        httpsServer.start();

        System.out.printf("✅ HTTPS server listening on https://localhost:%d/%n", HTTPS_PORT);
        System.out.println("Press <Enter> to stop…");
        System.in.read(); // block until Enter

        // 10️⃣ Clean shutdown
        httpsServer.stop(0);
        System.out.println("🛑 Server stopped.");
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Simple handler that returns a plain‑text greeting
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    static class RootHandler implements HttpHandler {
        @Override
        public void handle(HttpExchange exchange) throws IOException {
            String response = "Hello, HTTPS!\n";
            exchange.getResponseHeaders().add("Content-Type", "text/plain; charset=UTF-8");
            // HSTS (recommended for any production HTTPS service)
            exchange.getResponseHeaders().add("Strict-Transport-Security", "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains");
            exchange.sendResponseHeaders(200, response.getBytes().length);
            try (OutputStream os = exchange.getResponseBody()) {
                os.write(response.getBytes());
            }
        }
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Debug handler – dumps request headers & body (useful while developing)
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    static class DebugHandler implements HttpHandler {
        @Override
        public void handle(HttpExchange exchange) throws IOException {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            sb.append("Method: ").append(exchange.getRequestMethod()).append('\n');
            sb.append("URI: ").append(exchange.getRequestURI()).append('\n');
            sb.append("Headers:\n");
            exchange.getRequestHeaders().forEach((k, v) -> sb.append("  ").append(k).append(": ").append(String.join(", ", v)).append('\n'));

            // Read request body (if any)
            InputStream is = exchange.getRequestBody();
            String body = new String(is.readAllBytes());
            sb.append("Body:\n").append(body).append('\n');

            String response = sb.toString();
            exchange.getResponseHeaders().add("Content-Type", "text/plain; charset=UTF-8");
            exchange.sendResponseHeaders(200, response.getBytes().length);
            try (OutputStream os = exchange.getResponseBody()) {
                os.write(response.getBytes());
            }
        }
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Simple logging filter – prints the remote address and request line
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    static class LoggingFilter extends Filter {
        @Override
        public void doFilter(HttpExchange exchange, Chain chain) throws IOException {
            System.out.printf("%s %s %s%n",
                    exchange.getRemoteAddress(),
                    exchange.getRequestMethod(),
                    exchange.getRequestURI());
            chain.doFilter(exchange);
        }

        @Override
        public String description() {
            return "Logs remote address, HTTP method and URI";
        }
    }
}
```

