## 5. Multipart parsing utility – `MultipartParser.java`

We wrap Apache Commons FileUpload so that the rest of the code stays tidy. You could embed the full parsing directly in `ImageUploadHandler`, but a tiny separate class keeps responsibilities clear.

```java
package com.example;

import org.apache.commons.fileupload.*;
import org.apache.commons.fileupload.util.Streams;
import org.apache.commons.fileupload.disk.DiskFileItemFactory;

import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

/**
 * Light‑weight wrapper around Commons‑FileUpload that parses a raw InputStream
 * given the full Content‑Type header (including the boundary token).
 */
public class MultipartParser {

    private final InputStream input;
    private final String contentType;

    public MultipartParser(InputStream input, String contentType) {
        this.input = input;
        this.contentType = contentType;
    }

    /**
     * Parses the multipart request and returns a list of FileItem objects.
     */
    public List<FileItem> parse() throws FileUploadException, IOException {
        // We use a DiskFileItemFactory with a tiny in‑memory threshold because
        // we want to stay away from the default temporary directory (less I/O).
        DiskFileItemFactory factory = new DiskFileItemFactory();
        factory.setSizeThreshold(1024 * 1024); // 1 MiB kept in RAM, rest goes to temp file

        // The parser expects a *RequestContext* – we implement it inline.
        RequestContext rc = new RequestContext() {
            @Override public String getCharacterEncoding() { return "UTF-8"; }
            @Override public String getContentType() { return contentType; }
            @Override public int getContentLength() { return -1; } // unknown length
            @Override public InputStream getInputStream() { return input; }
        };

        FileUpload upload = new FileUpload(factory);
        // Safety guard – the same limit we enforce later, just in case.
        upload.setSizeMax(ImageUploadHandler.MAX_FILE_SIZE * 2);
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        List<FileItem> items = upload.parseRequest(rc);
        return items;
    }
}
```

