## 1. Architectural Overview

```
+-------------------+          TCP (TLS)          +-------------------+
|   Desktop PC      | <-------------------------> | Android Phone    |
|  (Java SE 17+)   |   Task Manager (Server)    | (Java 17 / Android)
|  ExecutorService |   ←  Object streams   →     |  ExecutorService |
+-------------------+                           +-------------------+

Key components
------------------------------
PC
 • TaskManagerServer – accepts connections, pushes tasks, receives results.
 • TaskQueue – thread‑safe in‑memory queue (LinkedBlockingQueue).
 • WorkerPool – ExecutorService / ForkJoinPool that pulls from the queue.
 • ResultAggregator – collects returned PartialResult objects.

Android
 • TaskManagerClient – opens a persistent socket to the PC, serialises tasks,
   reads partial results.
 • WorkerPool – same model as PC (but limited threads, UI‑thread safe).
 • Service (foreground) – keeps the network connection alive even when the
   UI is not active.
```

*Why use plain Java I/O?* – It is the simplest “lowest‑common‑denominator” that works on both Java SE and Android, requires no extra dependencies, and lets you illustrate the core concepts (serialization, thread‑pools, security boundaries).

*Why TLS?* – By wrapping the raw `Socket` in an `SSLSocket` you get confidentiality and server authentication for free; the client can also present a client certificate if you need mutual auth.

