## 2. Common Data Model (Serializable Task / Result)

Both sides must understand the same classes. Keep them in a **shared library** (`common.jar`) that you compile once and put on both the PC and the Android APK.

```java
// src/common/Task.java
package common;

import java.io.Serializable;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * A generic unit of work. You can extend this class for different
 * algorithms (e.g. matrix multiplication block, image‑filter tile, etc.).
 */
public class Task implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    // Simple example: compute the sum of an integer array segment.
    private final int[] payload;
    private final int taskId;                 // for correlating results

    public Task(int taskId, int[] payload) {
        this.taskId = taskId;
        this.payload = payload;
    }

    public int getTaskId() { return taskId; }
    public int[] getPayload() { return payload; }
}
```

```java
// src/common/PartialResult.java
package common;

import java.io.Serializable;

/**
 * Result produced by ONE worker (PC OR Android). The PC will aggregate
 * many PartialResult objects into a FinalResult.
 */
public class PartialResult implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    private final int taskId;
    private final long sum;               // simple example result

    public PartialResult(int taskId, long sum) {
        this.taskId = taskId;
        this.sum = sum;
    }

    public int getTaskId() { return taskId; }
    public long getSum() { return sum; }
}
```

You can replace the simple `int[]` payload with any `Serializable` data structure (e.g. `byte[]` for image tiles, protobuf messages, etc.). The key is that **both sides agree on the binary representation**.

