### 4.3. Android Side – Service + ExecutorService

Because Android apps can be killed when they go to the background, keep the networking thread **alive** by using a **foreground Service** (or a `WorkManager` if you want periodic background work).  

Below is a minimalist foreground service that:

* Opens an `SSLSocket` to the PC.
* Receives a `Task`, hands it to a `ThreadPoolExecutor`.
* Sends back the `PartialResult`.

> **Important** – Android does not allow network I/O on the UI thread. All streams must run on a background thread.

```java
// app/src/main/java/com/example/cluster/ClusterService.java
package com.example.cluster;

import android.app.*;
import android.content.Intent;
import android.os.*;
import androidx.annotation.Nullable;
import androidx.core.app.NotificationCompat;
import common.*;

import javax.net.ssl.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.security.KeyStore;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ClusterService extends Service {

    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    // Service lifecycle
    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    private ExecutorService workerPool;
    private SSLSocket socket;
    private ObjectOutputStream out;
    private ObjectInputStream in;
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    // Notification channel for foreground service (Android O+)
    private static final String CHANNEL_ID = "cluster_service";

    @Override
    public void onCreate() {
        super.onCreate();
        createNotificationChannel();
        startForeground(1, buildNotification("Connecting…"));
        initWorkerPool();
        new Thread(this::runClientLoop, "ClusterClient").start();
    }

    @Override
    public int onStartCommand(Intent intent, int flags, int startId) {
        // The service is sticky – it will be restarted if killed.
        return START_STICKY;
    }

    @Override
    public void onDestroy() {
        running = false;
        closeResources();
        workerPool.shutdownNow();
        super.onDestroy();
    }

    @Nullable
    @Override
    public IBinder onBind(Intent intent) {
        return null; // not a bound service
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    // Notification helper (required for Android 8+)
    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    private void createNotificationChannel() {
        if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= Build.VERSION_CODES.O) {
            NotificationChannel ch = new NotificationChannel(CHANNEL_ID,
                    "Cluster Service", NotificationManager.IMPORTANCE_LOW);
            ch.setDescription("Keeps the Android node connected to the PC");
            NotificationManager nm = getSystemService(NotificationManager.class);
            if (nm != null) nm.createNotificationChannel(ch);
        }
    }

    private Notification buildNotification(String text) {
        return new NotificationCompat.Builder(this, CHANNEL_ID)
                .setContentTitle("Cluster Worker")
                .setContentText(text)
                .setSmallIcon(R.drawable.ic_cpu) // your icon
                .build();
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    // Initialise ExecutorService – use the number of cores
    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    private void initWorkerPool() {
        int cores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();
        // ForkJoinPool is not available on Android API < 24; use ThreadPoolExecutor instead.
        workerPool = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
                cores, cores,
                0L, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS,
                new LinkedBlockingQueue<>());
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    // TLS socket creation (client side)
    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    private SSLSocket createSslSocket() throws Exception {
        // Trust the self‑signed PC cert (in a demo you can use a permissive TrustManager)
        TrustManager[] trustAll = new TrustManager[]{ new X509TrustManager() {
            public java.security.cert.X509Certificate[] getAcceptedIssuers() { return new java.security.cert.X509Certificate[0]; }
            public void checkClientTrusted(java.security.cert.X509Certificate[] chain, String authType) {}
            public void checkServerTrusted(java.security.cert.X509Certificate[] chain, String authType) {}
        }};

        SSLContext ctx = SSLContext.getInstance("TLSv1.3");
        ctx.init(null, trustAll, new java.security.SecureRandom());

        SSLSocketFactory f = ctx.getSocketFactory();
        // Replace with your PC's IP/DNS and port
        return (SSLSocket) f.createSocket("192.168.1.100", 8443);
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    // Main client loop – only one thread runs this
    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    private void runClientLoop() {
        try {
            socket = createSslSocket();
            socket.setSoTimeout(0); // infinite read
            out = new ObjectOutputStream(socket.getOutputStream());
            in  = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());

            // Update notification – connected
            startForeground(1, buildNotification("Connected to PC"));
            System.out.println("[Android] TLS connection established.");

            TaskProcessor processor = new SumProcessor(); // same implementation as PC

            while (running) {
                // 1️⃣ Block until PC sends a Task
                Task task = (Task) in.readObject();
                System.out.println("[Android] Received task #" + task.getTaskId());

                // 2️⃣ Submit to the pool and asynchronously send the result.
                workerPool.submit(() -> {
                    try {
                        PartialResult result = processor.process(task);
                        synchronized (out) {   // ObjectOutputStream is not thread‑safe
                            out.writeObject(result);
                            out.flush();
                        }
                        System.out.println("[Android] Sent result for task #" + task.getTaskId());
                    } catch (IOException e) {
                        e.printStackTrace();
                    }
                });
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            closeResources();
        }
    }

    private void closeResources() {
        try { if (out != null) out.close(); } catch (Exception ignored) {}
        try { if (in  != null) in .close(); } catch (Exception ignored) {}
        try { if (socket != null) socket.close(); } catch (Exception ignored) {}
    }
}
```

**Manifest entry** (required for a foreground service on Android 12+):

```xml
<service
    android:name=".ClusterService"
    android:foregroundServiceType="connectedDevice"
    android:exported="false"/>
```

**How it works**

| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| **TLS handshake** | `createSslSocket()` does a permissive trust‑all for demo only; replace with a proper `TrustManager` that validates the PC’s certificate. |
| **Task receipt** | `in.readObject()` blocks until the PC writes a `Task`. |
| **Thread‑pool processing** | `workerPool.submit()` executes `processor.process(task)` on a background thread, keeping the socket thread free to receive the next `Task`. |
| **Result send‑back** | The write operation is `synchronized` because `ObjectOutputStream` must not be used concurrently. |
| **Service resilience** | `START_STICKY` and a foreground notification keep Android from killing the process under memory pressure. |

---

## 5. Putting the Whole System Together

### 5.1. Build & Deploy Steps

| Platform | Build command | Deployment |
|----------|----------------|------------|
| **Common JAR** | `javac -d out src/common/**/*.java && jar cf common.jar -C out .` | Include `common.jar` on both the PC classpath and the Android Gradle module (`implementation files('libs/common.jar')`). |
| **PC** | `javac -cp common.jar -d out src/pc/**/*.java && jar cf pc.jar -C out .` | Run `java -cp pc.jar:common.jar pc.TaskManagerServer`. |
| **Android** | Add `common.jar` under `app/libs/`; Gradle sync. <br> Add `<service ...>` to `AndroidManifest.xml`. | Build APK, install on device, start the service (`adb shell am startservice -n com.example.cluster/.ClusterService`). |

### 5.2. Simple Test Run

1. **Start the PC server** – it will listen on `8443`.  
2. **Launch the Android foreground service** – you’ll see a persistent notification.  
3. **On PC, invoke `demo()`** (or your own job‑submission code).  
4. **Observe the console** – tasks should alternate between **local PC computation** and **remote Android computation**.  
5. Verify the aggregated sums match the expected total (`Σ Σ payload[i]`).

---

## 6. Security‑Research‑Focused Hardening Checklist

| Area | Threat | Mitigation |
|------|--------|------------|
| **Transport** | Eavesdropping / MITM | TLS 1.3 with server (and optionally client) certificates. Pin the server cert on Android (`X509TrustManager`). |
| **Authentication** | Rogue Android device hijacking the PC | Mutual TLS + per‑device token (e.g. JWT) sent as the first message after handshake. |
| **Authorization** | A compromised Android worker demanding all tasks | Keep a **task‑type whitelist** on the server; reject unknown or oversized payloads. |
| **Code Execution** | Malicious payload causing over‑flows or OOM | In `TaskProcessor.process`, enforce max array length (`payload.length <= MAX_LEN`). Run inside a `java.security.AccessControlContext` that denies `Runtime.exec` and file writes. |
| **Denial‑of‑Service** | Flood of connection attempts | Rate‑limit connections (e.g. `TokenBucket` per client IP). Set `ServerSocket.setReuseAddress(false)`. |
| **Resource Exhaustion** | Worker threads starve the system | Use `ThreadPoolExecutor` with bounded queue (`ArrayBlockingQueue`) and a “rejection handler” that sends back a `TaskFailed` message. |
| **Data Integrity** | Corrupted serialization stream | Add a small CRC (e.g. Adler32) around each `Task` / `PartialResult` payload, verify before deserialising. |
| **Side‑Channel Leakage** | Timing‑based inference of PC workload | Randomise task‑chunk sizes or insert small sleep jitter before sending each task. |
| **Android Lifecycle** | Service killed → orphaned sockets | Use `START_STICKY` + `onTaskRemoved()` to reconnect, or `WorkManager` with `constraints { network }`. |
| **Compliance** | Export‑controlled algorithms (e.g. encryption) | Ensure you have proper licensing to ship any third‑party crypto libraries. |

---

## 7. Extending the Prototype (Optional Enhancements)

| Feature | How to add it |
|---------|----------------|
| **Multiple Android workers** | Keep a concurrent `Map<Socket, WorkerState>` on the PC. Load‑balance by round‑robin or by measuring each socket’s average round‑trip time. |
| **Dynamic work‑stealing** | If the PC queue empties, request more work from Android (`TaskStealRequest`). Conversely, Android can request “burst” tasks when its CPU is idle. |
| **Better serialization** | Replace Java native `ObjectOutputStream` with **Protocol Buffers** (`protobuf-java`) or **FlatBuffers** for smaller payloads and language‑agnostic clients. |
| **Fault tolerance** | Persist all incoming tasks to a local **SQLite** DB; on restart, replay unfinished tasks. |
| **Monitoring & UI** | Expose a tiny HTTP endpoint (`/metrics`) on the PC (via **Jetty** or **NanoHTTPD**) and a simple Android Activity that shows “tasks processed / sec”. |
| **GPU off‑load** | On PC, replace `SumProcessor` with an OpenCL kernel; on Android, use **RenderScript** (deprecated) or **Vulkan Compute** for heavy linear algebra. |
| **Containerised deployment** | Run the PC server inside a Docker container; expose only the TLS port. Use `docker run -p 8443:8443 …`. |
| **Zero‑trust mesh** | Use **WireGuard** or **Tailscale** to give each device a stable, encrypted VPN address; simplifies network configuration. |

---

## 8. Summary – Your “Comparative Advantage” Blueprint

| What you gain | How it maps to the code |
|---------------|--------------------------|
| **Cross‑platform compute** | The same `Task`/`PartialResult` POJOs run on Java SE *and* Android (no platform‑specific APIs). |
| **Scalable parallelism** | `ExecutorService` / `ForkJoinPool` on each node automatically uses all cores. You can increase parallelism by simply raising the pool size. |
| **Low‑latency, binary‑efficient messaging** | Java’s `ObjectOutputStream` over TLS is fast enough for medium‑size payloads; you can swap in protobuf later without redesigning the pool logic. |
| **Fine‑grained control over security** | TLS + optional client‑certs + explicit validation of payload size give you a lab‑grade hardened channel. |
| **Extensible architecture** | Adding more workers, swapping in a GPU backend, or persisting tasks only requires plugging a new `TaskProcessor` implementation. |
| **Research‑friendly** | The whole stack lives in pure Java, meaning you can instrument, log, and even apply byte‑code instrumentation (e.g. **ASM** or **Byte Buddy**) to study execution characteristics. |

---

### TL;DR – “Here’s the recipe”

1. **Create a shared `common.jar`** with `Task`, `PartialResult`, and a `TaskProcessor` implementation.  
2. **On the PC**, spin a **TLS server socket**, hold a **BlockingQueue<Task>**, and expose a `submitTask(Task)` → `CompletableFuture<PartialResult>` API.  
3. **On Android**, run a **foreground Service** that makes a **TLS client socket**, reads `Task`s, hands them to a **ThreadPoolExecutor**, and writes back `PartialResult`s.  
4. **Both nodes** use **ExecutorService / ForkJoinPool** to parallelise local work.  
5. **Add TLS, input validation, resource limits, and a watchdog** to turn the prototype into a production‑grade research platform.

You now have a **complete, end‑to‑end, security‑aware clustering framework** that lets an Android CPU and a desktop PC CPU cooperate as a single parallel compute cluster. Happy hacking, and remember to keep the TLS keys out of version control! 🚀

