## 7. Global Hot‑Key Listener – `HotKeyListener.java`

```java
package com.example.mouserecorder;

import org.jnativehook.GlobalScreen;
import org.jnativehook.NativeHookException;
import org.jnativehook.keyboard.*;

import java.awt.AWTException;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference;
import java.util.logging.Level;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

/**
 * Registers Shift+P (start) and Shift+L (stop) globally.
 *
 * When start is pressed:
 *   - asks the user for a name (via a Swing `JOptionPane`);
 *   - creates a new `Recorder` and begins sampling.
 *
 * When stop is pressed:
 *   - stops the current Recorder;
 *   - saves the recording into the provided map and persists it.
 */
public class HotKeyListener implements NativeKeyListener {

    private final Map<String, Recording> recordings; // shared map
    private final AtomicReference<Recorder> activeRecorder = new AtomicReference<>(null);
    private final AtomicReference<String> activeName = new AtomicReference<>(null);

    public HotKeyListener(Map<String, Recording> recordings) {
        this.recordings = recordings;
    }

    /** Must be called once at app start. */
    public static void install(Map<String, Recording> recordings) throws NativeHookException {
        // Silence the noisy JNativeHook logger (log4j default)
        Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(GlobalScreen.class.getPackage().getName());
        logger.setLevel(Level.WARNING);

        GlobalScreen.registerNativeHook();
        GlobalScreen.addNativeKeyListener(new HotKeyListener(recordings));
    }

    @Override
    public void nativeKeyPressed(NativeKeyEvent e) {
        // Shift + P => start
        if (e.getKeyCode() == NativeKeyEvent.VC_P && e.getModifiers() == NativeKeyEvent.SHIFT_MASK) {
            startRecording();
        }

        // Shift + L => stop
        if (e.getKeyCode() == NativeKeyEvent.VC_L && e.getModifiers() == NativeKeyEvent.SHIFT_MASK) {
            stopRecording();
        }
    }

    @Override public void nativeKeyReleased(NativeKeyEvent e) {}
    @Override public void nativeKeyTyped(NativeKeyEvent e) {}

    private void startRecording() {
        if (activeRecorder.get() != null) {
            System.out.println("Already recording – ignore start request");
            return;
        }

        // Ask user for a friendly name (on EDT)
        String name = javax.swing.JOptionPane.showInputDialog(
                null,
                "Enter a name for this recording:",
                "New Mouse Recording",
                javax.swing.JOptionPane.QUESTION_MESSAGE);

        if (name == null || name.trim().isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("User cancelled or entered empty name – abort start");
            return;
        }
        name = name.trim();

        // Guard against name collision – append a number if needed
        String uniqueName = name;
        int suffix = 1;
        while (recordings.containsKey(uniqueName)) {
            uniqueName = name + "_" + suffix++;
        }

        Recording rec = new Recording(uniqueName);
        try {
            Recorder r = new Recorder(rec);
            r.start();
            activeRecorder.set(r);
            activeName.set(uniqueName);
            System.out.println("Recording started (name='" + uniqueName + "')");
        } catch (AWTException ex) {
            System.err.println("Failed to create Robot for recording: " + ex);
        }
    }

    private void stopRecording() {
        Recorder r = activeRecorder.getAndSet(null);
        String name = activeName.getAndSet(null);
        if (r == null) {
            System.out.println("No active recording – ignore stop request");
            return;
        }

        r.stopRecording();
        System.out.println("Recording stopped (name='" + name + "')");

        // Store and persist
        Recording rec = r.recording;
        recordings.put(name, rec);
        RecordingStore.save(recordings);
        System.out.println("Recording saved – now contains " + recordings.size() + " entry(ies)");
    }
}
```

> **Security note:**  
> * The hot‑key listener runs with **native OS privileges** (it injects a DLL). This is a privileged operation; on macOS you must grant “Input Monitoring”, on Windows “Accessibility/Automation” permission, and on Linux you may need to be in the `input` group.  
> * **Never** ship this component without a clear **user‑consent dialog** explaining that the program will capture mouse motion globally.  
> * Store recordings **encrypted** if they may contain sensitive UI locations (e.g., login fields). The example stores plain JSON for simplicity, but you should wrap the JSON with an AES‑256 cipher and a user‑derived key (PBKDF2).  
> * When doing a replay, consider adding a **failsafe** (e.g., moving the mouse to a corner of the screen aborts the playback) to prevent accidental “run‑away” automation.

