## 3️⃣ Security Checklist – Treat Pickles Like **Executable Code**

| ✅ | Item | Why it matters |
|----|------|-----------------|
| **1** | **Never deserialize a pickle you don’t fully trust**. | `pickle.load()` can execute arbitrary Python code via `__reduce__`, `__setstate__`, etc. |
| **2** | **Run untrusted pickle processing in a sandbox** (Docker, chroot, SELinux, AppArmor). | Limits the impact if malicious code runs. |
| **3** | **Drop privileges** for the Python process (e.g., `nobody` user, read‑only file system). | Mitigates file system or network abuse. |
| **4** | **Validate the content** after deserialization (type checks, size limits, schema validation). | Prevents logic bugs downstream. |
| **5** | **Prefer data‑only formats** (JSON, Protocol Buffers, Avro) for any public API. | Removes the code‑execution vector entirely. |
| **6** | **Log and monitor** deserialization attempts (process start, exit codes, any exceptions). | Detects abuse attempts early. |
| **7** | **Patch your libraries** – keep Jython, Python interpreter, and any pickle‑parsing libs up‑to‑date. | Reduces exposure to known CVEs (e.g., CVE‑2020‑15523 in `pickle`). |
| **8** | **Consider a “safe unpickler”** – custom Python subclass that overrides `find_class` and raises on unknown types. | Example (Python side):<br>`class SafeUnpickler(pickle.Unpickler):  def find_class(self, module, name):  raise pickle.UnpicklingError("Disallowed class")` |
| **9** | **If you must allow untrusted data**, convert it **once** in a hardened environment to a safe format and store that safe version. | Subsequent Java reads will never touch a pickle again. |
| **10**| **Document assumptions** – make it crystal clear in code/comments which pickles are “trusted” vs “untrusted”. | Future maintainers won’t accidentally relax security. |

