## 2️⃣  Why This Code Is *Secure* (Researcher’s Commentary)

| # | Issue | How the Sample Mitigates It |
|---|-------|------------------------------|
| **1** | **Clear‑text credentials on the network** | Uses **IMAPS** (`mail.store.protocol = imaps`) which forces TLS encryption. No plaintext SMTP/IMAP ports. |
| **2** | **Hard‑coded passwords** | Pulls **`IMAP_USER`** / **`IMAP_PASS`** from **environment variables**. In production you’d integrate with a secret manager (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, etc.). |
| **3** | **Debug logging leaking credentials** | `session.setDebug(false)` – disables JavaMail’s verbose debug (which prints passwords). |
| **4** | **Untrusted email content (HTML/JS, MIME tricks)** | Only parses **`text/plain`** parts. `extractPlainText` ignores HTML, avoids DOM‑based XSS or script execution. |
| **5** | **Potential DoS via huge mail bodies** | Sets reasonable connection & socket time‑outs (`mail.imaps.timeout`). You could also add a **max‑size** check before reading the body. |
| **6** | **Repeated processing of the same mail** | Searches only for **UNSEEN** messages (`FlagTerm`). After processing you may mark them SEEN (`INBOX.setFlags`). |
| **7** | **Thread‑safety / race conditions** | Uses the legacy **`Hashtable`** (synchronized). For modern code an explicit `ConcurrentHashMap` is preferred. |
| **8** | **Injection attacks when storing data** | The map only stores **plain strings**; there is **no SQL** or OS command invocation. If you later persist these values, always use prepared statements / parameterised APIs. |
| **9** | **Resource leakage** | All I/O objects (`Store`, `Folder`) are wrapped in **try‑with‑resources**, guaranteeing clean closure even on exceptions. |
| **10** | **Unvalidated numeric data** | Regex `\\b\\d{8}\\b` enforces exactly eight digits, preventing overly long strings or embedded code. You can tighten this (e.g., check range, checksum). |

