    /** -----------------------------------------------------------------------
     *   Boiler‑plate bean that creates the ReactiveRedisTemplate used above.
     *   In a real‑world project you would probably move this out into a dedicated
     *   @Configuration class – see the “Configuration” section later.
     *   ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    @Bean
    ReactiveRedisTemplate<String, Long> reactiveRedisTemplate(ReactiveRedisConnectionFactory factory) {
        JdkSerializationRedisSerializer keySer = new JdkSerializationRedisSerializer();
        StringRedisSerializer    stringSer = StringRedisSerializer.UTF_8;
        GenericToStringSerializer<Long> valueSer = new GenericToStringSerializer<>(Long.class);

        return new ReactiveRedisTemplate<>(factory,
                RedisSerializationContext.<String, Long>newSerializationContext(keySer)
                        .key(stringSer)
                        .value(valueSer)
                        .build());
    }

    /** -----------------------------------------------------------------------
     *   Helper – extracts host name from the optional remote address.
     *   ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    private String requestAddress(Optional<InetSocketAddress> maybeAddress) {
        return maybeAddress.map(InetSocketAddress::getHostString).orElse("unknown");
    }

    /** -----------------------------------------------------------------------
     *   The “transaction‑like” part – increment the counter and set its TTL.
     *   We use `ReactiveRedisTemplate.execute(...)` which guarantees that the
     *   two commands run on the same physical connection (no interleaving).
     *   ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    private Mono<ServerResponse> incrAndExpireKey(String key,
                                                   ServerRequest request,
                                                   HandlerFunction<ServerResponse> next) {

        return redisTemplate.execute(connection -> {
            ByteBuffer bbKey = ByteBuffer.wrap(key.getBytes());
            return Mono.zip(
                    connection.numberCommands().incr(bbKey),               // INCR key
                    connection.keyCommands().expire(bbKey, Duration.ofSeconds(59L)) // EXPIRE key 59
            ).then(Mono.empty());   // we ignore the results – they’re not needed here
        }).then(next.handle(request));
    }
}
```

**Key changes you must apply before moving on**

| Issue | Why it matters | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| `@Value` on a **static** field | Spring can’t inject into static fields → `MAX_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE` will stay `null`/default → rate‑limit never triggers. | Remove `static`. Add a sensible default (`:20`) or use `@ConfigurationProperties`. |
| Bean definition (`@Bean reactiveRedisTemplate`) lives **inside** the filter class. | This works, but mixing concerns makes the class harder to test and to reuse. | Extract the bean into a dedicated `@Configuration` class (see below). |
| The filter uses **host name** from `InetSocketAddress`. <br> In many deployments the real client IP is hidden behind a load‑balancer or CDN. | An attacker can spoof the host name → bypass the limiter. | Inspect `X‑Forwarded‑For` or `X‑Real-IP` headers (or configure Spring’s `ForwardedHeaderFilter`). |

---

## 2️⃣  Wiring everything in Spring‑WebFlux (no Gateway)

Below are two **flavours** you can choose from, depending on whether you prefer the *functional router* style or the classic `@RestController` style.

### 2.1  Functional routing (the natural home of `HandlerFilterFunction`)

```java
package com.mediaworld.apimanager.web;

import com.mediaworld.apimanager.configuration.redis.RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.*;

@Configuration
public class RouterConfig {

    /** ------------------------------------------------------------
     *  Declare the actual endpoint handler (your business logic)
     *  ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    @Bean
    public HandlerFunction<ServerResponse> helloHandler() {
        return request -> ServerResponse.ok()
                .bodyValue("👋 Hello from the API – you are not rate‑limited!");
    }

    /** ------------------------------------------------------------
     *  Build the router and *apply* the filter to the whole route
     *  ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    @Bean
    public RouterFunction<ServerResponse> routes(
            HandlerFunction<ServerResponse> helloHandler,
            RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction rateLimiter) {

        return RouterFunctions.route()
                .GET("/api/hello", helloHandler)   // any HTTP verb works
                .filter(rateLimiter)               // <-- the rate‑limiter is attached here
                .build();
    }
}
```

**What happens at runtime?**

1. A request arrives at `/api/hello`.
2. The router creates a `ServerRequest` and the `RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction.filter(...)` method runs **first**.
3. If the request is under the limit, `incrAndExpireKey` updates the Redis counter and `next.handle(request)` (the `helloHandler`) is invoked.
4. If the limit is exceeded, Spring immediately returns **429 Too Many Requests**.

### 2.2  Classic `@RestController` + a **WebFilter** that delegates to the same logic

If you already have a lot of `@RestController` classes and you don’t want to rewrite them as functional routes, you can install a **WebFlux `WebFilter`** that re‑uses the same `RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction` implementation.

```java
package com.mediaworld.apimanager.web;

import com.mediaworld.apimanager.configuration.redis.RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.HandlerStrategies;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.ServerRequest;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import org.springframework.web.server.WebFilter;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

/**
 * Bridge that adapts the functional HandlerFilterFunction into a
 * WebFlux WebFilter so it works with @RestController endpoints.
 */
@Configuration
public class FilterBridgeConfig {

    /** ------------------------------------------------------------
     *  Transform the HandlerFilterFunction into a WebFilter
     *  ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    @Bean
    public WebFilter rateLimiterWebFilter(RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction limiter) {

        // MessageReaders/MessageWriters are required to create a ServerRequest from the exchange
        var strategies = HandlerStrategies.builder().build();

        return (ServerWebExchange exchange, WebFilterChain chain) -> {

            // Build a ServerRequest compatible with the existing limiter
            ServerRequest serverRequest = ServerRequest.create(exchange, strategies.messageReaders());

            // The "next" lambda that continues the filter chain after the rate‑limiter
            HandlerFunction<ServerResponse> next = req -> {
                // `chain.filter` returns a `Mono<Void>`.  We need to adapt it to `Mono<ServerResponse>`
                return ServerResponse.fromBareResponse(exchange.getResponse())
                        .flatMap(resp -> resp.writeWith(chain.filter(exchange)));
            };

            // Delegate to the existing functional filter
            return limiter.filter(serverRequest, next)
                    // The filter returns a ServerResponse – we have to write it into the exchange
                    .flatMap(response -> response.writeTo(exchange, strategies));
        };
    }
}
```

> **Why wrap the functional filter instead of writing a brand‑new WebFilter?**  
> • You get **single source of truth** – the rate‑limit algorithm lives in one place.  
> • You keep the original, unit‑tested `RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction`.  
> • You can still use the same filter for functional routes **and** for classic controllers.

### 2.3  Putting the configuration together (Redis & the filter bean)

Create a dedicated configuration class that provides the *Redis factory*, the *template* and the *filter* bean.

```java
package com.mediaworld.apimanager.configuration.redis;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.data.redis.connection.ReactiveRedisConnectionFactory;
import org.springframework.data.redis.connection.lettuce.LettuceConnectionFactory;

/**
 * Centralised Redis configuration.
 */
@Configuration
public class RedisConfig {

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣  Lettuce (or Redisson) connection factory.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    public ReactiveRedisConnectionFactory reactiveRedisConnectionFactory() {
        // Adjust host/port as required (or externalise via properties)
        return new LettuceConnectionFactory("localhost", 6379);
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  ReactiveRedisTemplate – the bean is also used inside the filter
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    public ReactiveRedisTemplate<String, Long> reactiveRedisTemplate(
            ReactiveRedisConnectionFactory factory) {

        JdkSerializationRedisSerializer    keySer   = new JdkSerializationRedisSerializer();
        StringRedisSerializer              strSer   = StringRedisSerializer.UTF_8;
        GenericToStringSerializer<Long>    valSer   = new GenericToStringSerializer<>(Long.class);

        return new ReactiveRedisTemplate<>(factory,
                RedisSerializationContext.<String, Long>newSerializationContext(keySer)
                        .key(strSer)
                        .value(valSer)
                        .build());
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  The rate‑limiter bean that will be injected wherever needed
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    public RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction rateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction(
            ReactiveRedisTemplate<String, Long> redisTemplate) {
        return new RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction(redisTemplate);
    }
}
```

> **Note** – If you already have a `LettuceConnectionFactory` bean defined elsewhere (e.g. via Spring Boot’s `spring.redis.*` properties) you can simply *autowire* it here instead of creating a new one.

