## 5️⃣  How to write the binary payload to the NFC tag

| Platform | Typical API / Library | Sketch of the write call |
|----------|-----------------------|--------------------------|
| **Android** | `android.nfc.tech.NfcV` / `Ndef` | ```byte[] data = payload; NdefRecord rec = new NdefRecord(NdefRecord.TNF_MIME_MEDIA, "application/octet-stream".getBytes(), new byte[0], data); NdefMessage msg = new NdefMessage(new NdefRecord[]{rec}); ndefTag.writeNdefMessage(msg);``` |
| **Java (desktop) + libnfc** | `org.lindberg.nfc.NfcDevice` | ```device.write(tagId, payload, 0, payload.length);``` |
| **PHP (CLI) + external tool** | `nfc_write` (Linux) | ```exec("nfc-write -b ".escapeshellarg(bin2hex($payload)))``` |

> **Key point:** Write **raw bytes**, not a Base64 string. Most NFC SDKs allow you to supply a `byte[]` (Java) or a binary buffer (C/Python/Android).  

If you *must* transport the data via a textual medium (e.g. QR‑code for a later write‑in), you can Base64‑encode the payload, but remember that a Base64 string of 48 bytes becomes ~64 bytes (still well within the tag).  

