## 4️⃣ Controller – `FileUploadController.java`

```java
package com.example.fileupload.controller;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.util.StringUtils;
import org.springframework.validation.annotation.Validated;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.web.multipart.MultipartFile;

import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotNull;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/")
@Validated
public class FileUploadController {

    /**
     * Base directory where uploaded files are stored.
     * It is resolved relative to the current working directory.
     */
    private final Path uploadDir;

    /**
     * Allowed file extensions (lower‑case, without leading dot).
     * Adjust according to the type of files you want to accept.
     */
    private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_EXT = Set.of(
            "png", "jpg", "jpeg", "gif",   // images
            "pdf", "txt", "docx"           // documents
    );

    public FileUploadController(@Value("${app.upload.dir:uploads}") String dir) throws IOException {
        this.uploadDir = Paths.get(dir).toAbsolutePath().normalize();
        Files.createDirectories(this.uploadDir); // create if it does not exist
    }

    /** ---------------------------------------------------------
     *  Show a tiny HTML form (GET /)
     *  -------------------------------------------------------*/
    @GetMapping(produces = MediaType.TEXT_HTML_VALUE)
    public String index() {
        return """
                <!DOCTYPE html>
                <html lang="en">
                <head>
                  <meta charset="UTF-8">
                  <title>Simple Java File Upload</title>
                </head>
                <body>
                  <h2>Upload a file</h2>
                  <form method="POST" action="/upload" enctype="multipart/form-data">
                    <input type="file" name="file" required />
                    <button type="submit">Upload</button>
                  </form>
                </body>
                </html>
                """;
    }

    /** ---------------------------------------------------------
     *  Handle the file upload (POST /upload)
     *  -------------------------------------------------------*/
    @PostMapping(value = "/upload",
                 consumes = MediaType.MULTIPART_FORM_DATA_VALUE,
                 produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> uploadFile(
            @RequestParam("file") @NotNull MultipartFile file) {

        // ---- 1️⃣ Basic sanity checks ---------------------------------
        if (file.isEmpty()) {
            return errorResponse("Empty file uploaded.", HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
        }

        // ---- 2️⃣ Enforce size limits (configured in application.properties) ---
        // (Spring already rejects requests that exceed spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size)
        // you may still add an explicit check:
        long maxBytes = 5L * 1024 * 1024; // 5 MiB
        if (file.getSize() > maxBytes) {
            return errorResponse("File exceeds the maximum allowed size of 5 MiB.", HttpStatus.PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE);
        }

        // ---- 3️⃣ Validate filename & extension -------------------------
        String originalFilename = StringUtils.cleanPath(Objects.requireNonNull(file.getOriginalFilename()));
        // Remove path‑traversal characters, e.g. ".."
        if (originalFilename.contains("..")) {
            return errorResponse("Invalid path sequence in filename.", HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
        }

        String ext = com.google.common.io.Files.getFileExtension(originalFilename).toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);
        if (!ALLOWED_EXT.contains(ext)) {
            return errorResponse("File type not allowed. Allowed: " + ALLOWED_EXT, HttpStatus.UNSUPPORTED_MEDIA_TYPE);
        }

        // ---- 4️⃣ Generate a safe, unique filename -----------------------
        // Keep the original extension but replace the name with a UUID.
        String safeFilename = UUID.randomUUID() + "." + ext;

        // ---- 5️⃣ Store the file on disk ---------------------------------
        try {
            Path targetLocation = uploadDir.resolve(safeFilename);
            // Use COPY_ATTRIBUTES to preserve timestamps if you care.
            Files.copy(file.getInputStream(), targetLocation, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
        } catch (IOException ex) {
            return errorResponse("Could not store file: " + ex.getMessage(), HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR);
        }

        // ---- 6️⃣ Return a JSON success payload -------------------------
        Map<String, Object> resp = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        resp.put("message", "File uploaded successfully");
        resp.put("originalFilename", originalFilename);
        resp.put("storedFilename", safeFilename);
        resp.put("size", file.getSize());

        return ResponseEntity.ok(resp);
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    private ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> errorResponse(String msg, HttpStatus status) {
        Map<String, Object> body = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        body.put("error", msg);
        body.put("status", status.value());
        return ResponseEntity.status(status).body(body);
    }
}
```

> **Explanation of the hardening points**
> 1. **Upload directory outside the web‑root** – the `uploads` folder lives beside the JAR, not inside `src/main/resources/static`. This prevents a user from directly requesting the file via a URL unless you explicitly expose it (e.g., through a download controller or a static‑resource mapping with proper controls).  
> 2. **Filename sanitisation** – `StringUtils.cleanPath` removes leading slashes, and we reject any name containing "`..`". The stored filename is a freshly generated UUID, eliminating the risk of overwriting existing files or path traversal.  
> 3. **Allowed extensions** – the whitelist (`ALLOWED_EXT`) mitigates the chance of executable code (e.g., `.jsp`, `.php`, `.sh`) being uploaded. You can replace it with a MIME‑type check (`file.getContentType()`) if you prefer.  
> 4. **Size limits** – the explicit size check plus the Spring Boot configuration (`spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size` and `max-request-size`) stop giant payloads from exhausting RAM/disk.  
> 5. **Exception handling** – we swallow low‑level `IOException`s and return a generic 500 response, avoiding information leakage.

---

