Below is a **complete, production‑grade example** of a small Android‑compatible Java class that:

1. Opens a **TCP connection** to a remote host.
2. Wraps that connection in **TLS/SSL** (using `SSLSocket`).
3. Gives you a **simple read/write API** that you can use from a background thread (or a `Coroutine` / `ExecutorService`).
4. Shows **secure defaults** – proper certificate validation, hostname verification, and the Android‑specific network‑security‑config integration.

> **TL;DR** – Do **not** ship a custom “trust‑all” `X509TrustManager` in production. The code below validates certificates against the system trusted store (or a custom pinned store) and will refuse connections to servers presenting an invalid or self‑signed certificate.

---

## 1. Manifest & Network‑Security‑Config (Android ≥ 7)

Add the Internet permission:

```xml
<!-- AndroidManifest.xml -->
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
```

If you need to **pin** a certificate (highly recommended for critical services) create a network‑security‑config XML and reference it in the manifest:

```xml
<!-- res/xml/network_security_config.xml -->
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<network-security-config>
    <!-- Default system CAs are used -->
    <base-config>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!-- Example of a pinned certificate for a specific host -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">example.com</domain>
        <pin-set expiration="2025-12-31">
            <pin algorithm="sha256">BASE64_ENCODED_SHA256_OF_CERT</pin>
        </pin-set>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
```

Reference it in the manifest (or in `android:networkSecurityConfig` of the `<application>` tag):

```xml
<application
    android:name=".MyApp"
    android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
    ...>
```

> **Why?** Android 7+ (API‑24) blocks clear‑text (non‑TLS) traffic by default when a security config is present, and it enforces hostname verification automatically for `HttpsURLConnection` and `SSLSocketFactory`‑based connections.

---

## 2. The Core Java Class

```java
package com.example.securetcp;

import android.util.Log;

import javax.net.ssl.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.net.Socket;
import java.security.KeyStore;
import java.security.SecureRandom;
import java.security.cert.CertificateException;

/**
 * SecureTcpClient – a thin wrapper around SSLSocket.
 *
 * <p>Usage (from a background thread):
 *
 * <pre>
 *   SecureTcpClient client = new SecureTcpClient("example.com", 443);
 *   client.connect();                 // throws IOException on failure
 *   client.send("HELLO\n".getBytes()); // send raw bytes
 *   String response = client.readLine(); // reads up to LF
 *   client.close();
 * </pre>
 *
 * <p>All I/O is blocking; you must call it off the UI thread.
 */
public final class SecureTcpClient {

    private static final String TAG = "SecureTcpClient";

    private final String host;
    private final int    port;
    private final int    connectTimeoutMs;
    private final int    readTimeoutMs;

    private SSLSocket socket;
    private BufferedReader reader;
    private BufferedWriter writer;

    /**
     * @param host               Remote hostname (will be verified against the certificate)
     * @param port               Remote TLS port (usually 443)
     * @param connectTimeoutMs  Timeout for TCP connect
     * @param readTimeoutMs     Socket read timeout (0 = infinite)
     */
    public SecureTcpClient(String host,
                           int port,
                           int connectTimeoutMs,
                           int readTimeoutMs) {
        this.host = host;
        this.port = port;
        this.connectTimeoutMs = connectTimeoutMs;
        this.readTimeoutMs = readTimeoutMs;
    }

    /** Convenience ctor with sensible defaults (10 s connect, 30 s read). */
    public SecureTcpClient(String host, int port) {
        this(host, port, 10_000, 30_000);
    }

    /** --------------------------------------------------------------
     *  PUBLIC API
     *  -------------------------------------------------------------- */

    /**
     * Establishes the TCP + TLS connection.
     *
     * @throws IOException on any network or TLS error.
     */
    public void connect() throws IOException {
        if (socket != null && socket.isConnected() && !socket.isClosed()) {
            Log.w(TAG, "Already connected – ignoring duplicate connect()");
            return;
        }

        // 1️⃣  Create a plain TCP socket with a timeout
        Socket rawSocket = new Socket();
        rawSocket.connect(new InetSocketAddress(host, port), connectTimeoutMs);
        rawSocket.setSoTimeout(readTimeoutMs);

        // 2️⃣  Upgrade to TLS using the default SSLContext (system trust store)
        try {
            SSLContext sslContext = SSLContext.getInstance("TLS");
            // The default TrustManagerFactory loads the Android system CA store.
            sslContext.init(null, null, new SecureRandom());
            SSLSocketFactory factory = sslContext.getSocketFactory();

            // Wrap the existing TCP socket – this avoids an extra TCP handshake.
            socket = (SSLSocket) factory.createSocket(rawSocket,
                    host,
                    port,
                    true); // autoClose = true → close rawSocket when SSLSocket is closed

            // Enable modern cipher suites & TLS versions only.
            enableSecureProtocolsAndCiphers(socket);

            // Perform the TLS handshake (this also validates the server cert + hostname)
            socket.startHandshake();

            // 3️⃣  Prepare buffered text I/O helpers
            reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream(), "UTF-8"));
            writer = new BufferedWriter(new OutputStreamWriter(socket.getOutputStream(), "UTF-8"));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Ensure raw socket is closed on failure to avoid resource leaks.
            rawSocket.close();
            throw new IOException("TLS init failed", e);
        }
    }

    /** Write raw bytes (no framing). */
    public void send(byte[] data) throws IOException {
        ensureConnected();
        writer.write(new String(data, "UTF-8"));
        writer.flush();
    }

    /** Write a line terminated with LF (convenient for many text protocols). */
    public void sendLine(String line) throws IOException {
        ensureConnected();
        writer.write(line);
        writer.write('\n');
        writer.flush();
    }

    /** Read a line terminated by LF (the newline is NOT returned). */
    public String readLine() throws IOException {
        ensureConnected();
        String line = reader.readLine(); // returns null on EOF
        if (line == null) {
            throw new EOFException("Remote closed the connection");
        }
        return line;
    }

    /** Read exactly {@code len} bytes (blocking). */
    public byte[] readExact(int len) throws IOException {
        ensureConnected();
        byte[] buf = new byte[len];
        int off = 0;
        while (off < len) {
            int read = socket.getInputStream().read(buf, off, len - off);
            if (read == -1) {
                throw new EOFException("Remote closed before delivering all bytes");
            }
            off += read;
        }
        return buf;
    }

    /** Close the socket and all streams. Idempotent. */
    public void close() {
        try {
            if (writer != null) writer.close();
        } catch (IOException ignored) {}
        try {
            if (reader != null) reader.close();
        } catch (IOException ignored) {}
        try {
            if (socket != null) socket.close();
        } catch (IOException ignored) {}
        socket = null;
        writer = null;
        reader = null;
    }

    /** --------------------------------------------------------------
     *  INTERNAL HELPERS
     *  -------------------------------------------------------------- */

    private void ensureConnected() throws IOException {
        if (socket == null || !socket.isConnected() || socket.isClosed()) {
            throw new IOException("Not connected – call connect() first");
        }
    }

    /**
     * Enforces the use of only strong TLS versions (TLS 1.2/1.3) and
     * disables any legacy cipher suites that are considered weak.
     *
     * <p>Android 5.0+ supports TLS 1.2 by default; Android 10+ also has TLS 1.3.
     */
    private static void enableSecureProtocolsAndCiphers(SSLSocket sslSocket) {
        // 1️⃣ Preferred TLS protocols
        String[] enabledProtocols = filterArray(
                sslSocket.getSupportedProtocols(),
                protocol -> protocol.equals("TLSv1.3") || protocol.equals("TLSv1.2"));
        sslSocket.setEnabledProtocols(enabledProtocols);

        // 2️⃣ Preferred cipher suites – keep only those with > 128‑bit security
        String[] enabledCiphers = filterArray(
                sslSocket.getSupportedCipherSuites(),
                suite -> suite.matches(".*_(AES_256|CHACHA20)_.*") ||
                        suite.matches(".*_GCM_.*"));
        sslSocket.setEnabledCipherSuites(enabledCiphers);
    }

    private static String[] filterArray(String[] src, java.util.function.Predicate<String> predicate) {
        java.util.List<String> keep = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
        for (String s : src) {
            if (predicate.test(s)) keep.add(s);
        }
        return keep.toArray(new String[0]);
    }
}
```

### How the code satisfies **security‑by‑design** requirements:

| Concern | How it’s addressed |
|---------|-------------------|
| **Certificate validation** | Uses the default `TrustManagerFactory` → Android’s system trust store (or the app‑specific store if you provide a custom `KeyStore`). No `X509TrustManager` that blindly trusts everything. |
| **Hostname verification** | `SSLSocket.startHandshake()` internally validates that the certificate’s Subject‑Alt‑Names contain the hostname you passed to the constructor (`host`). |
| **TLS protocol version** | Forces TLS 1.2/1.3 only; disables SSL v2/v3 and TLS 1.0/1.1 which are deprecated. |
| **Cipher suite selection** | Only strong AEAD ciphers (AES‑GCM, AES‑256‑CBC, ChaCha20‑Poly1305) are enabled. |
| **Network‑security‑config** | Gives you an easy place to pin certificates (`pin-set`) or restrict clear‑text traffic. |
| **Threading / UI safety** | The socket I/O is **blocking**; the class must be used from a background thread (e.g., `ExecutorService`, `AsyncTask`, Kotlin `Dispatchers.IO`, or a Service). |
| **Resource cleanup** | `close()` is idempotent and closes streams/sockets in the correct order. |
| **Error handling** | All public methods throw `IOException` (or subclass) with a clear cause, making it trivial for the caller to differentiate network errors from TLS handshake failures. |

---

## 3. Typical Call‑Site (Background Thread)

