---

## 5. Extending the Shim

### 5.1 Adding More Built‑in Modules

Add more objects to the `require` dispatcher:

```kotlin
when (moduleName) {
    "math" -> MathModule
    "fs" -> FsModule   // simple file read/write shim (wrap java.io)
    "net" -> NetModule // basic HTTP client via java.net.HttpURLConnection
    else -> loadJsModule(Path.of(moduleName))
}
```

### 5.2 Limiting Java Access (Security)

If you **don’t want JS scripts to call arbitrary Java classes**, instantiate Nashorn with a **`ClassFilter`**:

```kotlin
val filter = jdk.nashorn.api.scripting.ClassFilter { className ->
    // Allow only whitelisted packages
    className.startsWith("java.lang.") ||
    className == "java.util.HashMap"
}
val engine = NashornScriptEngineFactory()
    .getScriptEngine(filter, "--no-java")
```

Replace the engine creation line in `NodeShim` with the above.

### 5.3 Full Node‑like REPL (Optional)

If you need a true REPL (interactive input), you can drive the Node process **bidirectionally**:

```kotlin
fun sendToNode(command: String) {
    nodeWriter?.apply {
        write("$command\n")
        flush()
    }
}
```

Then expose a `eval` function to JS:

```kotlin
val node = object {
    fun eval(code: String) = sendToNode(code)
}
globalBindings["node"] = node
```

From Minecraft you could call `node.eval("console.log('hello')")` and see the result instantly.

