## 1. Front‑end – Capturing the Canvas and Sending the Blob

### 1.1 JavaScript Code (modern, async/await)

```html
<canvas id="myCanvas" width="800" height="600"></canvas>
<button id="saveBtn">Save as PNG</button>

<script>
  const canvas = document.getElementById('myCanvas');
  const saveBtn = document.getElementById('saveBtn');

  // Example drawing – replace with your own logic
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
  ctx.fillStyle = '#2196F3';
  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
  ctx.fillStyle = '#fff';
  ctx.font = '48px sans-serif';
  ctx.fillText('Hello world', 50, 150);

  async function uploadCanvas() {
    // 1️⃣ Convert canvas → Blob
    const blob = await new Promise(resolve => canvas.toBlob(resolve, 'image/png'));

    // 2️⃣ Build FormData (multipart/form-data)
    const fd = new FormData();
    fd.append('image', blob, 'canvas.png'); // the filename is optional but nice

    // 3️⃣ POST to the Java endpoint
    const response = await fetch('/api/uploadCanvas', {
      method: 'POST',
      credentials: 'same-origin', // send CSRF token cookie if you have one
      body: fd,
    });

    if (!response.ok) {
      const err = await response.text();
      console.error('Upload failed:', err);
      return;
    }

    const json = await response.json(); // { id: "...", url: "/images/..." }
    console.log('Image stored, accessible at', json.url);
    // You can now set <img src="{json.url}"> or any other UI update.
  }

  saveBtn.addEventListener('click', uploadCanvas);
</script>
```

### 1.2 Security‑relevant Front‑end Practices  

| Issue | Mitigation |
|-------|------------|
| **CSRF** | Send a CSRF token (e.g., via a cookie + request header). Most frameworks (Spring Security) have built‑in CSRF protection. |
| **CSP / Referrer‑Policy** | Set `Content‑Security‑Policy: default-src 'self'` and `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin` to prevent data leakage. |
| **File‑size blow‑out** | Browsers limit canvas export size, but you may still enforce a client‑side size check before sending (`blob.size`). |
| **Mime spoofing** | The `toBlob` API forces a MIME type (`image/png`). Never let the client choose arbitrary types. |
| **XSS** | Never directly inject the server‑returned URL into HTML without sanitisation. Use DOM APIs (`img.src = url`). |

