## 3. Security Deep‑Dive  

Below is a **checklist** of the most common attack surfaces when handling canvas‑generated images.

| Attack Vector | Where it Happens | Mitigation |
|---------------|------------------|------------|
| **1️⃣ MIME‑type spoofing** | Client can claim any `Content-Type`. Browsers may also send the filename extension, which can be misleading. | **Never trust the client‑sent header**. Use a server‑side “sniffing” library (`Apache Tika`, `java.awt.image.ImageIO`, or `LibreOffice`). Only allow a *whitelist* of known image MIME types (`image/png`, `image/jpeg`, `image/webp`). |
| **2️⃣ Malicious image payloads** | Certain image parsers have historically had vulnerabilities (e.g., **CVE‑2022‑21534** – ImageMagick). | - Use **built‑in JDK `ImageIO`** (limited parser surface).<br>- Keep libraries up‑to‑date.<br>- Prefer **defensive decoding**: read the image and re‑encode it (`ImageIO.write`) to a known safe format before persisting.<br>- Optionally run **AV scanning** (ClamAV, Sophos, etc.) on the uploaded bytes. |
| **3️⃣ File‑size / DoS** | An attacker can send gigabytes of data causing memory or disk exhaustion. | - Enforce strict **max‑file-size** both at the HTTP layer (Tomcat/Jetty `maxPostSize`) and at the framework (`spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size`).<br>- Set **upload‑rate limits** (e.g., using a filter or CDN). |
| **4️⃣ Path Traversal** | `filename` may contain `../` to escape the storage directory. | - Generate **random UUID filenames** only on the server.<br>- Validate any filename received from a GET request against a strict regex (`^[0-9a-fA-F-]{36}\\.[a-z]{3,4}$`).<br>- Resolve `Path.normalize()` and verify `path.startsWith(storageRoot)`. |
| **5️⃣ XSS via data URLs** | If you embed the image directly using `src="data:image/png;base64,...."` the data is under your control and safe, but some browsers may allow embedded scripts in SVG. | - **Reject SVG** unless you explicitly support it and sanitize it (e.g., with `OWASP Java HTML Sanitizer`).<br>- Serve PNG/JPEG only. |
| **6️⃣ Content Sniffing** | Browsers might guess the content type and execute it if the header is missing or ambiguous. | - Always set `Content-Type` correctly.<br>- Add `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`. |
| **7️⃣ Cache Poisoning** | If you reuse URLs for different images (e.g., same UUID) the cache can serve the wrong resource. | - Use **immutable URLs** (UUID per upload).<br>- Set `Cache-Control: private, max-age=86400, immutable`. |
| **8️⃣ CSRF** | An attacker could trick an authenticated user into uploading a malicious canvas. | - Require a **CSRF token** (Spring Security does this automatically).<br>- Enforce **SameSite=Lax/Strict** on auth cookies. |
| **9️⃣ Open Redirect / Referrer leakage** | Sending the image URL back to the client in JSON can be used for open‑redirect attacks if you later accept arbitrary URLs. | - Do **not** reflect user‑controlled URLs without validation. The URL you return is always a *relative* path (`/images/<id>`). |
| **10️⃣ Information Disclosure** | Stack traces or error messages may leak server version or paths. | - Return **generic error messages** (`{ "error": "Invalid image" }`).<br>- Use a global exception handler that hides stack traces in production. |

---

## 4. Optional Enhancements  

| Feature | Why it helps | How to add |
|---------|--------------|------------|
| **Re‑encode to canonical PNG** | Normalises image, strips malicious metadata (EXIF, ICC profiles). | After `ImageIO.read`, call `ImageIO.write(bufferedImage, "png", out)`. |
| **Thumbnail generation** | Reduces bandwidth when you only need a preview; also validates image dimensions. | Use `Thumbnails.of(...).size(200, 200).toFile(thumbnailPath);` (Thumbnailator library). |
| **Signed URLs for download** | Prevents unauthorized users from guessing UUIDs. | Generate HMAC‑signed query parameter (`/images/{id}?sig=...`) and verify on GET. |
| **Rate‑limiting / CAPTCHA** | Thwarts automated bulk uploads. | Apply a `Bucket4j` filter or Cloudflare Turnstile on the upload endpoint. |
| **Audit logging** | Tracks who uploaded what and when for forensics. | Log `userId, ip, filename, size, mime`. Ensure logs are write‑only for the app. |
| **Secure storage backend** | If you store images on S3, enable bucket policies and encryption (SSE‑S3). | Use `AmazonS3ClientBuilder` with `SSEAwsKeyManagementParams`. |

