## 2. Service that hides the “old” implementation details  

A thin façade that glues together the two legacy parts (`AuthServiceImpl` and `NcsAuthenticationProvider`).  
All the heavy lifting (hashing, DB look‑ups, token caching…) stays exactly where it already lives – we just call it.

```java
package com.ncs.api.auth.service;

import com.ncs.api.auth.dto.*;
import com.ncs.company.security.NcsAuthenticationProvider;
import com.ncs.security.service.AuthServiceImpl;
import com.ncs.security.service.AuthResult;
import com.ncs.security.domain.Token;
import com.ncs.employee.domain.Employee;
import com.ncs.employee.service.EmployeeService;
import com.ncs.security.exception.NcsAuthenticationException;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

/**
 * Public façade used by the REST controller.
 */
@Service
public class FrontendAuthService {

    private final AuthServiceImpl authService;
    private final EmployeeService employeeService;        // you must have a way to fetch Employee by userId
    private final NcsAuthenticationProvider authProvider; // the original provider (or we copy its logic)

    public FrontendAuthService(AuthServiceImpl authService,
                               EmployeeService employeeService,
                               NcsAuthenticationProvider authProvider) {
        this.authService   = authService;
        this.employeeService = employeeService;
        this.authProvider  = authProvider;
    }

    /**
     * Execute the whole flow: basic auth → extra checks → final token + enriched info.
     *
     * @throws AuthFailureException   when one of the checks fails (messages are filled in)
     */
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public AuthSuccessResponse authenticate(String login,
                                            String password,
                                            String clientIp) throws AuthFailureException {

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1️⃣  BASIC AUTH (the original AuthServiceImpl.auth)
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        AuthResult authResult = authService.auth(login, password, clientIp);

        if (!authResult.isValid()) {
            // the internal service already prepared a nice error set – just forward it
            throw new AuthFailureException(
                    authResult.getMessage(),
                    authResult.getTitle(),
                    authResult.getHint(),
                    authResult.getResultType(),
                    authResult.isLockAccount()
            );
        }

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 2️⃣  Load the Employee (the “principal”) that belongs to this user
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        Employee employee = employeeService.findByUserId(authResult.getUserId())
                .orElseThrow(() -> new AuthFailureException(
                        "Системный сбой: не найден профиль сотрудника.",
                        "Ошибка аутентификации",
                        "Обратитесь в службу поддержки.",
                        "DANGER",
                        false
                ));

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3️⃣  Run the *additional* checks that are implemented in NcsAuthenticationProvider#getAuthToken
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        try {
            // the provider returns a Spring Security token that already contains the
            // UserInfo object inside .details.  We only need the details part.
            var springAuthToken = authProvider.getAuthToken(
                    employee.getEmail(),          // principal (any object – we reuse email)
                    null,                         // credentials – not needed any more
                    employee
            );

            // If we are here the provider did NOT throw – auth is successful.
            // Extract the UserInfo we prepared earlier.
            var userInfo = (com.ncs.company.security.UserInfo) springAuthToken.getDetails();

            // -----------------------------------------------------------------
            // 4️⃣  Build the final REST response
            // -----------------------------------------------------------------
            Token tokenEntity = authResult.getToken();   // already persisted by authService

            AuthSuccessResponse resp = new AuthSuccessResponse();
            resp.setToken(tokenEntity.getToken());
            resp.setTokenValidity(tokenEntity.getTokenValidity());
            resp.setUserId(tokenEntity.getUserId());

            // Map the internal UserInfo → DTO (you may use MapStruct / ModelMapper)
            UserInfoDto dto = mapUserInfo(userInfo);
            resp.setUserInfo(dto);

            return resp;

        } catch (NcsAuthenticationException ex) {
            // The provider threw – rewrap as a nice API‑error.
            throw new AuthFailureException(
                    ex.getMessage(),          // title
                    ex.getShortMessage(),    // short description
                    ex.getHint(),            // support hint
                    ex.getResultType().name(),
                    ex.isLockAccount()
            );
        }
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Helper – map the internal UserInfo to the public DTO.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    private UserInfoDto mapUserInfo(com.ncs.company.security.UserInfo info) {
        UserInfoDto dto = new UserInfoDto();
        dto.setId(info.getId());
        dto.setLogin(info.getLogin());
        dto.setPhone(info.getPhone());
        dto.setFirstName(info.getFirstName());
        dto.setLastName(info.getLastName());
        dto.setMiddleName(info.getMiddleName());
        dto.setCompanyCssDir(info.getCompanyCssDir());
        dto.setAccessType(info.getAccessType());

        // partners
        List<PartnerDto> partners = info.getPartners().stream().map(p -> {
            PartnerDto pd = new PartnerDto();
            pd.setId(p.getId());
            pd.setName(p.getName());
            return pd;
        }).toList();
        dto.setPartners(partners);
        if (!partners.isEmpty()) {
            dto.setPartner(partners.get(0));
        }
        return dto;
    }
}

/**
 * Custom runtime exception used to bubble up a structured error
 * to the controller/advice layer.
 */
class AuthFailureException extends RuntimeException {
    private final String title;
    private final String message;
    private final String hint;
    private final String type;      // WARNING, DANGER …
    private final boolean lockAccount;

    public AuthFailureException(String title, String message, String hint,
                                String type, boolean lockAccount) {
        super(message);
        this.title = title;
        this.message = message;
        this.hint = hint;
        this.type = type;
        this.lockAccount = lockAccount;
    }

    // getters for the fields
    public String getTitle() { return title; }
    public String getMessage() { return message; }
    public String getHint() { return hint; }
    public String getType() { return type; }
    public boolean isLockAccount() { return lockAccount; }
}
```

> **Why a façade?**  
> * Keeps the controller ultra‑thin (only HTTP concerns).  
> * Isolates the rest of the code base from any future refactorings of `AuthServiceImpl` or the provider.  
> * Allows unit‑testing the whole flow without starting a web container.

