## 2️⃣ Blockchain.java – the ledger that connects the blocks

```java
package com.example.blockchain;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Minimal blockchain implementation.
 *
 * SECURITY NOTES:
 *   • The internal list is never exposed directly; callers receive an
 *     *unmodifiable* copy when they need to inspect it.
 *   • Validation checks both hash integrity *and* proof‑of‑work difficulty.
 *   • No networking, no consensus (this is a single‑node demo).  In a real
 *     system you would need a peer‑to‑peer protocol, Byzantine‑fault‑tolerant
 *     consensus, signed transactions, etc.
 */
public class Blockchain {

    private final List<Block> chain = new ArrayList<>();
    private final int difficulty;      // constant difficulty for the demo

    /**
     * Initialise the blockchain with a genesis block.
     *
     * @param difficulty number of leading zero *bits* required for PoW
     */
    public Blockchain(int difficulty) {
        this.difficulty = difficulty;
        Block genesis = createGenesisBlock();
        genesis.mineBlock();         // mine the genesis block once
        chain.add(genesis);
    }

    /** Build the first block – see Bitcoin's "genesis block". */
    private Block createGenesisBlock() {
        return new Block(0, "Genesis Block", "0", difficulty);
    }

    /** Append a new block with the given payload to the chain. */
    public Block addBlock(String data) {
        Block previousBlock = getLatestBlock();
        Block newBlock = new Block(previousBlock.getIndex() + 1,
                                   data,
                                   previousBlock.getHash(),
                                   difficulty);
        newBlock.mineBlock();
        chain.add(newBlock);
        return newBlock;
    }

    /** Retrieve the most recent block (head of the chain). */
    public Block getLatestBlock() {
        return chain.get(chain.size() - 1);
    }

    /** Return a *read‑only* snapshot of the chain. */
    public List<Block> getChain() {
        return List.copyOf(chain);
    }

    /** Validate the entire chain – detect any tampering. */
    public boolean isChainValid() {
        for (int i = 1; i < chain.size(); i++) {
            Block current = chain.get(i);
            Block previous = chain.get(i - 1);

            // 1. Re‑compute the current hash and compare
            if (!current.getHash().equals(current.calculateHash())) {
                System.out.println("Invalid hash at block " + i);
                return false;
            }

            // 2. Ensure the stored previous‑hash actually matches the predecessor
            if (!current.getPreviousHash().equals(previous.getHash())) {
                System.out.println("Invalid previous‑hash link at block " + i);
                return false;
            }

            // 3. Verify proof‑of‑work difficulty
            String target = "0".repeat(difficulty);
            if (!current.getHash().substring(0, difficulty).equals(target)) {
                System.out.println("Insufficient PoW at block " + i);
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (Block b : chain) {
            sb.append(b).append("\n");
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

### Security‑focused sanity checks  

| Validation step | What it protects against |
|-----------------|--------------------------|
| **Re‑hash** (`calculateHash()`) | Detects any in‑memory mutation of a block after it was mined. |
| **Previous‑hash linkage** | Guarantees the chronological order – an attacker cannot splice an arbitrary block without breaking the hash chain. |
| **Difficulty check** | Prevents someone from inserting a pre‑computed block with a weak hash (i.e., bypassing PoW). |

