## 4️⃣ Security‑Research Discussion (beyond the code)

Below is a concise “research‑notes” checklist you can use when reviewing or extending this prototype.

| Area | Potential weakness in the prototype | Real‑world mitigations |
|------|--------------------------------------|------------------------|
| **Hash function** | SHA‑256 is *currently* safe, but any future weakness (e.g., quantum attacks) would jeopardise integrity. | Adopt post‑quantum hash functions (SHA‑3, BLAKE3) or hybrid constructions. |
| **Difficulty calibration** | Fixed difficulty leads to predictable block times; low difficulty makes PoW trivial. | Implement **dynamic difficulty retargeting** (e.g., every 2016 blocks) based on observed block time. |
| **Block size & transaction format** | No size limit; data can be arbitrarily large → DoS via large payloads. | Enforce a maximum block size, use Merkle trees for compact transaction commitment. |
| **No signatures** | Anyone can write arbitrary data → no authentication of transactions. | Integrate **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** signatures; every transaction carries a public‑key hash and signature. |
| **No consensus / network layer** | Single‑node chain – cannot detect fork attacks or double spends. | Build a peer‑to‑peer network, use a consensus algorithm (PoW, PoS, BFT) with block propagation and fork resolution. |
| **Replay attacks** | If two chains share the same genesis hash, an attacker could replay a block from a different fork. | Add a **chain identifier** (network ID) into the block header. |
| **Timestamp manipulation** | An attacker could forge future timestamps to accelerate difficulty adjustments. | Enforce **median past timestamp** rules (as Bitcoin does) and reject blocks far ahead of real time. |
| **Memory / state pruning** | The whole chain lives in RAM; unbounded growth leads to OOM. | Implement **pruning** (UTXO set only) or **light client** / SPV verification. |
| **Randomness source for nonce** | Linear search of nonces is deterministic; could be vulnerable to side‑channel timing attacks. | Use a cryptographically secure RNG for the starting nonce or adopt a more complex PoW (e.g., memory‑hard). |
| **Side‑channel leakage** | Mining loop may leak power consumption patterns revealing private keys (if used for signing). | Constant‑time implementations, hardware isolation, or use of dedicated ASICs. |

