| **4** | Logged the full exception server‑side only (commented sample logger line). | Ops teams can still investigate attacks while adversaries never see the details. |
| **5** | Set `server.error.whitelabel.enabled=false`. | Prevents the generic Spring error page (which can show the “There was an unexpected error (type=Internal Server Error, status=500)” message and even a stack trace when `debug=true`). |
| **6** | `server.error.include-stacktrace=never`. | Explicitly tells Spring not to embed the stack trace in *any* error JSON/HTML response, even if an `Accept: */*` request is made. |
| **7** | Returned a simple **static view** (`error.html`) with **no dynamic reflection of request parameters**. | Disallows reflected XSS via malicious query strings that could otherwise be echoed back in a templated error page. |
| **8** | Sanitised model attributes (`status`, `error`, `message`, `timestamp`). | Guarantees only safe strings are rendered. If you ever need to display user‑supplied data (e.g., a query that caused the error), make sure you **HTML‑escape** it (`th:text` automatically does that). |
| **9** | (Optional) Consider using a **custom `ErrorAttributes`** implementation to further filter data (e.g., suppress “path”, “error” details, or add a correlation ID for logs). | Enables a consistent “no‑leak” policy across all error responses. |
| **10** | Make sure the **error view files are not writable** by the web server user. | Prevents an attacker who gains limited file‑write access from injecting malicious HTML/JS into the error page. |

