Below is a **complete, step‑by‑step guide** for building a **pure‑static** (HTML + CSS + JS) site that can **load and render Markdown (MD) files** in the browser **safely**.  
The focus is on the **security researcher’s perspective** – what you need to do to keep the site from becoming an XSS vector, how to lock down the page with a Content‑Security‑Policy (CSP), and how to verify that the implementation is safe.

---

## 1. High‑level Architecture

| Component | Purpose | Security notes |
|-----------|---------|----------------|
| `index.html` | The only HTML page that the browser loads. | Must have a restrictive CSP, no inline `<script>` or `<style>` (unless hashed). |
| `style.css` | Presentation – nothing security‑related. | Serve with `Content-Type: text/css`. |
| `script.js` | Fetches a Markdown file, parses it, sanitises the HTML, then injects it into the DOM. | Use a well‑maintained Markdown → HTML library (e.g., **marked**), and a **HTML sanitizer** (e.g., **DOMPurify**). No `eval`, `new Function`, etc. |
| `markdown/` directory | Holds static `.md` files the site can render. | Files are static, but treat them as **untrusted input** – always sanitise after parsing. |
| Optional: `serve/` | A tiny static‑file server for local testing (e.g., `python -m http.server`). | No server‑side processing, so the attack surface is limited to the client. |

---

## 2. Directory Layout

```
static-md-site/
│
├─ index.html
├─ style.css
├─ script.js
│
├─ libs/
│   ├─ marked.min.js      (Markdown parser)
│   └─ dompurify.min.js   (HTML sanitizer)
│
└─ markdown/
    ├─ demo.md
    └─ another.md
```

All files are served **as‑is** – no backend logic, no templating engine.

---

## 3. Security‑First HTML Boilerplate (`index.html`)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Static Markdown Viewer</title>

  <!-- 1️⃣ Strict Content‑Security‑Policy -->
  <meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"
        content="
          default-src 'self';
          script-src 'self' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net;   /* mark external libs if you use CDN */
          style-src  'self' 'unsafe-inline';          /* allow inline styles only if you inline CSS */
          img-src    'self' data:;
          font-src   'self';
          object-src 'none';
          base-uri   'none';
          frame-ancestors 'none';
        ">

  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>

<body>
  <header>
    <h1>Markdown Viewer</h1>
    <nav>
      <label for="fileSelect">Choose a file:</label>
      <select id="fileSelect">
        <option value="markdown/demo.md">Demo</option>
        <option value="markdown/another.md">Another</option>
      </select>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main id="content">
    <!-- Rendered Markdown will go here -->
  </main>

  <!-- Load external libraries –‑ use Subresource Integrity (SRI) if you pull from a CDN -->
  <script src="libs/marked.min.js" integrity="sha384-<hash>" crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
  <script src="libs/dompurify.min.js" integrity="sha384-<hash>" crossorigin="anonymous"></script>

  <!-- Your own script –‑ no inline code, therefore CSP is satisfied -->
  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Why this CSP?

- **`default-src 'self'`** – only resources that live on the same origin are allowed.
- **`script-src 'self' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net`** – you can add the exact CDN you trust; if you host the libraries locally, only `'self'` is needed.
- **`style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'`** – we use a *single* external CSS file, but allow inline styles only if you truly need them (e.g., quick style resets). If you want *zero* inline, remove `'unsafe-inline'` and move everything to `style.css`.
- **`img-src 'self' data:`** – images can be loaded from the site or embedded data URIs (useful for SVGs that may be inside MD).
- **`object-src 'none'`** – disables plugins (Flash, Java, etc.).
- **`frame-ancestors 'none'`** – prevents click‑jacking.

If you ever serve the page over HTTPS, the CSP will be enforced by modern browsers. You can test it with the **Security** panel in Chrome/FireFox dev tools.

---

## 4. Minimal CSS (`style.css`)

```css
