## 1.  High‑level design

| Step | What happens | Why it solves the problem |
|------|--------------|---------------------------|
| **A. Create a single caret element** (`<span class="caret-block"></span>`) | The element lives **outside** the editable area and is absolutely positioned over the current text caret. | No need to insert/remove DOM nodes on every key press → no accumulation of timers, no stray nodes that can get stuck. |
| **B. CSS blink animation** (`animation: blink 1s steps(2, start) infinite`) | The visual block blinks on its own. | The animation runs continuously; we never have to start/stop a timeout for it. |
| **C. Re‑position on every caret change** (`selectionchange`, `input`, `keydown`, `mousedown`, `touchstart`, `resize`) | In each of those events we read the **caret rectangle** via `range.getBoundingClientRect()` and move the caret element with `style.left/top`. | Guarantees the block follows the real caret even when the user types extremely fast or resizes the window. |
| **D. Debounce heavy calculations** (`resize` → 50 ms debounce) | Window resize can fire dozens of times per second; we only recompute the caret position after the user stops moving the window for a short period. | Prevents needless layout thrashing and eliminates the “multiple‑timeouts” symptom. |
