**TL;DR** – The only parts of the parser that need a rewrite are the two helper‑regexes that use *look‑behinds*:

```js
Patterns.getBreakLineBeforeTag()
Patterns.getBreakLineAfterTag()
```

iOS < 15 (and the current WebKit version used by many Safari‑based WebViews) does **not** implement the `(?<= … )` construct, so the parser crashes with *“Invalid regular expression: lookbehind not supported”*.  

The fix is to replace the look‑behind patterns with **equivalent patterns that capture the surrounding tag** and then post‑process the match in the `replace()` callback.  
Below is a drop‑in replacement for the whole `Patterns` class together with the tiny adjustments you have to make in the `prepareContent()` method where those patterns are used.

---

## 1️⃣  Why the original patterns need a look‑behind  

| Original pattern | What it matches | Why look‑behind is used |
|------------------|----------------|------------------------|
| `(?<=\[\ /?(.*?)\])\n+(?=\[\/?(?:TAGLIST)\])` | *one or more* `\n` **between** two BB‑code tags | The `(?<= … )` makes sure the newline is preceded by a *closing* or *opening* tag, but the newline itself is the *only* part that is replaced. |
| `(?<=\[\ /?(?:TAGLIST)\])\n` | a newline **right after** a BB‑code tag | Same idea – we want to delete the newline, not the tag that precedes it. |

Both patterns return **only the newline characters**, so the later code can simply do:

```js
contents = contents.replace(afterTags, '');
contents = contents.replace(beforeTags, (match, tag) => …);
```

On iOS the look‑behind throws an exception, so we have to **capture the surrounding tag** and then keep it while discarding the newline.

---

## 2️⃣  New look‑behind‑free patterns

```js
class Patterns {
  /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   *  Helper: build a pipe‑separated list of the tags, longest first.
   * ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  static prepareTags(...tags) {
    return tags.sort((a, b) => b.length - a.length).join('|');
  }

  /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   *  Opening tag –   [tag]   or   [tag=someValue]
   * ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  static getOpeningTag(tagTypes, flags = '') {
    const tags = Patterns.prepareTags(...tagTypes);
    return new RegExp(`\\[(${tags})=?(.*?)\\]`, flags);
  }

  /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   *  Closing tag –   [/tag]
   * ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  static getClosingTag(tagTypes, flags = '') {
    const tags = Patterns.prepareTags(...tagTypes);
    return new RegExp(`\\[\\/(${tags})\\]`, flags);
  }

  /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   *  Full tag –   [tag] … [/tag]   (captures inner text)
   * ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  static getFullTag(tagTypes, flags = '') {
    const tags = Patterns.prepareTags(...tagTypes);
    return new RegExp(`^\\[(${tags})=?(.*?)\\](.*)\\[\\/\\1\\]$`, flags);
  }

  /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   *  **BREAK‑LINE‑BEFORE‑TAG** – match a *newline sequence* that sits
   *  between two tags. We capture the *preceding* tag (group 1) so that the
   *  callback can decide whether to keep or drop the newline.
   *
   *  Old (look‑behind)   :  (?<=\[\ /?(.*?)\])\n+(?=\[\/?(?:TAGLIST)\])
   *  New (capture)       :  \[\/?(.*?)\]\n+(?=\[\/?(?:TAGLIST)\])
   * ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  static getBreakLineBeforeTag(tagTypes, flags = '') {
    const tags = Patterns.prepareTags(...tagTypes);
    //               1️⃣  the tag that appears *before* the newline
    //                               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^  look‑ahead stays unchanged
    return new RegExp(`\\[\\/?(.*?)\\]\\n+(?=\\[\\/?(?:${tags})\\])`, flags);
  }

  /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   *  **BREAK‑LINE‑AFTER‑TAG** – match a newline that follows a tag.
   *  We capture the tag (group 1) and then replace the whole match with just
   *  the captured tag, effectively *deleting* the newline only.
   *
   *  Old (look‑behind) :  (?<=\[\ /?(?:TAGLIST)\])\n
   *  New (capture)     :  (\[\/?(?:TAGLIST)\])\n
   * ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  static getBreakLineAfterTag(tagTypes, flags = '') {
    const tags = Patterns.prepareTags(...tagTypes);
    //               1️⃣  the tag itself
    //                               \n  – the newline we want to strip
    return new RegExp(`(\\[\\/?(?:${tags})\\])\\n`, flags);
  }

  /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   *  Non‑parameterized opening tags – e.g. [b] [ul] …
   * ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  static getNotParameterizedOpeningTag(tagTypes, flags = '') {
    const tags = Patterns.prepareTags(...tagTypes);
    return new RegExp(`\\[(${tags})\\]`, flags);
  }
}
```

### What changed?

| Method | Old pattern | New pattern | How it’s used |
|--------|-------------|-------------|----------------|
| `getBreakLineBeforeTag` | `(?<=\[\ /?(.*?)\])\n+(?=\[\/?(?:TAGLIST)\])` | `\[\ /?(.*?)\]\n+(?=\[\/?(?:TAGLIST)\])` | Captures the *previous* tag in group 1; the newline is still the part that gets replaced. |
| `getBreakLineAfterTag` | `(?<=\[\ /?(?:TAGLIST)\])\n` | `(\[\ /?(?:TAGLIST)\])\n` | Captures the tag in group 1; the replace‑call keeps the tag and discards the newline. |

Both patterns **do not use any look‑behind** and therefore run on every iOS version.

