## 3️⃣  Small changes in `prepareContent()`

Only two lines have to be tweaked – the rest of the parser can stay exactly the same.

```js
prepareContent(contents = '') {
  if (!contents || typeof contents !== 'string') {
    return '';
  }

  const tags = [...this.tags.getBreakLineTags(), 'li'];

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  BREAK‑LINE‑AFTER‑TAG  –  remove the newline that follows a tag
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const afterTags = Patterns.getBreakLineAfterTag(tags, 'g');

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  BREAK‑LINE‑BEFORE‑TAG –  may keep the newline for certain tags
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const beforeTags = Patterns.getBreakLineBeforeTag(['ul', 'ol', 'li'], 'g');

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // The rest of the preprocessing (unchanged)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  contents = contents.replace(/\[ml\]/g, '');
  contents = contents.replace(/\[\/ml\]/g, '\n');

  contents = contents.replace(/\n\[\/(center|justify|right|code)\]/g, (match, tag) => `[/${tag}]\n`);

  contents = contents.replace(/\[\/quote\]/g, (match) => match + '\n');

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Remove the newline *after* a tag – keep the tag itself
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  //   The captured tag is `$1`; we replace the whole match with that tag.
  contents = contents.replace(afterTags, '$1');

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Remove the newline *before* a tag unless the preceding tag
  //     is a list‑type tag (ul / ol / li).  The preceding tag name is
  //     supplied as the first capture group.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  contents = contents.replace(beforeTags, (match, prevTag) => {
    // `tags` is the full list of tags that are allowed to keep the newline.
    // If the previous tag is one of them we *preserve* the newline;
    // otherwise we drop it.
    if (tags.includes(prevTag)) return match;            // keep the newline
    return match.replace(/\n/, '');                     // strip the newline
  });

  return contents;
}
```

### Why the replacement strings look different

* **After‑tag** – `'$1'` tells `replace()` to insert the captured tag (group 1) and discard the newline that followed it.
* **Before‑tag** – the callback receives the whole match (`[prevTag]\n`) plus the captured previous‑tag name (`prevTag`).  
  If `prevTag` belongs to the whitelist (`tags`) we keep the original match (newline stays).  
  Otherwise we strip the newline by `match.replace(/\n/, '')`.

Both callbacks run **exactly the same way** as the original look‑behind version, only they now operate on a pattern that works everywhere.

