## 2️⃣ JavaScript – `DisplayCountryData`

```javascript
/**
 * Securely fetches and displays country information.
 * Called, for example, on the <select> change event.
 */
async function DisplayCountryData() {
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1. Grab the selected country name (user‑controlled input)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    const countrySelect = document.getElementById("countrySelect");
    const countryName = countrySelect.value?.trim();

    // Bail out early if nothing is selected – no network request needed.
    if (!countryName) {
        clearOutput();
        return;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2. Build a safe request URL (encode the value to avoid injection)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    const baseUrl = "https://restcountries.com/v3.1/name/";
    const requestUrl = `${baseUrl}${encodeURIComponent(countryName)}?fullText=true`;

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3. Perform the fetch with a timeout (prevents hanging requests)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    const controller = new AbortController();
    const timeoutMs = 8000; // 8‑second ceiling
    const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), timeoutMs);

    try {
        const resp = await fetch(requestUrl, { signal: controller.signal });

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 4. Verify we got a 2xx response – otherwise treat as error
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        if (!resp.ok) {
            throw new Error(`API returned ${resp.status}`);
        }

        const data = await resp.json();

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 5. Sanity‑check the shape of the response (defence‑in‑depth)
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // The API returns an array; we need the first element.
        const country = Array.isArray(data) && data[0];
        if (!country || typeof country !== "object") {
            throw new Error("Unexpected API payload");
        }

        // Extract fields we care about – guard against missing properties.
        const officialName = country.name?.official ?? "N/A";
        const population = typeof country.population === "number"
            ? country.population.toLocaleString()
            : "N/A";
        const area = typeof country.area === "number"
            ? `${country.area.toLocaleString()} km²`
            : "N/A";

        // Compute density only if we have both numbers (avoid division by zero).
        const density = (typeof country.population === "number" && typeof country.area === "number" && country.area > 0)
            ? (country.population / country.area).toFixed(2)
            : "N/A";

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 6. Render the data – using textContent to avoid XSS vectors
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        document.getElementById("country-name").textContent = officialName;
        document.getElementById("country-population").textContent = `Population: ${population}`;
        document.getElementById("country-area").textContent = `Area: ${area}`;
        document.getElementById("country-density").textContent = `Density: ${density} per km²`;

        // Optionally, you can put a JSON snapshot into #country-details for debugging.
        document.getElementById("country-details").textContent = JSON.stringify({
            name: officialName,
            population,
            area,
            density
        }, null, 2);
    } catch (err) {
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 7. Graceful error handling – never expose raw exceptions to the UI.
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        console.error("DisplayCountryData error:", err);
        clearOutput();
        document.getElementById("country-details").textContent = `❌ Could not load data for “${countryName}”.`;
    } finally {
        clearTimeout(timeoutId);
    }
}

/**
 * Clears all output fields (used when the dropdown is empty or on error).
 */
function clearOutput() {
    document.getElementById("country-name").textContent = "";
    document.getElementById("country-population").textContent = "";
    document.getElementById("country-area").textContent = "";
    document.getElementById("country-density").textContent = "";
    document.getElementById("country-details").textContent = "";
}

/**
 * Bind the function to the <select> element – you can also call it elsewhere.
 */
document.getElementById("countrySelect").addEventListener("change", DisplayCountryData);
```

---

## 3️⃣ Why This Is Secure (Security‑Researcher Perspective)

| Threat Vector | Mitigation in the Code |
|---------------|------------------------|
| **Cross‑Site Scripting (XSS)** | All DOM updates use `.textContent` (never `.innerHTML`). Even if the API returned malicious markup, it would be rendered as plain text. |
| **Injection via URL** | `encodeURIComponent()` is applied to the user‑controlled country name before it is appended to the request URL, neutralising characters like `?`, `&`, `/`, etc. |
| **Untrusted JSON** | The response is validated (`Array.isArray`, property existence checks) before any data is accessed. Unexpected shapes trigger an error instead of silent misuse. |
| **Denial‑of‑Service (DoS) via hanging request** | An `AbortController` with a strict timeout (8 s) guarantees the script won’t wait indefinitely for a dead endpoint. |
| **Information Leakage** | Errors are logged to the console (developer‑only) but never revealed to the end‑user; the UI only shows a generic “could not load data” message. |
| **Open Redirect / Phishing** | No redirection is performed. All network traffic stays within the same origin (the API endpoint is explicitly whitelisted). |
| **Excessive Privilege** | The script only requests the needed endpoint (`/name/...`) and does not request authentication tokens or user data. |
| **Rate‑Limiting / Abuse** | Though not enforced client‑side, the public API already imposes rate limits. If you host your own proxy, add a server‑side rate limiter. |

---

## 4️⃣ Quick Test

Paste the **HTML** and **JavaScript** snippets into a fresh `index.html` file and open it in a modern browser. Selecting a country should instantly fill the five placeholders with clean, readable data. Open the browser console → you’ll see any network or parsing errors logged (useful for a security audit).

