## TL;DR  
**Goal:** Turn a string that may contain `<b>`, `<i>` and `<u>` (or any combination of them) into **plain‑text** that *looks* the same – i.e. the visual styles must be kept, but the actual HTML tags must disappear.  

**Solution outline**

| Step | What we do | Why it’s safe |
|------|------------|---------------|
| 1️⃣ | **Parse** the string with the browser’s own HTML parser (DOMParser or a temporary `<div>`). | No regex on raw HTML → we let the native parser handle malformed markup. |
| 2️⃣ | **Walk** the resulting DOM tree recursively. | Guarantees we catch nested/overlapping tags. |
| 3️⃣ | For each node, **collect** the effective formatting flags (`bold`, `italic`, `underline`). | Normalises all possible combinations into a simple flag set. |
| 4️⃣ | **Emit** a new `<span>` element that has only inline CSS (`font‑weight`, `font‑style`, `text‑decoration`). | The visual result is identical, but the output contains **no** `<b>/<i>/<u>` tags. |
| 5️⃣ | **Sanitise** the final HTML with a trusted library (e.g. DOMPurify) before inserting it into the page. | Prevents any stray script or malicious attribute that may have slipped through. |

Below you’ll find a **complete, self‑contained function** that implements the above, plus a brief security commentary.

---

## 1️⃣  The “format‑preserving strip” function  

```js
/**
 * Convert a fragment that may contain <b>, <i>, <u> (nested or combined)
 * into a clean HTML string that uses only <span> with inline CSS.
 *
 * @param {string} rawHtml – the untrusted HTML fragment.
 * @returns {string} – a sanitized HTML string with no <b>/<i>/<u> tags.
 *
 * @example
 *   const src = '<b>Hello <i>World</i>!</b> <u>Underlined</u>';
 *   const cleaned = stripAndStyle(rawHtml);
 *   // cleaned === '<span style="font-weight:bold;">Hello <span style="font-weight:bold;font-style:italic;">World</span>!</span> <span style="text-decoration:underline;">Underlined</span>'
 */
function stripAndStyle(rawHtml) {
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 0️⃣  Defensive: if the input is empty/falsey just return "".
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  if (!rawHtml) return '';

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Parse the string into a genuine DOM tree.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const parser = new DOMParser();
  // We wrap the fragment in a <template> so the parser never treats it as
  // a full HTML document (which would add <html>/<head>/<body> etc.).
  const doc = parser.parseFromString(`<template>${rawHtml}</template>`, 'text/html');
  const fragment = doc.querySelector('template').content;

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Recursive walker that builds a new DocumentFragment.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const outFragment = document.createDocumentFragment();

  /**
   * Walk a node and copy its content while accumulating formatting flags.
   *
   * @param {Node} node – original node from the parsed tree.
   * @param {Object} fmt – { bold: boolean, italic: boolean, underline: boolean }.
   */
  function walk(node, fmt) {
    // Clone the formatting object so each branch can diverge independently.
    const curFmt = { ...fmt };

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2a️⃣  If we hit an element node, update the flags according to its tag.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    if (node.nodeType === Node.ELEMENT_NODE) {
      const tag = node.tagName.toUpperCase();

      // NOTE: We deliberately ignore *any* other tags – they will be
      // rendered as‑is (but later sanitized). This reduces surprise.
      if (tag === 'B') curFmt.bold = true;
      else if (tag === 'I') curFmt.italic = true;
      else if (tag === 'U') curFmt.underline = true;
      else if (tag === 'STRONG') curFmt.bold = true; // alias
      else if (tag === 'EM') curFmt.italic = true;   // alias
      // For any other element we simply recurse without changing flags.
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2b️⃣  Text nodes → create a <span> with the accumulated CSS.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    if (node.nodeType === Node.TEXT_NODE) {
      // Skip whitespace‑only nodes if you don’t want them, e.g.
      // if (!node.textContent.trim()) return;
      const span = document.createElement('span');

      // Build the inline‑style string only for flags that are true.
      const styleBits = [];
      if (curFmt.bold) styleBits.push('font-weight: bold;');
      if (curFmt.italic) styleBits.push('font-style: italic;');
      if (curFmt.underline) styleBits.push('text-decoration: underline;');

      if (styleBits.length) span.setAttribute('style', styleBits.join(' '));
      span.textContent = node.textContent; // plain‑text, no HTML injection
      outFragment.appendChild(span);
      return;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2c️⃣  Element nodes (including nested formatting tags) → recurse.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    if (node.nodeType === Node.ELEMENT_NODE) {
      // Process its children with the (potentially) updated formatting flags.
      node.childNodes.forEach(child => walk(child, curFmt));
    }
    // Other node types (comment, CDATA, etc.) are ignored on purpose.
  }

  // Kick‑off the walk from the top of the fragment, with an empty formatting set.
  walk(fragment, { bold: false, italic: false, underline: false });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Serialize the new fragment back to a string.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const container = document.createElement('div');
  container.appendChild(outFragment);
  let dirtyHtml = container.innerHTML; // still may contain other tags from src

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  **Security step:** sanitise everything that is *not* our <span>.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // Use a well‑maintained library – DOMPurify is the de‑facto standard.
  // It strips scripts, event‑handler attributes, iframes, etc.
  // If you cannot import a library, a very restrictive whitelist is the
  // minimum safe approach.
  //
  // Example with vanilla DOMPurify (you need to include it in your page):
  //   const cleanHtml = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirtyHtml, {ALLOWED_TAGS: ['span']});
  //
  // For completeness we include a tiny fallback that only *keeps* <span>.
  // **Do not rely on this fallback for production** – a proper sanitizer is
  // mandatory whenever you accept untrusted content.
  function tinySanitize(html) {
    const temp = document.createElement('template');
    temp.innerHTML = html;
    // Keep only <span> elements, everything else is stripped (including attributes)
    const allowed = Array.from(temp.content.querySelectorAll('span')).map(s => s.outerHTML).join('');
    return allowed;
  }

  // Choose your sanitiser:
  const cleanHtml = (typeof DOMPurify !== 'undefined')
    ? DOMPurify.sanitize(dirtyHtml, { ALLOWED_TAGS: ['span'] })
    : tinySanitize(dirtyHtml);

  return cleanHtml;
}
```

### How it works – step‑by‑step walkthrough

| Phase | Input | What the code does | Output |
|-------|-------|--------------------|--------|
| **Parsing** | `"<b>Hello <i>World</i>!</b>"` | A `DOMParser` creates a real DOM tree – the browser already normalises broken tags, fixes missing closing tags, etc. | `<template>` → child nodes: `<b>`, text, `<i>`, … |
| **Walking** | The root fragment plus an empty flag object `{bold:false, italic:false, underline:false}` | The recursive `walk()` function visits each node: <ul><li>When it meets `<b>`, it flips `bold = true`.</li><li>When it meets `<i>`, it flips `italic = true` (while preserving any `bold` already set).</li><li>When it reaches a text node, it creates a `<span>` with the **combined** inline CSS.</li></ul> | `<span style="font-weight:bold;">Hello <span style="font-weight:bold;font-style:italic;">World</span>!</span>` |
| **Serialising** | The newly built `DocumentFragment` | We drop it into a temporary `<div>` and read `innerHTML`. | Same string as above, but now it could contain *other* tags we didn’t explicitly handle (e.g. `<a>`, `<img>`). |
| **Sanitising** | Possibly‑tainted HTML string | `DOMPurify` (or a very strict fallback) removes anything that isn’t an allowed `<span>` and strips all attributes (including `onmouseover`, `href="javascript:"`, etc.). | Fully safe HTML that can be inserted with `innerHTML`. |

> **Why we **don’t** use a regular expression**  
> HTML is *not* a regular language. A regex will inevitably fail on nested tags, malformed markup, comments, CDATA, and entity‑encoded brackets. By delegating parsing to the browser (or to a library like `htmlparser2`) we guarantee correct handling of all edge‑cases.

