## 2️⃣  Security considerations – a quick “researcher’s checklist”

| Issue | Why it matters | How we mitigate it |
|-------|----------------|--------------------|
| **Cross‑Site Scripting (XSS)** | Untrusted fragments can embed `<script>`, `<img onerror=…>`, `javascript:` URLs, etc. | **Never** inject raw HTML. Use a *trustworthy* sanitizer (`DOMPurify`, `sanitize-html`, etc.) **after** we have transformed the markup. |
| **Event‑handler attributes (`onclick`, `onload`, …)** | Even a harmless `<span>` can become a vector if it keeps an attribute like `onclick="stealCookies()"`. | The sanitizer must **strip all attributes** (unless you explicitly whitelist safe ones). In the code above we only keep `<span>` without *any* attribute. |
| **CSS‑based attacks** – e.g. `style="position:absolute; left:-9999px;"` or `@import url(javascript:…)` | Inline styles can be abused for click‑jacking or leaky data‑exfiltration. | By constructing the `style` attribute **programmatically** (only the three allowed rules), we guarantee no extra CSS can slip in. |
| **HTML Entities & Unicode tricks** | Encoded `<` (`&lt;`) or weird Unicode bidi characters can bypass naïve filtering. | The **parser** automatically resolves entities to real characters before we walk the tree, so they never appear as literals in the output. |
| **Denial‑of‑Service (DoS) via huge fragments** | An attacker could feed a megabyte‑size string with deeply nested tags, causing the browser to choke. | If you expect huge inputs, **limit the size** (e.g. `if (rawHtml.length > 5e5) throw …`) **and** set a recursion depth guard inside `walk()`. |
| **Open redirect or data‑leak via `<a href="data:…">`** | Even if we strip the tag, the URL might be stored elsewhere (e.g. logs). | Because the final output contains **only `<span>`**, no hyperlinks survive. If you *do* need to keep links, whitelist only `http(s)://` URLs and sanitise them. |

> **Bottom line:** *Parsing* + *controlled re‑serialization* + a *battle‑tested sanitizer* = a robust pipeline against XSS and related vector attacks.

