## 9. Security hardening checklist (what a security researcher would look at)

| Area | Why it matters | What the code does (or should do) |
|------|----------------|-----------------------------------|
| **Input Sanitisation** | Prevent XSS when inserting data into the DOM. | All user‑supplied text (search term, reservation form values) is either **HTML‑escaped** (`escHTML`) *or* inserted into attributes that enforce proper typing (`type="email"` etc.). |
| **Content‑Security‑Policy (CSP)** | Mitigates XSS and data‑exfiltration. | Add a CSP header (or meta tag) on each page, e.g.: <br>`<meta http‑equiv="Content‑Security‑Policy" content="default-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: https:; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; frame-src https://www.youtube.com;">` |
| **Same‑Origin Policy** | Guarantees that the static site can only talk to its own origin. | All resources (`fetch` for `equipment.json`) are loaded with a relative URL, so they stay on‑origin. |
| **LocalStorage protection** | Users could manipulate stored reservations. | The reservation data is **non‑critical** – it only lives on the client. If you ever need a real back‑end, replace the `localStorage` logic with a proper API and verify data server‑side. |
| **HTTPS** | Prevent man‑in‑the‑middle tampering. | Deploy the site on HTTPS (GitHub Pages, Netlify, etc. provide it automatically). |
| **Rate‑limiting / spam protection** | Users could spam the reservation form. | Because the form is client‑only there is no server load. If you add a backend later, add CAPTCHA or rate‑limit. |
| **Headers** | Defense‑in‑depth (X‑Content‑Type‑Options, X‑Frame‑Options). | Configure the web server to send `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN`, `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin`. |
| **Accessibility** | Users with assistive tech need clear semantics. | Semantic HTML (`<article>`, `<section>`, proper `aria-label`s) and live region for reservation messages. |
| **Responsive design** | Mobile users must have a good experience. | CSS uses fluid grids &‑media queries; images are set to `max-width:100%`. |

