## 4. Full, battle‑tested snippet (userscript style)

```js
// ==UserScript==
// @name        WS Interceptor (generic)
// @namespace   http://example.com
// @version     1.0
// @description Intercept all WebSocket traffic on the page
// @match       *://*/*
// @run-at      document-start
// ==/UserScript==

(() => {
  // 1️⃣ Save the native class before we clobber it
  const NativeWS = window.WebSocket;

  // 2️⃣ Build a subclass with hooks
  class InterceptedWS extends NativeWS {
    constructor(url, protocols) {
      super(url, protocols);
      // <-- OUTGOING
      const origSend = this.send;
      this.send = data => {
        console.log('[WS ⬆]', url, data);
        // Example: suppress a secret token
        if (typeof data === 'string') {
          data = data.replace(/mySecretToken=\w+/g, 'mySecretToken=[redacted]');
        }
        return origSend.call(this, data);
      };
      // <-- INCOMING
      this.addEventListener('message', ev => {
        console.log('[WS ⬇]', url, ev.data);
        // If you need to rewrite inbound data:
        // const newData = ev.data.replace(/foo/g, 'bar');
        // this.dispatchEvent(new MessageEvent('message', { data: newData }));
      });
    }
  }

  // 3️⃣ Preserve static constants (CLOSING, OPEN, etc.)
  Object.assign(InterceptedWS, NativeWS);

  // 4️⃣ Replace the global constructor
  window.WebSocket = InterceptedWS;

  // 5️⃣ OPTIONAL: patch any *already‑created* sockets you can find
  // (e.g., if a framework stored them on `window` before us)
  // Example for a hypothetical global `app.ws`:
  if (window.app && window.app.ws instanceof NativeWS) {
    console.log('[WS] Patching pre‑existing socket');
    // reuse the same logic as above
    const origSend = window.app.ws.send;
    window.app.ws.send = function (data) {
      console.log('[WS ⬆ (pre‑existing)]', data);
      return origSend.call(this, data);
    };
    window.app.ws.addEventListener('message', ev => {
      console.log('[WS ⬇ (pre‑existing)]', ev.data);
    });
  }
})();
```

*Save this as a **Chrome/Firefox userscript** (e.g., Tampermonkey) or inject it via a browser extension.*  
Because it runs at `document-start`, the subclass is installed **before** any page script does `new WebSocket()`, guaranteeing full coverage.

---

## 5. Debugging checklist

| Step | What to verify |
|------|----------------|
| **Script runs early** | Open DevTools → Sources → check that the script appears before any network request to `wss://…`. |
| **`window.WebSocket` points to your subclass** | In console: `window.WebSocket.name` should be `"InterceptedWS"` (or whatever you called it). |
| **Outgoing logs appear** | Trigger an action that you know sends a WS message. You should see `[WS ⬆]` in the console. |
| **Incoming logs appear** | Wait for a server‑push or send a ping; you should see `[WS ⬇]`. |
| **Binary payloads** | If the app uses `ArrayBuffer`, add a check: `if (data instanceof ArrayBuffer) console.log('[binary]', new Uint8Array(data));` |
| **CSP errors** | Look for `Refused to evaluate a string as JavaScript because 'unsafe-eval'` or “script-src … blocked”. If you hit a CSP, you need an extension‑level injection (content script) rather than a userscript. |
| **Multiple realms (iframes)** | Each iframe gets its own `window.WebSocket`. If the target lives inside an iframe, you must inject the same patch into that frame (e.g., `document.querySelector('iframe').contentWindow`). |

---

## 6. Security‑research “what‑if” scenarios

| Scenario | How to handle it |
|----------|------------------|
| **TLS termination on the server** – you can’t see the plaintext at the browser level without the JS hook. | This exact interceptor gives you the plaintext *inside* the browser before it gets encrypted. |
| **WebSocket with per‑message encryption (application‑level)** – the payload is already ciphertext when `send` is called. | You’d need to hook **before** the app encrypts (i.e., find the API that builds the plaintext), or reverse‑engineer the encryption routine. |
| **The page uses a custom WebSocket wrapper (e.g., `socket.io-client`)** – it may store the native WS object in a private closure. | Patch the **global constructor** *first*, then the wrapper will pick up your subclass automatically. If the wrapper caches the native constructor early, you may need to replace the wrapper’s own reference (`socket.io-client` typically does `var WS = window.WebSocket;`). |
| **WebSocket is created in a Web Worker** – workers have their own `self.WebSocket`. | Inject the same patch *inside* the worker script (e.g., via `blob:` URL injection, or intercept `Worker` constructor and rewrite the script source). |
| **The page uses `new WebSocket(url, protocols)` with a **`String`** `protocols` argument** – you must preserve that overload. | The subclass constructor signature (`constructor(url, protocols)`) already forwards both args to `super`. No extra work needed. |
| **Browser extensions with “webRequest” API** – you might wonder if you can do the same server‑side. | `webRequest` only sees the *network* traffic (still encrypted). The only way to see the clear text is *client‑side* JS hooking (the technique above). |

---

## 7. Take‑away checklist for a reliable interceptor

1. **Run as early as possible** (document‑start or before any script that creates a socket).  
2. **Replace `window.WebSocket` with a subclass or factory** that forwards everything except the hooks you care about.  
3. **Preserve static properties** (`CONNECTING`, `OPEN`, etc.) so other code doesn’t break.  
4. **Handle binary data** – don’t assume `string`.  
5. **For already‑opened sockets**, manually patch each known instance (if you can reach them).  
6. **Test in multiple browsers** – subclassing works in Chrome/Edge/Firefox (≥53). Legacy IE needs a different approach (e.g., using the `proxy` pattern).  
7. **Watch for CSP** – if you can’t inject script, shift to a browser‑extension content script (runs in isolated world but can still override `window.WebSocket`).  

With those steps you’ll get a solid, reusable interceptor that works for *any* WebSocket traffic the page generates, and you’ll have a clear path for handling the edge cases that appear when you’re digging into real‑world web applications. Happy hunting!

