## TL;DR  
Yes – you can change the visible “button” (the colored dot) of an HTML `<input type="radio">` in pure CSS.  

There are **three practical approaches** today:

| Method | Browser support | How it works | When to use it |
|--------|----------------|--------------|----------------|
| **`accent-color` CSS property** | Chrome 93+, Edge 93+, Firefox 92+, Safari 15.4+ | Lets the UA paint native form controls (radio, checkbox, range, progress, etc.) with a custom accent colour. | Quick, no extra markup, works for simple colour tweaks. |
| **Hide the native control + custom markup** (`appearance:none` / `opacity:0` + fake UI via `::before/::after`) | All modern browsers | You replace the native widget with a CSS‑only “fake” that you fully control. | Full visual redesign, complex shapes, animated states, theming. |
| **Vendor‑specific pseudo‑elements** (`::-webkit-radio`, `::-ms-check`) | Chrome/Safari (WebKit) / Edge (Blink) | Provides a thin hook into the native widget; you can only tweak limited parts (border, background). | Legacy hacks when you only need modest changes and can accept limited coverage. |

Below you’ll find a **complete, accessible example** covering the first two approaches, along with a short note on the third (vendor pseudo‑elements).  

---  

## 1️⃣ The modern, easiest way – `accent-color`

```html
<form>
  <label>
    <input type="radio" name="color" value="red"   style="accent-color:#e53935">
    Red
  </label>
  <label>
    <input type="radio" name="color" value="green" style="accent-color:#43a047">
    Green
  </label>
  <label>
    <input type="radio" name="color" value="blue"  style="accent-color:#1e88e5">
    Blue
  </label>
</form>
```

*Why it works* – The UA draws the control using the user‑agent default accent colour (normally the OS/desktop theme). The CSS `accent-color` property tells the UA to paint the control with the supplied colour.

**Pros**

* Zero extra markup.
* Keeps native focus/keyboard behaviour, ARIA semantics, and hit‑testing.
* Works with dark‑mode / high‑contrast media queries automatically (the UA still handles contrast checks).

**Cons**

* You can only change the **overall accent** (the outer ring and the inner dot will share it).  
* No control over the *shape* or *size* – you’re limited to the native widget.

**Browser support matrix** (as of early 2024)

| Chrome | Edge | Firefox | Safari | Android WebView | iOS Safari |
|--------|------|---------|--------|-----------------|------------|
| 93+    | 93+  | 92+     | 15.4+  | 93+             | 15.4+      |

If you need to support browsers **older than those versions**, fall back to the custom‑control method (next section).

---

## 2️⃣ Full‑control custom radio – hide native, style a pseudo‑element

### HTML (semantic + accessible)

```html
<div class="radio-group">
  <label class="custom-radio">
    <input type="radio" name="fruit" value="apple">
    <span class="radio-indicator"></span>
    Apple
  </label>

  <label class="custom-radio">
    <input type="radio" name="fruit" value="banana">
    <span class="radio-indicator"></span>
    Banana
  </label>

  <label class="custom-radio">
    <input type="radio" name="fruit" value="cherry">
    <span class="radio-indicator"></span>
    Cherry
  </label>
</div>
```

*Why we use a `<span>`:*  
The `<span class="radio-indicator">` becomes the visual “button”. The real `<input>` stays in the DOM (screen‑readers, form submission, keyboard navigation) but is visually hidden.

### CSS – the core of the trick

```css
/* 1️⃣ Visually hide the native radio but keep it accessible */
.custom-radio input[type="radio"] {
  position: absolute;
  opacity: 0;
  width: 0;
  height: 0;
  margin: 0;
}

/* 2️⃣ Layout the label */
.custom-radio {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  cursor: pointer;
  margin-right: 1.5rem;               /* spacing between options */
  user-select: none;                  /* avoid accidental text selection */
}

/* 3️⃣ The fake radio “button” */
.radio-indicator {
  width: 1.2rem;
  height: 1.2rem;
  border: 2px solid #777;            /* default outline colour */
  border-radius: 50%;                /* circle */
  display: inline-block;
  position: relative;
  margin-right: 0.5rem;
  transition: border-color .18s ease, background .18s ease;
}

/* 4️⃣ Inner dot – hidden by default */
.radio-indicator::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 4px;                         /* 4px = (size - inner‑dot‑size)/2 */
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: #fff;                  /* will be overridden when checked */
  transform: scale(0);
  transition: transform .12s ease;
}

/* 5️⃣ When the hidden input is checked, style the fake button */
.custom-radio input[type="radio"]:checked + .radio-indicator {
  border-color: #0069d9;              /* chosen accent when active */
  background: #0069d9;
}

/* 6️⃣ Reveal the inner dot */
.custom-radio input[type="radio"]:checked + .radio-indicator::after {
  background: #fff;                  /* colour of the dot */
  transform: scale(1);
}

/* 7️⃣ Focus outline – very important for keyboard users */
.custom-radio input[type="radio"]:focus + .radio-indicator {
  outline: 2px solid #ffab00;        /* an accent that meets WCAG contrast */
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* 8️⃣ Optional hover effect (non‑essential but nice) */
.custom-radio:hover .radio-indicator {
  border-color: #555;
}
```

**Explanation of the steps**

| Step | What it does |
|------|--------------|
| **1** | Makes the real `<input>` invisible but still focusable (`opacity:0`, not `display:none`). |
| **2** | Turns the `<label>` into a flex container so the custom circle and text line up. |
| **3** | Draws an empty circle that mimics the native radio’s outer ring. |
| **4** | Adds a pseudo‑element (`::after`) that will become the white inner “dot”. It starts scaled to `0`. |
| **5‑6** | When the hidden input is `:checked`, change the colour of the outer circle (`border-color`, `background`) and scale the inner dot to `1` (visible). |
| **7** | Shows a focus ring – **mandatory** for accessibility. |
| **8** | Minor hover styling (optional). |

### Customising colours

Just replace the two colour values used in Steps 5‑6:

```css
/* Example: a “danger” red theme */
.custom-radio input[type="radio"]:checked + .radio-indicator {
  border-color: #d32f2f;   /* active border */
  background: #d32f2f;     /* active fill */
}

/* Example: a “success” green theme */
.custom-radio input[type="radio"]:checked + .radio-indicator {
  border-color: #388e3c;
  background: #388e3c;
}
```

If you need **multiple themes** in the same form, you can scope them with a parent class:

```html
<div class="theme-red"> … </div>
<div class="theme-green"> … </div>
```

```css
.theme-red .custom-radio input[type="radio"]:checked + .radio-indicator {
  border-color:#d32f2f; background:#d32f2f;
}
.theme-green .custom-radio input[type="radio"]:checked + .radio-indicator {
  border-color:#388e3c; background:#388e3c;
}
```

### Accessibility checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| **Keyboard navigation** – The hidden `<input>` receives focus via `Tab`/`Shift+Tab` and toggles with `Space`. |
| **Screen‑reader label** – The `<label>` wraps the `<input>`, so the accessible name is automatically the label text. |
| **Focus indicator** – The `outline` in step 7 ensures vision‑impaired users can see the focus. |
| **Contrast** – Ensure `border-color` + `background` meet WCAG 2.1 AA (≥ 4.5:1) against the page background. |
| **Touch targets** – Keep the clickable area at least 44 × 44 px (use padding on the label if needed). |
| **Form submission** – The hidden `<input>` still carries its `name`/`value` pair, so the server receives the right data. |

---

## 3️⃣ Vendor‑specific pseudo‑elements (for legacy “quick‑and‑dirty” tweaks)

```css
/* Chrome, Edge (Blink/WebKit) – only changes border */
input[type="radio"]::-webkit-radio {
  accent-color: #ff5722; /* Same as accent-color but only works in WebKit */
}

/* Firefox – works via the generic accent-color as of v92 */
input[type="radio"] {
  accent-color: #ff5722;
}
```

*Limitations*  

* Only works on **WebKit/Blink** forks; does **not** affect Firefox or Safari earlier than the `accent-color` spec.  
* You cannot change **size**, **shape**, or **animation** – only colours of the native parts that the engine exposes.  

**Bottom line** – Use this only if you have to support a tiny subset of browsers and you merely need a colour change. For anything beyond that, go with approach 1 or 2.

---

## 4️⃣ Security‑focused considerations (why a “security researcher” mindset matters)

While styling radio buttons is a UI concern, a security‑aware developer should keep the following in mind:

| Risk | Description | Mitigation |
|------|-------------|------------|
| **CSS Injection / XSS** | If you accept user‑generated CSS (e.g., from a WYSIWYG editor) that targets `input[type=radio]`, an attacker could hide or relocate the control, potentially tricking users into clicking the wrong option. | **Sanitise** any user‑supplied styles using a whitelist approach (e.g., CSP + CSP‑style‑src, DOMPurify). |
