### TL;DR  
Yes – you can show a “live” preview of something that lives on another page when a user hovers over an element. The usual approaches are:

| Technique | How it works | Security considerations |
|-----------|--------------|--------------------------|
| **Inline iframe** (or `<object>`/`<embed>`) | Load the target page (or a fragment of it) inside a sandboxed iframe that pops up on `:hover`. | Must be same‑origin **or** the target must send a permissive `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin` header. Use `sandbox`, `allow‑scripts`, `allow‑same‑origin` only if required. Set `X‑Frame‑Options: SAMEORIGIN` or use CSP `frame‑ancestors`. |
| **AJAX‑fetched HTML fragment** | `fetch()` the HTML of the element, inject it into a tooltip‑style `<div>` that becomes visible on hover. | Same‑origin rule applies. If the content is from a different domain you need CORS. Always **sanitize** before inserting (`DOMPurify`, CSP `script-src ‘nonce’`). |
| **Server‑side rendering of a screenshot** | A headless browser (Puppeteer, Playwright, wkhtmltopdf, etc.) renders the target page to PNG/JPEG; the image is then shown in a tooltip. | No direct DOM interaction, so XSS risk is low. Still enforce authentication/authorisation on the screenshot endpoint. |
| **CSS `content: url()`** (static image) | Use a sprite sheet or a pre‑generated preview image that appears via `::after` on `:hover`. | No JavaScript, but limited to static images. |

Below you’ll find a **step‑by‑step guide** for the two most flexible patterns (iframe & AJAX) along with a deep dive into the security implications that a security‑researcher would look for.

---

## 1. Using a Sandbox‑ed iframe (the “live” approach)

### 1.1. When to use it
- You need a **live, interactive** preview (e.g., a small form, a video player, a widget that reacts to user input).
- The preview page is **under your control** (same origin) or you have an explicit CORS/`X‑Frame‑Options` agreement with the owner.

### 1.2. Minimal HTML / CSS

```html
<style>
  .preview‑wrapper {
    position: relative;               /* anchor for tooltip */
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .preview‑wrapper:hover .preview‑frame {
    display: block;                  /* show on hover */
  }

  .preview‑frame {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    top: 120%;                         /* below the trigger element */
    left: 0;
    width: 300px;
    height: 200px;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.2);
    z-index: 1000;
    background: #fff;
  }
</style>

<div class="preview‑wrapper">
  <button>Hover me →</button>
  <iframe class="preview‑frame"
          src="/preview‑page.html#target‑element"
          sandbox="allow‑same‑origin allow‑scripts"
          loading="lazy"></iframe>
</div>
```

### 1.3. Server‑side preparation

1. **Expose a fragment**: `/preview‑page.html#target‑element` should contain only the element you want to show, or you can use CSS on that page to hide everything else when `#target‑element` is present:

   ```css
   body:not(#target‑element) > * { display:none; }
   ```

2. **Headers** (important!):

   ```http
   Content‑Security‑Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-<random>'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';
   X‑Frame‑Options: SAMEORIGIN          # prevent other sites from framing you
   ```

   If you *must* allow other origins, replace with:

   ```http
   Content‑Security‑Policy: frame‑ancestors 'self' https://example.com;
   ```

### 1.4. Security pitfalls & mitigations

| Issue | Why it matters | Mitigation |
|-------|----------------|------------|
| **Cross‑origin framing** | An attacker could embed your page in a hidden `<iframe>` and trick users into interacting with it (clickjacking). | Set `X‑Frame‑Options: SAMEORIGIN` **or** CSP `frame‑ancestors`. Use `sandbox` on the iframe to restrict APIs (`allow‑same‑origin` only if you need to read cookies). |
| **Script injection via preview** | If the preview page contains unsanitised user‑generated content, an attacker could inject `<script>` that runs inside the iframe (still isolated, but can steal cookies if `allow‑same‑origin`). | Prefer `sandbox` without `allow‑same‑origin`. If you need same‑origin, ensure all content is **HTML‑escaped** or sanitized with `DOMPurify`. |
| **Resource leakage** | The preview page may load large images, fonts, third‑party scripts. | Lazy‑load (`loading="lazy"`), keep the preview size small, and use a **dedicated sub‑domain** (`preview.example.com`) with tighter CSP. |
| **Denial‑of‑service** | Hover triggers a network request for each user. | Cache the preview (HTTP `Cache‑Control`) and enable `prefetch` or `preload` to load it ahead of time. |
| **Information disclosure** | The preview might expose data that is only intended for the full page (e.g., user‑specific settings). | Perform **authorisation checks** on the preview endpoint. Do not expose any sensitive data in the preview. |

---

## 2. Using AJAX to fetch a HTML fragment (the “static‑snapshot” approach)

### 2.1. When to use it
- You only need a **static snapshot** of the target element (no interactivity needed).
- The target page is same‑origin **or** the remote server supports CORS.
- You want full control over sanitisation before insertion into the DOM.

### 2.2. High‑level flow

```
Hover → start fetch → receive HTML snippet → sanitize → inject into hidden <div> → reveal tooltip
```

### 2.3. Example implementation (vanilla JS + DOMPurify)

```html
<!-- Element that triggers the preview -->
<button id="trigger" data-preview-url="/api/preview?elem=card‑42">
  Hover for preview
</button>

<!-- Tooltip container (initially hidden) -->
<div id="tooltip" style="display:none; position:absolute; padding:8px; background:#fff; border:1px solid #ddd; box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.2);">
  <div id="tooltip‑content"></div>
</div>

<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/dompurify/2.4.4/purify.min.js"
        integrity="sha512-... (add SRI)" crossorigin="anonymous"></script>

<script>
  const trigger = document.getElementById('trigger');
  const tooltip = document.getElementById('tooltip');
  const tooltipContent = document.getElementById('tooltip-content');

  // Optional: memoise the fetched fragment to avoid repeat network calls
  const cache = {};

  trigger.addEventListener('mouseenter', async (e) => {
    const url = trigger.dataset.previewUrl;
    if (!cache[url]) {
      try {
        const resp = await fetch(url, { credentials: 'same-origin' }); // include cookies if needed
        if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${resp.status}`);
        const rawHTML = await resp.text();

        // **Sanitise** everything that could become executable JavaScript
        const cleanHTML = DOMPurify.sanitize(rawHTML, {
          ADD_TAGS: ['iframe'],          // only if you truly need them
          ADD_ATTR: ['target', 'allow'], // whitelist extra attrs
          FORBID_TAGS: ['script', 'style'],
          FORBID_ATTR: ['on*']           // block any on* event handlers
        });

        cache[url] = cleanHTML;
      } catch (err) {
        console.error('Preview fetch error:', err);
        cache[url] = `<em>Failed to load preview.</em>`;
      }
    }

    // Populate tooltip & position it
    tooltipContent.innerHTML = cache[url];
    const rect = trigger.getBoundingClientRect();
    tooltip.style.top = `${rect.bottom + window.scrollY + 4}px`;
    tooltip.style.left = `${rect.left + window.scrollX}px`;
    tooltip.style.display = 'block';
  });

  trigger.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
    tooltip.style.display = 'none';
  });
</script>
```

### 2.4. Server‑side endpoint (`/api/preview`)

```python
# Example in Flask (Python)
from flask import Flask, request, abort, make_response
import bleach   # sanitiser for server‑side double check

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/api/preview')
def preview():
    elem_id = request.args.get('elem')
    if not elem_id:
        abort(400)

    # Simple auth check – only logged‑in users may request
    if not request.cookies.get('session'):
        abort(403)

    # Load the full page/template, then extract the element
    # (you could also store a pre‑rendered fragment in a DB)
    html = render_template('full_page.html')
    # Use lxml/BeautifulSoup to extract the element by id
    from bs4 import BeautifulSoup
    soup = BeautifulSoup(html, 'html.parser')
    target = soup.find(id=elem_id)
    if not target:
        abort(404)

    # Server‑side sanitisation (defence in depth)
    safe_html = bleach.clean(str(target), tags=bleach.sanitizer.ALLOWED_TAGS, attributes=bleach.sanitizer.ALLOWED_ATTRIBUTES)

    resp = make_response(safe_html)
    # Allow AJAX from same origin only (no CORS needed)
    resp.headers['Cache-Control'] = 'public, max-age=300'   # 5‑min cache
    return resp
```

### 2.5. Security checklist for the AJAX route

| Threat | Counter‑measure |
|--------|-----------------|
| **XSS injection** | - **Client‑side**: `DOMPurify` before inserting.<br>- **Server‑side**: also run `bleach` or similar.<br>- Use a strict CSP: `script-src 'self'` (no inline scripts). |
| **CSRF** | The preview endpoint should **not perform state‑changing actions**; still, require a `SameSite=Lax` or `Strict` session cookie and optionally a custom CSRF token for POST‑type preview requests. |
| **Information leakage** | Check authorisation (is the authenticated user allowed to see this element?). Do **not** include hidden fields containing secrets. |
| **Open redirect / URL manipulation** | Validate the `?elem=` param against a whitelist of known IDs; never use raw user input to build a file path. |
| **Denial‑of‑service** | Rate‑limit the endpoint (e.g., 5 req/sec per IP). Cache fragments for a short period. |
| **CORS mis‑configuration** | If you *must* allow other origins, restrict it to a known list: `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://trusted.example.com`. Never use `*` for an endpoint that returns user‑specific HTML. |

---

## 3. When you can’t rely on same‑origin (cross‑domain previews)

If the preview lives on **another domain you don’t control**, the options shrink:

1. **Ask the remote site to expose a **preview API** (CORS‑enabled) that returns a **JSON** representation or an **image** of the element.  
2. **Use a server‑side proxy**: your backend fetches the remote page, extracts the fragment, sanitises it, and serves it from your domain (so the browser sees it as same‑origin).  

   *Be aware* that this creates a **proxy** that can be abused for open‑redirect or open‑proxy attacks. Secure it by:
   - Whitelisting allowed hostnames.
   - Enforcing `Referer`/`Origin` checks.
   - Limiting response size and timeouts.

---

## 4. Alternative: Pre‑generated image sprites (no JS)

If you only need a **static visual cue** (e.g., a thumbnail of a product card), the simplest, most secure way is to generate an image in advance (via a CI job or a headless‑browser service) and use CSS:

```css
.preview-btn:hover::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 120%;
  left: 0;
  width: 200px;
  height: 120px;
  background: url('/img/previews/product‑42.png') no-repeat center/contain;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.15);
}
```

*Pros*: Zero JavaScript surface, no XSS risk, trivial CSP.  
*Cons*: No interactivity, needs a pipeline to keep images up‑to‑date.

---

## 5. Full “Security‑Researcher” checklist before you ship

| Area | Question | Action |
|------|----------|--------|
| **Origin & headers** | Is the preview page served from the same origin? Do you control `X‑Frame‑Options` / CSP? | Use `SAMEORIGIN` or explicit `frame‑ancestors`. |
| **Sandboxing** | Does the iframe need any privileged APIs (`allow‑scripts`, `allow‑same‑origin`)? | Prefer the tightest sandbox possible. |
| **Input validation** | Are URL parameters (`?elem=`) validated against a whitelist? | Use a strict allowlist or map IDs to internal keys. |
| **Output sanitisation** | Is HTML from the preview sanitised both on server and client? | Double‑layer sanitisation (`DOMPurify` + Bleach). |
| **CSP** | Does the page have a Content‑Security‑Policy that blocks inline scripts and disallows unknown origins? | Add `script-src 'self'` and `object-src 'none'`. |
| **Rate limiting** | Can an attacker hammer the preview endpoint to exhaust resources? | Implement IP‑based or auth‑token‑based throttling (`/api/preview`). |
| **Authentication** | Does the preview reveal data that only logged‑in users should see? | Verify session token before serving. |
| **Cache** | Are you inadvertently caching personalised previews for other users? | Set `Cache‑Control: private, max-age=300` for user‑specific content. |
| **Third‑party scripts** | Does the preview page load any third‑party libraries? | Pin versions, use Subresource Integrity (SRI), limit `allow‑scripts` if needed. |
| **Accessibility** | Is the hover preview usable with keyboards / screen‑readers? | Provide a focusable fallback (e.g., `onfocus` + `aria-describedby`). |

---

## 6. Quick “starter‑kit” code snippet (iframe + CSP)

```html
<!-- index.html -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Hover preview demo</title>
<style>
  .hover‑target { position:relative; display:inline-block; }
  .hover‑target:hover .preview‑frame { display:block; }
  .preview‑frame {
    display:none;
    position:absolute;
    top:100%; left:0;
    width:320px; height:240px;
    border:1px solid #ddd;
    background:#fff;
    z-index:100;
    box-shadow:0 4px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.2);
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="hover‑target">
  <button>Hover me</button>
  <iframe class="preview‑frame"
          src="/preview.html#card‑7"
          sandbox="allow‑same‑origin allow‑scripts"
          loading="lazy"></iframe>
</div>
</body>
</html>
```

