## 4️⃣ Security‑Focused Checklist (what the generator already does)

| Feature | Why it matters | Where it lives |
|---------|----------------|----------------|
| **Helmet** | Sets HTTP‑security headers (HSTS, X‑Frame‑Options, …) | `server.js` |
| **CORS whitelist** | Stops browsers from calling API from untrusted origins | `server.js` (`corsOrigin` env var) |
| **Rate limiting** | Prevents credential‑stuffing/brute‑force on `/auth/login` and any endpoint | `server.js` (`apiLimiter`) |
| **JWT authentication** | Stateless, signed token; no session‑id leakage | `passport-jwt` config in `server.js` |
| **express‑validator** | Whitelists allowed fields, sanitises strings, checks types | `router` files |
| **Sequelize prepared statements** | All DB calls are parameterised → no SQL‑i | Model / controller (handled by Sequelize) |
| **Environment‑based secrets** | Keeps keys out of source code (dev vs prod) | `.env` (JWT secret, DB config) |
| **Strict `allowNull` / `unique` enforcement** | Mirrors DB constraints at the API layer, reduces attack surface | Generated model definitions |
| **No `eval` / dynamic code generation** | The generator only writes static template files | `generator.js` (safe I/O only) |

> **Important:** Before you push to production, **change** the default JWT secret (`CHANGE_ME_IN_PROD`) and **tighten** the CORS origin (e.g., `https://myapp.example.com`). Also replace the demo login route with a real user‑store + password hashing (`bcrypt`).

