### 2.2 `background.js` (service worker)

```js
// background.js – ES6 module style (supported by MV3)
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((request, sender, sendResponse) => {
  if (request.type !== 'lookup') return false; // ignore unrelated messages

  const term = encodeURIComponent(request.term.trim());
  const apiUrl = `https://my-backend.example.com/quote?term=${term}`;

  fetch(apiUrl, {
    method: 'GET',
    credentials: 'omit'   // no cookies, keep it stateless
  })
    .then(resp => {
      if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${resp.status}`);
      return resp.json();
    })
    .then(data => {
      // Expected shape: {quote: string, sourceUrl: string}
      sendResponse({ success: true, quote: data.quote, sourceUrl: data.sourceUrl });
    })
    .catch(err => {
      console.error('Quote lookup failed', err);
      sendResponse({ success: false, error: err.message });
    });

  // Return true to indicate we will respond async
  return true;
});
```

**Security notes**

- **`credentials: 'omit'`** ensures no cookies or auth headers leak to the back‑end.  
- All **error handling** is internal – we never expose stack traces to the page.  
- The back‑end must enforce **CORS** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://<extension-id>.chromiumapp.org`) to prevent other sites from abusing the endpoint.

