## 4️⃣ Security Hardening Checklist (Researcher Lens)

| # | Concern | Mitigation (implemented / recommended) |
|---|---------|----------------------------------------|
| 1 | **Content‑Security‑Policy** (CSP) | Header set in `next.config.mjs` (restrict scripts, styles, fonts). Adjust to fit any external resources you actually need. |
| 2 | **XSS via MDX** | Using `rehype-sanitize` on the rendered HTML (see `PostPage`). Avoid `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` without sanitisation. |
| 3 | **Clickjacking** | `X‑Frame‑Options: DENY` header prevents embedding. |
| 4 | **MIME sniffing** | `X‑Content‑Type‑Options: nosniff`. |
| 5 | **Referrer leakage** | `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin`. |
| 6 | **Permissions** | `Permissions-Policy` disables camera/mic/geolocation. |
| 7 | **Static assets** | Serve images, fonts from your own domain (or trusted CDN). If using a CDN, add it to CSP `style-src`/`font-src`. |
| 8 | **Dependency hygiene** | Run `npm audit` & `npm outdated` periodically. Keep `next` and security‑related libs up‑to‑date. |
| 9 | **Rate‑limiting & bots** | For a static site this isn’t a concern, but if you ever add APIs (contact forms, comment system) enforce rate‑limits. |
|10| **HTTPS** | Deploy on Vercel/GitHub Pages → automatically HTTPS. Never serve over plain HTTP. |
|11| **Secure headers on static export** | When using GitHub Pages, you can’t set custom headers directly; instead, add a `netlify.toml` or `vercel.json` if you host elsewhere, or use Cloudflare Workers to inject CSP. |
|12| **Open Graph & SEO** | Avoid user‑controlled OG titles/descriptions – they come from your MDX front‑matter only. Validate length. |
|13| **File inclusion** | The loader only reads files from `posts/` directory; never expose arbitrary paths. |
|14| **Server‑Side Rendering (SSR) safety** | In this starter we use **static generation** only (`output: 'export'`). No server‑side code runs per request, reducing attack surface. |
|15| **Rate‑limit build-time data fetching** | Not applicable (all data locally). If you later pull from a headless CMS, add caching/TTL. |

**Quick test**: After deployment, run an automated scanner (e.g., `OWASP ZAP` or `nikto`) against the live URL to verify headers and see if any XSS vectors slip through.

