## 5️⃣ Deploying

### 5.1 Deploy to Vercel (one‑click)

1. Push the repo to GitHub.  
2. Go to <https://vercel.com/new> → import your repo.  
3. Vercel auto‑detects Next.js & Tailwind; click **Deploy**.  

> Vercel automatically adds the same security headers defined in `next.config.mjs`.

### 5️⃣2 Deploy as **Static Site** on GitHub Pages

```bash
# Build + export
npm run build
npm run export   # outputs to ./out
```

Now push the `out` folder to the `gh-pages` branch (or configure GitHub Actions).

#### GitHub Actions (optional)

Create `.github/workflows/deploy.yml`:

```yaml
name: Deploy to GitHub Pages

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  build-deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: write
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Setup Node
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: '20'

      - name: Install deps
        run: npm ci

      - name: Build & Export
        run: |
          npm run build
          npm run export

      - name: Deploy to GitHub Pages
        uses: peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v4
        with:
          github_token: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
          publish_dir: ./out
          # optional: if your repo is named my-blog and you want repo‑root:
          #   destination_dir: .
```

> After the workflow runs, your blog will be live at `https://<username>.github.io/<repo>/`.  
> You can also add a custom domain in the repository Settings → Pages.

