## 5️⃣  Deploying Securely (Production Tips)

| ✅ | Recommendation | Why it matters |
|---|----------------|----------------|
| **CSP Header** | Serve the CSP via an HTTP response header (e.g., `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com; img-src 'self' data:;`). | Guarantees the browser blocks any unintended resources, even if the HTML is tampered. |
| **HTTPS Only** | Use TLS (let’s Encrypt is free). | Prevents man‑in‑the‑middle tampering of scripts or data. |
| **Subresource Integrity (SRI)** | Keep the hashes in the `<link>`/`<script>` tags up‑to‑date whenever you upgrade a library. | Guarantees the exact code you audited is executed. |
| **Strict Transport Security (HSTS)** | `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains`. | Forces browsers to always use HTTPS. |
| **Secure Cookies (if you ever add auth)** | `Set-Cookie: …; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax`. | Mitigates session‑hijacking. |
| **Server‑Side Rate Limiting** | If you expose any API (e.g., price‑lookup), throttle per‑IP. | Thwarts DoS attempts. |
| **Regular Dependency Audits** | Run `npm audit` or `snyk test` on the libraries you use. | Detects newly disclosed vulnerabilities. |
| **Static Code Analysis** | Tools like **ESLint** with security plugins (e.g., `eslint-plugin-security`). | Catches insecure patterns before they ship. |

