# Chapter X – Displaying API Data  
*(Learning through Real‑World Examples – JavaScript for Beginners)*  

> **Security‑first mindset** – Even when you’re just learning how to pull data from a public API, it’s a great habit to think about the security consequences of the code you write. In this chapter we’ll not only show you **how** to fetch and display data, we’ll also sprinkle **security‑focused “why” notes** so you can carry these ideas into every project you build.

---

## 1. Why Pull Data from an API?

Modern web apps are rarely static.  They get dynamic content from **APIs** (Application Programming Interfaces) – services that expose data (weather, news, product catalogs, etc.) over HTTP.  

*Real‑world analogy:*  
Think of an API as a vending machine. You insert a request (coin + button press) and the machine dispenses a snack (JSON payload). Your JavaScript code is the person standing in front of the machine, asking politely for the snack and then displaying it on the page.

---

## 2. The Core Steps

| Step | What you do | Security tip |
|------|-------------|--------------|
| **1️⃣ Choose an API** | Pick a public, documented endpoint (e.g., OpenWeather, GitHub, The Cat API). | Prefer APIs that support **HTTPS** – it encrypts data in transit and protects against eavesdropping. |
| **2️⃣ Request the data** | Use `fetch()` (or `axios`/`XMLHttpRequest`) with the correct URL and HTTP method. | **Never** embed secret keys in client‑side code. If the API requires an API key, keep it on a server or use a proxy. |
| **3️⃣ Parse the response** | Convert the raw HTTP response to usable JavaScript objects (`response.json()`). | Validate the shape of the data (type‑checking, schema validation) before using it. |
| **4️⃣ Render to the DOM** | Create HTML elements or update existing ones with the data. | **Sanitize** any content that might contain user‑controlled strings to prevent XSS. |
| **5️⃣ Handle errors** | Catch network & logic errors and give feedback. | Do not expose internal error details to users; log them securely on the server if needed. |

---

## 3. Example 1 – A Weather Widget (OpenWeatherMap)

> **Goal:** Show the current temperature for a city the user types in.  
> **API used:** `https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q={city}&units=metric&appid={API_KEY}` (HTTPS, public documentation).  
> **Security focus:** No API key in the client (we’ll use a tiny server‑side proxy).

### 3.1. Folder layout

```
weather-widget/
│
├─ index.html
├─ style.css
├─ main.js
└─ server/
   └─ proxy.js      (Node.js – tiny Express server)
```

### 3.2. The HTML (index.html)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>🌤️ Weather Widget</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Weather at a Glance</h1>

  <form id="weather-form">
    <input type="text" id="city" placeholder="Enter city name" required />
    <button type="submit">Get Weather</button>
  </form>

  <section id="result" aria-live="polite"></section>

  <script src="main.js" type="module"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

> **Security note:**  
> *The `aria-live="polite"` attribute improves accessibility without increasing attack surface.*

### 3.3. Styling (style.css) — keep it simple, not the focus of this chapter.

```css
body { font-family: sans-serif; padding: 2rem; }
#result { margin-top: 1rem; }
.error { color: crimson; }
```

### 3.4. The browser JavaScript (main.js)

```js
// main.js – runs in the browser
const form = document.getElementById('weather-form');
const cityInput = document.getElementById('city');
const resultDiv = document.getElementById('result');

// Helper: safely set innerHTML of an element
function setSafeHTML(el, html) {
  // Create a temporary element to leverage the browser's parsing
  const temp = document.createElement('template');
  temp.innerHTML = html.trim();
  // Remove any <script> tags that could have slipped in
  temp.content.querySelectorAll('script').forEach(s => s.remove());
  el.innerHTML = '';
  el.appendChild(temp.content);
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Event listener – user submits a city name
form.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();                      // stop page reload
  const city = cityInput.value.trim();

  // Basic client‑side validation (avoid empty requests)
  if (!city) {
    setSafeHTML(resultDiv, '<p class="error">Please type a city name.</p>');
    return;
  }

  // ----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Call our *proxy* – never hits the real API key from the browser
  try {
    const resp = await fetch(`/api/weather?city=${encodeURIComponent(city)}`);

    // 3️⃣ Check HTTP status – non‑200 is an error from the server
    if (!resp.ok) {
      if (resp.status === 404) {
        throw new Error('City not found.');
      }
      throw new Error(`Server error (${resp.status})`);
    }

    const data = await resp.json(); // 4️⃣ Parse JSON
    // 5️⃣ Validate shape – simple check, production code might use a schema lib
    if (!data.main || typeof data.main.temp !== 'number') {
      throw new Error('Unexpected API response');
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣ Render safely – we only inject numbers, not raw strings
    const html = `
      <h2>Weather in ${escapeHTML(data.name)}</h2>
      <p>🌡️ ${data.main.temp.toFixed(1)} °C</p>
      <p>${escapeHTML(data.weather[0].description)}</p>
    `;
    setSafeHTML(resultDiv, html);
  } catch (err) {
    // 7️⃣ Friendly error handling
    console.error(err); // <-- dev only, do not expose stack to user
    setSafeHTML(resultDiv, `<p class="error">${escapeHTML(err.message)}</p>`);
  }
});

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Tiny HTML‑escaping helper – prevents XSS when we interpolate strings
function escapeHTML(str) {
  return String(str)
    .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')
    .replace(/</g, '&lt;')
    .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')
    .replace(/"/g, '&quot;')
    .replace(/'/g, '&#39;');
}
```

#### What’s happening?

| Line | Action | Security reasoning |
|------|--------|--------------------|
| `e.preventDefault()` | Stops default form submission (full page reload). | Reduces attack surface: the page stays a single‑page app, no accidental data leak through query strings. |
| `encodeURIComponent(city)` | URL‑encodes the city name. | Prevents injection attacks on the query string, ensures proper CORS handling. |
| `if (!resp.ok) …` | Handles non‑200 responses early. | Avoids using malformed data that could cause downstream errors. |
| `escapeHTML()` | Sanitizes any user‑controlled strings before inserting into the DOM. | Stops **Cross‑Site Scripting (XSS)** – a malicious city name like `<script>alert(1)</script>` would be rendered harmless. |
| `setSafeHTML()` | Strips any `<script>` tags that might slip through. | Defense‑in‑depth against XSS. |
| `console.error(err)` | Logs the error for developers only. | Does not expose stack traces or internal details to end‑users (information leakage). |

---

### 3.5. The Proxy Server (server/proxy.js)

> **Why a proxy?** Because many free APIs require an **API key**. If you embed that key in the client, anyone can copy it and abuse your quota. A tiny server acts as a gatekeeper: it holds the secret key, forwards the request, and returns only the data you need.

```js
// proxy.js – tiny Express server (Node.js 18+)
import express from 'express';
import fetch from 'node-fetch';
import dotenv from 'dotenv';
dotenv.config();               // loads .env file with API_KEY

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
const API_KEY = process.env.OPENWEATHER_API_KEY; // NEVER commit this to Git!

// Enable CORS only for our own origin (good practice)
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const allowedOrigin = process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGIN || 'http://localhost:3000';
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', allowedOrigin);
  next();
});

// Endpoint that the front‑end calls
app.get('/api/weather', async (req, res) => {
  const city = req.query.city;
  if (!city) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'city query param required' });

  const url = `https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q=${encodeURIComponent(city)}&units=metric&appid=${API_KEY}`;

  try {
    // Forward request to real API
    const apiResp = await fetch(url);
    if (!apiResp.ok) {
      // Forward appropriate HTTP status (e.g., 404 for unknown city)
      const errBody = await apiResp.json();
      return res.status(apiResp.status).json(errBody);
    }

    const data = await apiResp.json();

    // OPTIONAL: filter out any fields you don’t need – reduces data leakage
    const safeData = {
      name: data.name,
      main: { temp: data.main.temp },
      weather: [{ description: data.weather[0].description }],
    };
    res.json(safeData);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Proxy error:', err);
    // Never leak internal error messages to the client
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
  }
});

app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`🚀 Proxy listening on ${PORT}`));
```

#### Proxy‑side security highlights

| Concern | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| **API key exposure** | Stored in environment variable (`.env`), never in source control. |
| **Cross‑Site Request Forgery (CSRF)** | Not an issue for a **GET** endpoint that only reads public data. If you add POST/PUT later, use CSRF tokens or SameSite cookies. |
| **Rate‑limiting / abuse** | You could add a simple in‑memory counter or use a package like `express-rate-limit`. |
| **CORS** | Header restricts which origins can call the proxy (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`). |
| **Error leakage** | Server returns generic messages (`Internal server error`) to the client, while detailed logs stay on the server. |

---

## 4. Example 2 – Displaying a List of GitHub Repositories (Public API)

Often you’ll work with **public, unauthenticated APIs** that don’t need a secret key. GitHub’s REST API is a perfect candidate.

### 4.1. What we’ll build

* A simple page that lets a user type a GitHub username and shows the **first 5 public repositories**, each with a link, description, and star count.

### 4.2. HTML (minimal)

```html
<form id="gh-form">
  <input id="gh-user" placeholder="GitHub username" required />
  <button type="submit">Show repos</button>
</form>
<ul id="repo-list"></ul>
```

### 4.3. JavaScript (modules)

```js
// gh.js – no server involved, API is public and uses HTTPS
const form = document.getElementById('gh-form');
const userInput = document.getElementById('gh-user');
const list = document.getElementById('repo-list');

form.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const username = userInput.value.trim();

  // Simple validation – avoid massive queries
  if (!/^[a-zA-Z0-9-]{1,39}$/.test(username)) {
    list.innerHTML = '<li class="error">Invalid username format.</li>';
    return;
  }

  try {
    const resp = await fetch(`https://api.github.com/users/${encodeURIComponent(username)}/repos?per_page=5&sort=updated`);
    if (resp.status === 404) throw new Error('User not found');
    if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`GitHub API error (${resp.status})`);
    const repos = await resp.json();

    // Defensive check – GitHub always returns an array
    if (!Array.isArray(repos)) throw new Error('Unexpected response format');

    // Clear previous results
    list.innerHTML = '';

    repos.forEach(repo => {
      // We only use fields that are guaranteed to be strings/numbers
      const li = document.createElement('li');
      li.innerHTML = `
        <a href="${escapeHTML(repo.html_url)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          ${escapeHTML(repo.name)}
        </a>
        ${repo.description ? `<p>${escapeHTML(repo.description)}</p>` : ''}
        ⭐ ${repo.stargazers_count}
      `;
      // Using innerHTML *only* for controlled data (no user input)
      list.appendChild(li);
    });
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    list.innerHTML = `<li class="error">${escapeHTML(err.message)}</li>`;
  }
});

// Tiny escape helper – same as before
function escapeHTML(str) {
  return String(str)
    .replace(/[&<>"']/g, ch => ({
      '&': '&amp;',
      '<': '&lt;',
      '>': '&gt;',
      '"': '&quot;',
      "'": '&#39;'
    }[ch]));
}
```

#### Security Nuggets

| Situation | Why it matters | What we do |
|-----------|----------------|------------|
| **User‑supplied username goes into a URL** | Could be used for path‑traversal style attacks on the fetch call (e.g., `../../etc/passwd`). | `encodeURIComponent()` + regex whitelist (letters, numbers, dash). |
| **Injecting repo fields into HTML** | GitHub data is generally safe, but a malicious user could create a repo with a JavaScript‑bearing description. | Escape everything (`escapeHTML`) and *never* use `innerHTML` with raw user data. |
| **Opening links in a new tab** | `target="_blank"` can be abused to give the new page access to `window.opener`. | Add `rel="noopener noreferrer"` to break that link. |
| **Rate‑limiting** | GitHub enforces **unauthenticated** rate limits (60 requests/hour per IP). | The UI disables the button for a short “cool‑down” after 5 rapid queries – a simple courtesy. |

---

## 5. Common Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them

| Pitfall | Symptoms | Fix (with security lens) |
|---------|----------|--------------------------|
| **Hard‑coding API keys** | Everyone can view them in the browser dev tools. | Move the key to a server‑side environ variable and expose only a minimal proxy endpoint. |
| **Trusting API responses blindly** | App crashes or shows wrong data. | Validate the JSON schema (`Array.isArray`, `typeof`, optional libraries like `ajv`). |
| **Injecting raw strings into the DOM** | XSS when the API returns HTML or malicious content. | Use `textContent` instead of `innerHTML` when possible, or sanitize with a library like DOMPurify. |
| **Ignoring CORS errors** | “Blocked by CORS policy” in console. | Ensure the API supports CORS for your origin. If you control the API, set the appropriate `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header. |
| **Over‑exposing error details** | Stack traces appear in the UI. | Log details on the server; return generic messages (`"Something went wrong"`) to the client. |
| **Image/Script URL injection** | `<img src="javascript:alert(1)">` – a classic XSS vector. | Whitelist allowed URL schemes (`http`, `https`) before setting `src` attributes. |

---

## 6. Quick Checklist – “Did I Sec‑Ready This Feature?”

Before you ship a page that displays API data, run through this **5‑point checklist**:

1. **HTTPS everywhere** – Both your site and the API endpoint must be served over TLS.  
2. **No secret leaks** – API keys are stored server‑side; the browser never sees them.  
3. **Input validation** – All user‑controlled values (search boxes, URL params) are validated/escaped.  
4. **Output sanitization** – Every piece of data you inject into the DOM is either `textContent` or escaped HTML.  
5. **Error handling** – Users see friendly messages; internals stay in logs.

If you can answer “yes” to each, you’re in good shape.

---

## 7. TL;DR – Minimal Code to Display API Data (Secure)

```html
<!-- index.html -->
<form id="f"><input id="q"><button>Go</button></form>
<div id="out"></div>

<script type="module">
  const f = document.getElementById('f');
  const q = document.getElementById('q');
  const out = document.getElementById('out');

  function esc(s) { return String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g,ch=>({ '&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[ch])); }

  f.addEventListener('submit', async e=>{
    e.preventDefault();
    const term = encodeURIComponent(q.value.trim());
    if (!term) return out.textContent = 'Enter something';

    try {
      const r = await fetch(`/api/proxy?search=${term}`);
      if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`API ${r.status}`);
      const d = await r.json();
      out.innerHTML = `<pre>${esc(JSON.stringify(d, null, 2))}</pre>`;
    } catch (err){ out.textContent = err.message; }
  });
</script>
```

- **One line of fetch** → Calls a server‑side proxy that hides keys.  
- **Escaping** (`esc`) prevents XSS.  
- **`innerHTML` only on data we just escaped** → No raw HTML ever touches the page.

---

## 8. Take‑away for the Reader

You now have:

* **A concrete, real‑world example** (weather widget) that shows the full flow – from user input → proxy → API → DOM.  
* **A second, key‑free example** (GitHub repos) that demonstrates how to stay safe even with public data.  
* **Security‑first patterns** you can copy‑paste into any future project: *HTTPS, server‑side secrets, input validation, output sanitization, sensible error handling*.

When you move on to more advanced topics (POSTing data, authentication, websockets), the same checklist will keep you on the right side of security. Happy coding—and happy securing! 🚀

