## 4️⃣ OPERATIONAL & DEPLOYMENT HARDENING

| Area | Recommended Settings |
|------|----------------------|
| **MongoDB Connection** | Use **TLS 1.2+** (`mongodb+srv://.../?tls=true`). Verify server certificate (`tlsAllowInvalidCertificates: false`). |
| **Database Users** | Create dedicated users: `appUser` (read/write on `users,rooms,messages` only) and `adminUser` (limited to admin tasks). Do **not** run the app as `root`. |
| **Field‑Level Encryption** | If you can, enable **MongoDB Client‑Side FLE** (requires `mongocryptd` or the newer driver‑side encryption). Store the key vault in a separate collection with strict access. |
| **Backup / Restore** | Use encrypted snapshots (e.g., AWS EBS encryption). Verify backup integrity weekly. |
| **Secrets Management** | Store `JWT_SECRET`, `CHAT_MESSAGE_ENC_KEY`, DB credentials in a vault (AWS Secrets Manager / HashiCorp Vault). Load at runtime via ENV vars only. |
| **Container / VM Hardening** | Run with non‑root user, drop unnecessary capabilities (`NET_RAW` etc.), set `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true` if possible. |
| **Monitoring & Auditing** | Enable MongoDB **auditing** (`auditAuthorizationSuccess:true`). Forward logs to a SIEM. Alert on: >10 failed login attempts from same IP, sudden spike in writes, unusual admin actions. |
| **Rate Limiting & DoS Protection** | In addition to per‑endpoint limits, deploy a **Web Application Firewall (WAF)** in front of the API gateway. |
| **Compliance** | If you store PII, consider GDPR “right to be forgotten”: implement a **soft‑delete** (set `deletedAt`) and a background job that purges data after the retention period. |
| **Testing** | Run automated security tests with **OWASP ZAP**, **npm audit**, and a static analysis tool (e.g., **CodeQL**). Include unit tests for permission checks. |
| **Patch Management** | Keep MongoDB server, driver, and Node runtime up‑to‑date (critical CVEs are fixed quickly). |

