Below is a tiny, self‑contained HTML page that changes the page’s background colour each time the user clicks anywhere on the page.  
It uses plain JavaScript (no external libraries) and generates a random colour on every click.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Click‑to‑Change Background</title>
    <style>
        /* Make the whole viewport clickable */
        html, body {
            height: 100%;
            margin: 0;
            cursor: pointer;               /* give a visual cue */
            transition: background 0.3s;   /* smooth colour change */
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: center;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            color: #fff;
        }

        h1 {
            text-shadow: 0 0 5px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body onclick="changeBackground()">
    <h1>Click anywhere to change the background colour</h1>

    <script>
        /**
         * Generates a random colour in hexadecimal format.
         * @returns {string} e.g. "#3fa9e7"
         */
        function randomHexColor() {
            // Math.random() gives a float [0,1); multiply by 0xFFFFFF+1 and floor it.
            const randomInt = Math.floor(Math.random() * 0x1000000);
            // Convert to hex and pad with leading zeros if needed.
            return "#" + randomInt.toString(16).padStart(6, "0");
        }

        /**
         * Called on every click – changes the document background colour.
         */
        function changeBackground() {
            const newColour = randomHexColor();
            document.body.style.backgroundColor = newColour;

            // Optionally, update the heading text so the user can see the hex value.
            const heading = document.querySelector("h1");
            if (heading) {
                heading.textContent = "Background: " + newColour;
            }
        }

        // (Optional) Set an initial colour on page load.
        window.addEventListener('load', changeBackground);
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works
1. **`onclick="changeBackground()"`** – The `<body>` tag listens for any click on the page and calls `changeBackground()`.
2. **`randomHexColor()`** – Generates a random 6‑digit hex colour (`#RRGGBB`).
3. **`changeBackground()`** – Sets the generated colour as the page’s background (`document.body.style.backgroundColor`) and updates the heading so the user sees the current colour code.
4. A short CSS transition (`background 0.3s`) makes the colour change appear smooth.

You can copy the above code into a file named `index.html` and open it in any modern browser to see the effect. Feel free to extend it – for example, you could cycle through a predefined palette, store the last colour in `localStorage`, or add a “reset” button.

