Below is a concise, **security‑focused** walk‑through for moving a Quasar project from the `quasar dev` hot‑reload environment to a hardened, production‑ready server.  
The steps are split into three logical phases:

1. **Build the production artefacts** – make sure the bundle is stripped of any development‑only code, source‑maps, console noise, etc.  
2. **Choose a runtime** – static‑file serving (SPA/PWA), Node‑based SSR, or a hybrid setup.  
3. **Hardening the web server / reverse‑proxy** – TLS, CSP, HSTS, rate‑limiting, headers, etc.

---

## 1️⃣ Build the Production Bundle

| Command | What it does | Security‑relevant flags |
|---------|--------------|--------------------------|
| `quasar build` | Generates the SPA/PWA files in `dist/spa` (or `dist/pwa`) | `--prod` (default), `--skip-pkg`, `--max-old-space-size=4096` if you hit memory limits. |
| `quasar build -m ssr` | Generates a server‑side‑rendered (SSR) bundle (`dist/ssr`). | Same as above, plus it creates a Node entry point (`server/index.js`). |
| `quasar build -m cordova` / `-m capacitor` / `-m electron` | Not relevant for a web server. | — |

### Tips for a “clean” build

* **Disable source‑maps** for the final production bundle (they leak stack traces):  

  ```bash
  # In quasar.conf.js
  build: {
    sourceMap: false,
    // also good to turn off Vue devtools
    vueRouterMode: 'history',
    // minify the code
    terserOptions: {
      compress: {
        drop_console: true,   // removes console.* statements
        drop_debugger: true
      }
    }
  }
  ```

* **Environment variables** – Never ship a `.env` file with secret keys. Use the `env` object in `quasar.conf.js` and inject only **public** config at build time. Keep private API keys in the backend.

* **Static asset fingerprinting** – Quasar already adds hash fingerprints (`app.[hash].js`). This prevents cache‑poisoning attacks.

---

## 2️⃣ Serve the Bundle

### 2A – Pure SPA/PWA (static files)

The output of `quasar build` is a directory of static files (`index.html`, JS, CSS, assets). Any **lightweight HTTP server** can serve it:

```bash
# 1️⃣ Install a minimal static file server (node)
npm i -g serve   # or use you own preferred binary
serve -s dist/spa   # -s = single‑page mode (fallback to index.html)
```

**Production‑grade options**

| Server | Why choose it | Quick start example |
|--------|---------------|---------------------|
| **NGINX** | Mature, can terminate TLS, fine‑grained rate‑limit & header injection | `nginx -c /path/to/nginx.conf` |
| **Caddy** | Automatic HTTPS (Let's Encrypt) + simple config | `caddy run --config Caddyfile` |
| **Apache** | Legacy environments | `apachectl -k start` |
| **Node‑http‑server** (e.g., `express`) | If you already have a Node API on the same port | See “SSR” section below |

**Typical Nginx config (static SPA)**

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com www.example.com;

    # Enforce HTTPS – redirect to cert‑managed listener (see below)
    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name example.com www.example.com;

    # ------------------- TLS -------------------
    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem;
    ssl_protocols       TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers         HIGH:!aNULL:!MD5;
    add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload" always;

    # ------------------- Security Headers -------------------
    add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;
    add_header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN" always;
    add_header X-XSS-Protection "1; mode=block" always;
    add_header Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin" always;
    # Content‑Security‑Policy (customise to your CSP)
    add_header Content-Security-Policy "
        default-src 'self';
        script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net;
        style-src  'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com;
        img-src    'self' data:;
        font-src   'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com;
        connect-src 'self' https://api.mybackend.com;
        object-src 'none';
        frame-ancestors 'self';
    " always;

    # ------------------- Static Files -------------------
    root /var/www/example.com/dist/spa;
    index index.html;

    # SPA fallback (all non‑file routes -> index.html)
    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    }

    # Optional: gzip / brotli compression
    gzip on;
    gzip_types text/plain text/css application/javascript image/svg+xml;
    # If you have brotli compiled:
    # brotli on;
}
```

> **Security note:** In a static‑file scenario the server does **not** execute any application code, so the attack surface is limited to the web‑server itself (TLS, headers, path traversal, etc.). Make sure the `root` directory **does not** contain unwanted source files, `.env`, or build artefacts outside the `dist/` folder.

### 2B – Server‑Side Rendering (SSR)

If you need SSR (e.g., SEO, faster first‑paint), Quasar creates a Node entry point:

```bash
# Build SSR
quasar build -m ssr
# The output lives in dist/ssr
```

**Running the SSR server in production**

```bash
# Install production dependencies only (skip dev deps)
cd dist/ssr
npm ci --only=production   # respects package-lock.json

# Optional: use a process manager (PM2, systemd, Docker)
pm2 start server/index.js --name quasar-ssr --watch false
```

**Putting a reverse‑proxy in front**

Most production deployments put Nginx (or Caddy) in front of the Node SSR process for TLS termination, static‑asset caching, and request buffering.

```nginx
# Nginx example – forward all non‑static requests to Node
location / {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;   # Node SSR app
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    proxy_set_header Connection "";
    # Timeouts & buffering
    proxy_read_timeout 60s;
    proxy_send_timeout 60s;
}
# Static assets (same as static config above)
location /assets/ {
    expires 30d;
    access_log off;
    add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=2592000, immutable";
}
```

**Security‑hardening the Node process**

1. **Run as non‑root** – create a dedicated user (e.g., `quasar`), set `USER` in Dockerfile, or configure `pm2 start --uid`.
2. **Limit memory/cpu** – via Docker `--memory`, `--cpus`, or systemd `MemoryLimit=` / `CPUQuota=`.
3. **Turn off `NODE_ENV=development`** – ensure `process.env.NODE_ENV=production`. Quasar reads this automatically but double‑check.
4. **Avoid eval / Function constructors** – Quasar’s production build disables dev‑tools, but audit any third‑party plugins for dynamic code generation.
5. **Secure cookies** – If you set any cookies from the SSR server, use `Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict` flags.

---

## 3️⃣ Harden the Web Server (General Checklist)

| Area | Recommended Setting | Why it matters |
|------|---------------------|----------------|
| **TLS** | Use Let’s Encrypt (or a paid cert). Enforce `TLSv1.2+`, prefer `TLSv1.3`. | Prevent downgrade attacks. |
| **HSTS** | `max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload` | Forces browsers to always use HTTPS. |
| **CSP** | Restrictive default‑src + whitelisted CDN domains. Use `report-uri`/`report-to` for monitoring. | Mitigates XSS & data‑injection. |
| **X‑Frame‑Options** | `SAMEORIGIN` (or `DENY` if you never embed). | Prevent click‑jacking. |
| **X‑Content‑Type‑Options** | `nosniff`. | Stops MIME‑type sniffing attacks. |
| **Referrer‑Policy** | `strict-origin-when-cross-origin`. | Reduces leakage of URLs. |
| **Feature‑Policy / Permissions‑Policy** | Disable unnecessary APIs (e.g., `geolocation 'none'`). | Reduces attack surface from the browser. |
| **Rate Limiting** | Nginx `limit_req_zone` / `limit_req` or Cloudflare firewall. | Thwarts brute‑force & DoS. |
| **Logging** | Enable `access_log` with JSON format for SIEM. | Auditable trail. |
| **File System** | Serve only the `dist/` directory, no source code or hidden files. | Prevents accidental exposure of secrets. |
| **Content Compression** | Enable gzip/brotli **only** for safe MIME types. | Improves performance without opening compression‑based attacks. |
| **Security Scanning** | Run `nikto`, `OWASP ZAP`, or `npm audit` on the built bundle. | Detect misconfigurations before go‑live. |

---

## 4️⃣ Quick “One‑Liner” Cheat‑Sheet

```bash
# 1️⃣ Build production assets
quasar build                # SPA/PWA
# OR for SSR
# quasar build -m ssr

# 2️⃣ Serve with a hardened static server (example using Nginx)
sudo cp -r dist/spa /var/www/example.com
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/example.com.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx

# 3️⃣ If you need SSR:
cd dist/ssr && npm ci --only=production
pm2 start server/index.js --name quasar-ssr
# (Nginx reverse‑proxy config as shown above)

# 4️⃣ Verify TLS & headers
curl -I https://example.com | grep -iE "strict-transport-security|content-security-policy|x-frame-options"

# 5️⃣ Run a quick scan
docker run --rm -v $(pwd)/dist/spa:/site aquasec/trivy fs --security-checks vuln /site
```

---

## 5️⃣ TL;DR Summary

| Step | Command | What you get |
|------|---------|--------------|
| **Build** | `quasar build` *(SPA/PWA)*<br>`quasar build -m ssr` *(SSR)* | Optimized, fingerprinted static assets in `dist/…` |
| **Serve** | `serve -s dist/spa` *(quick node dev server)*<br>`nginx` / `caddy` / `apache` for production | Static files or Node‑SSR behind a reverse proxy |
| **Secure** | TLS, HSTS, CSP, X‑* headers, rate‑limit, run as non‑root, disable console/debug | Hardened surface, mitigated XSS/CSRF/DoS |
| **Validate** | `curl -I https://…` and run a scanner (ZAP, Trivy, Nikto) | Verify you didn’t miss a header or an exposed file |

Follow the checklist, and you’ll have the same bundle that `quasar dev` launches but served safely behind a production‑grade web server. If you need any deeper dive (e.g., Dockerizing the whole stack, CI/CD pipeline hardening, or CSP tuning), let me know!

