## 2️⃣ PHP – `register.php`

> **Important** – Put **all** the code **before any HTML output** (so you can set headers, start sessions, etc.).  
> Save this file as `register.php` (or any name you prefer) in the same directory as the HTML page.

```php
<?php
/* ==============================================================
   0️⃣  SECURITY SETTINGS
   ============================================================= */
declare(strict_types=1);
error_reporting(E_ALL);
ini_set('display_errors', '0');           // Never show raw errors to the user
ini_set('log_errors', '1');
ini_set('error_log', __DIR__.'/php_errors.log');

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Start session → needed for CSRF token handling
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
session_start();

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  Generate / Validate CSRF token
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function getCsrfToken(): string {
    if (empty($_SESSION['csrf_token'])) {
        $_SESSION['csrf_token'] = bin2hex(random_bytes(32));
    }
    return $_SESSION['csrf_token'];
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Database connection – use mysqli with exception mode
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
$mysqli = new mysqli('localhost', 'admin', 'admin1234', 'input');
if ($mysqli->connect_errno) {
    // Do NOT expose credentials – log and show generic message
    error_log('MySQL connection error: ' . $mysqli->connect_error);
    http_response_code(500);
    exit('Internal server error');
}
$mysqli->set_charset('utf8mb4');

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  Helper: redirect with a message (GET‑based flash)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function redirectWithMessage(string $msg, string $type = 'info'): void {
    $msg = urlencode($msg);
    $type = urlencode($type);
    header("Location: index.php?msg={$msg}&type={$type}");
    exit;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  Process POST request
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {

    // ---- CSRF check -------------------------------------------------
    if (!isset($_POST['csrf_token'], $_SESSION['csrf_token']) ||
        !hash_equals($_SESSION['csrf_token'], $_POST['csrf_token'])) {
        redirectWithMessage('Invalid CSRF token', 'error');
    }

    // ---- Input sanitisation / validation ---------------------------
    // (All fields are required in this example – adapt as needed)

    $name   = trim($_POST['name']   ?? '');
    $email  = trim($_POST['email']  ?? '');
    $phone  = trim($_POST['phone']  ?? '');
    $pass   = $_POST['pass']        ?? '';
    $gender = $_POST['gender'] ?? '';

    // Simple validation – replace with a library (e.g. Respect/Validation) if you need more
    if ($name === '' || $email === '' || $phone === '' || $pass === '' || $gender === '') {
        redirectWithMessage('All fields are required.', 'error');
    }

    if (!filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)) {
        redirectWithMessage('Invalid e‑mail format.', 'error');
    }

    if (!preg_match('/^[0-9+().\s-]{7,}$/', $phone)) {
        redirectWithMessage('Invalid phone number format.', 'error');
    }

    $allowedGenders = ['male', 'female', 'other'];
    if (!in_array($gender, $allowedGenders, true)) {
        redirectWithMessage('Invalid gender selection.', 'error');
    }

    // ---- Password handling -----------------------------------------
    // **Never** store passwords in plain text.
    $passwordHash = password_hash($pass, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);
    if ($passwordHash === false) {
        // Very unlikely, but we handle it
        redirectWithMessage('Failed to hash the password.', 'error');
    }

    // ---- Prepared statement (SQL‑Injection safe) --------------------
    $stmt = $mysqli->prepare(
        'INSERT INTO students (name, email, phone, password, gender)
         VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)'
    );
    if ($stmt === false) {
        error_log('Prepare failed: ' . $mysqli->error);
        redirectWithMessage('Database error (prepare).', 'error');
    }

    // Bind parameters: s = string, i = integer, d = double, b = blob
    $stmt->bind_param('sssss', $name, $email, $phone, $passwordHash, $gender);

    if ($stmt->execute()) {
        // Success – clear CSRF token (avoid double‑submit)
        unset($_SESSION['csrf_token']);
        redirectWithMessage('Registration successful! You can now log in.', 'info');
    } else {
        // Duplicate e‑mail? (Assuming you have a UNIQUE constraint)
        if ($stmt->errno === 1062) {
            redirectWithMessage('E‑mail already registered.', 'error');
        } else {
            error_log('Execute failed: ' . $stmt->error);
            redirectWithMessage('Database error (execute).', 'error');
        }
    }
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   6️⃣  If we reach this point the request was NOT POST → show the form.
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
$csrf_token = getCsrfToken();   // used by the HTML file (or embed the HTML directly here)
?>
```

### What the PHP does (step‑by‑step)

| # | Action | Why it matters |
|---|--------|----------------|
| 0 | Enable strict typing, hide raw errors, log them | Prevents information leakage & makes debugging easier |
| 1 | `session_start()` | Needed for CSRF token persistence |
| 2 | Generate a **cryptographically‑secure CSRF token** and store it in the session | Defends against Cross‑Site Request Forgery |
| 3 | Connect to MySQL using **mysqli** with `utf8mb4` charset | Avoids character‑set issues & SQLi |
| 4 | Helper redirects with a flash GET‑parameter | Allows you to inform the user without echoing raw HTML from PHP |
| 5 | **Validate** every field (non‑empty, e‑mail format, phone regex, gender whitelist) | Stops malformed data from entering the DB |
| 5 | **Hash** the password (`password_hash`) | Storing plain‑text passwords is a fatal security flaw |
| 5 | Use a **prepared statement** (`$stmt->prepare()`) – **never** concatenate user input into SQL | Eliminates SQL‑Injection risk |
| 5 | Capture duplicate‑key errors (e‑mail already exists) | Better UX |
| 6 | If not a POST request, simply render the form (`index.php`) with the CSRF token | Separation of concerns, easier to maintain |

