## 3. Database Layer – PDO with Strong Settings

```php
<?php
// src/Database.php
declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App;

use PDO;
use PDOException;

final class Database
{
    private static ?PDO $pdo = null;

    public static function getConnection(): PDO
    {
        if (self::$pdo !== null) {
            return self::$pdo;
        }

        Config::load();

        $dsn = sprintf(
            'mysql:host=%s;dbname=%s;charset=utf8mb4',
            Config::get('DB_HOST'),
            Config::get('DB_NAME')
        );

        $options = [
            PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE            => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION, // Throw on error
            PDO::ATTR_DEFAULT_FETCH_MODE => PDO::FETCH_ASSOC,
            PDO::ATTR_EMULATE_PREPARES   => false, // Use native prepares (prevents injection)
        ];

        try {
            self::$pdo = new PDO($dsn, Config::get('DB_USER'), Config::get('DB_PASS'), $options);
        } catch (PDOException $e) {
            // Log internally, show generic error to user
            error_log('DB connection error: ' . $e->getMessage());
            http_response_code(500);
            exit('Internal Server Error');
        }

        return self::$pdo;
    }
}
```

*Why these options?*  

- `ERRMODE_EXCEPTION` forces us to handle errors instead of silently ignoring them.  
- `EMULATE_PREPARES = false` ensures the driver uses real server‑side prepared statements, which eliminates many forms of SQL injection.  

