Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** of a tiny PHP‑based site that:

1. Presents a form so the user can upload a video file.
2. Stores the uploaded file in a temporary folder.
3. Calls **FFmpeg** to transcode the file to MP4 (H.264 video, AAC audio).
4. When the conversion finishes, the script returns a small HTML page that automatically starts the download of the newly created MP4 file.

> **⚠️ Security note** – This example is deliberately simple so you can see the core flow. In a production environment you **must** harden it (file‑type validation, size limits, sandboxed FFmpeg execution, safe temporary directories, CSRF protection, etc.). Those hardening steps are highlighted after the code.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| Requirement | Why it’s needed |
|-------------|-----------------|
| **PHP 7.4+** (with `exec()` enabled) | To run the server‑side script |
| **FFmpeg** (binary available in the system `$PATH` or a known absolute path) | Performs the video transcoding |
| **A writable temporary directory** (e.g., `uploads/` and `converted/`) | Stores the original upload and the resulting MP4 |

Make sure FFmpeg works from the command line:

```bash
ffmpeg -version
```

If it’s not in `$PATH`, replace `ffmpeg` in the code with the full path, e.g. `/usr/local/bin/ffmpeg`.

---

## 2. Directory layout

```
/var/www/video-converter/
│
├─ index.php          ← main page (upload form & processing)
├─ download.php       ← serves the final MP4 (optional, see below)
├─ uploads/           ← temporary storage for raw uploads (chmod 755)
└─ converted/         ← where the MP4 files end up (chmod 755)
```

Create the two sub‑folders and give the web‑server write permission:

```bash
mkdir uploads converted
chmod 755 uploads converted
chown www-data:www-data uploads converted   # adjust user/group as needed
```

---

## 3. The code

### `index.php`

```php
<?php
// ----------- CONFIGURATION -------------------------------------------------
$UPLOAD_DIR    = __DIR__ . '/uploads/';
$CONVERT_DIR   = __DIR__ . '/converted/';
$MAX_UPLOAD_MB = 200;                 // max size you want to allow
$ALLOWED_MIMES = [
    'video/mp4',
    'video/mpeg',
    'video/quicktime',
    'video/x-ms-wmv',
    'video/x-msvideo',
    'video/webm',
    // add more if you wish
];

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Helper: pretty‑print errors and stop execution
function abort($msg) {
    echo "<h2 style='color:#c33;'>Error: $msg</h2>";
    exit;
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST' && isset($_FILES['video'])) {

    // 1️⃣  Basic sanity checks
    if ($_FILES['video']['error'] !== UPLOAD_ERR_OK) {
        abort('Upload error (code ' . $_FILES['video']['error'] . ')');
    }

    // 2️⃣  Size limit
    $maxBytes = $MAX_UPLOAD_MB * 1024 * 1024;
    if ($_FILES['video']['size'] > $maxBytes) {
        abort("File exceeds the $MAX_UPLOAD_MB MB size limit.");
    }

    // 3️⃣  MIME‑type validation (relies on PHP’s built‑in detection)
    $finfo = new finfo(FILEINFO_MIME_TYPE);
    $mime  = $finfo->file($_FILES['video']['tmp_name']);
    if (!in_array($mime, $ALLOWED_MIMES, true)) {
        abort('Unsupported file type: ' . htmlspecialchars($mime));
    }

    // 4️⃣  Generate safe names
    $origName = preg_replace('/[^a-zA-Z0-9_\-\.]/', '_', $_FILES['video']['name']);
    $baseName = pathinfo($origName, PATHINFO_FILENAME);
    $uploadPath = $UPLOAD_DIR . uniqid($baseName . '_', true);

    // 5️⃣  Move the uploaded file out of the PHP temp folder
    if (!move_uploaded_file($_FILE['video']['tmp_name'], $uploadPath)) {
        abort('Failed to move uploaded file.');
    }

    // 6️⃣  Prepare output file name
    $outputPath = $CONVERT_DIR . $baseName . '_' . time() . '.mp4';

    // 7️⃣  Build the FFmpeg command safely
    //   -c:v libx264   : H.264 video
    //   -preset veryfast : encoder speed vs. compression trade‑off
    //   -crf 23        : constant‑rate‑factor (quality, 0‑51, lower = better)
    //   -c:a aac       : AAC audio
    //   -b:a 128k      : audio bitrate
    $ffmpeg = 'ffmpeg'; // replace with absolute path if needed
    $cmd = sprintf(
        '%s -i %s -c:v libx264 -preset veryfast -crf 23 -c:a aac -b:a 128k -y %s 2>&1',
        escapeshellcmd($ffmpeg),
        escapeshellarg($uploadPath),
        escapeshellarg($outputPath)
    );

    // 8️⃣  Run FFmpeg, capture its output (use exec() or shell_exec())
    $output = [];
    $returnVar = null;
    exec($cmd, $output, $returnVar);

    // 9️⃣  Check conversion success
    if ($returnVar !== 0 || !file_exists($outputPath)) {
        // Keep the raw upload for debugging (or delete it)
        abort('FFmpeg failed. Return code: ' . $returnVar . '<br>Output:<br>' .
               nl2br(htmlspecialchars(implode("\n", $output))));
    }

    // 🔟  Clean up the original upload (optional but recommended)
    @unlink($uploadPath);

    // 1️⃣1️⃣  Emit a tiny page that triggers the download automatically
    $downloadUrl = 'download.php?file=' . urlencode(basename($outputPath));
    ?>
    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <html lang="en">
    <head>
        <meta charset="utf-8">
        <title>Conversion complete – starting download…</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <p>Conversion finished. Your download should start automatically.</p>
        <script>
            // Create an invisible link and "click" it
            const a = document.createElement('a');
            a.href = <?= json_encode($downloadUrl) ?>;
            a.download = <?= json_encode(basename($outputPath)) ?>;
            document.body.appendChild(a);
            a.click();
            // Optionally redirect after a few seconds
            setTimeout(() => { window.location = 'index.php'; }, 5000);
        </script>
    </body>
    </html>
    <?php
    exit;
}

// ---------- If we reach here – show the upload form -------------------------
?>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>Video → MP4 Converter</title>
    <style>
        body {font-family:Arial,sans-serif; margin:2em;}
        .container {max-width:500px; margin:auto;}
        .note {color:#777; font-size:0.9em;}
    </style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">
    <h1>Upload a video to convert to MP4</h1>
    <form action="" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data">
        <label for="video">Select video file (max <?= $MAX_UPLOAD_MB ?> MiB):</label><br><br>
        <input type="file" name="video" id="video" accept="video/*" required><br><br>
        <button type="submit">Convert</button>
    </form>
    <p class="note">Supported formats: <?= implode(', ', $ALLOWED_MIMES) ?></p>
</div>
</body>
</html>
```

### `download.php`

```php
<?php
// Simple, safe file‑serve script – never expose arbitrary paths!
$BASE_DIR = __DIR__ . '/converted/';

if (!isset($_GET['file'])) {
    http_response_code(400);
    exit('Missing file parameter.');
}

// Sanitize filename: allow only alphanumerics, dash, underscore, dot
$filename = preg_replace('/[^a-zA-Z0-9_\-\.]/', '', $_GET['file']);
$filepath = realpath($BASE_DIR . $filename);

if ($filepath === false || strpos($filepath, $BASE_DIR) !== 0 || !file_exists($filepath)) {
    http_response_code(404);
    exit('File not found.');
}

// Send appropriate headers
header('Content-Type: video/mp4');
header('Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="'.basename($filepath).'"');
header('Content-Length: ' . filesize($filepath));
header('Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate');

// Stream the file (readfile is fine for small files; for large files you may want a chunked read)
readfile($filepath);
exit;
?>
```

---

## 4. Why the code looks the way it does

| Step | What we’re doing & why it matters |
|------|-----------------------------------|
| **MIME‑type validation** | Prevents users from uploading arbitrary files (e.g., PHP scripts). Using `finfo` is more reliable than trusting the client‑side `accept` attribute. |
| **Size limit** | Stops huge uploads that would consume disk or CPU. Adjust `$MAX_UPLOAD_MB` to match your environment. |
| **Safe filenames** | Removes dangerous characters, then prefixes a random `uniqid()` for the temporary upload and a timestamp for the final MP4. This prevents collisions and path‑traversal attacks. |
| **`escapeshellarg()` & `escapeshellcmd()`** | Guarantees that the FFmpeg command cannot be hijacked by a crafted filename. |
| **`exec()` capture** | Gives us FFmpeg’s console output for debugging; in production you may log this to a file instead of showing it to the user. |
| **Separate `download.php`** | The auto‑download page only knows the *file name*; the script checks that it lives inside the known `converted/` directory and streams it with correct headers, preventing directory‑traversal attacks. |
| **Automatic download** | A tiny inline JavaScript creates an `<a>` element with the `download` attribute and clicks it, which triggers the browser’s “save as” dialog (or silent download if the user’s settings allow it). |
| **Cleanup** | After conversion, the original upload is deleted (`unlink`). You may also want a cron job that removes old files from `converted/` after a certain retention period. |

---

## 5. **Production‑grade hardening checklist**

| Area | Recommendation |
|------|----------------|
| **File type** | Consider using a **whitelist of extensions** (`.mov`, `.avi`, `.webm`, …) and/or running `ffprobe` to confirm the file really is a video. |
| **Size & duration** | Enforce a *maximum duration* (e.g., 30 min) by probing with `ffprobe` before conversion. |
| **Execution sandbox** | Run FFmpeg inside a **restricted container** (Docker, Firejail, or a dedicated user with `nosuid`, `noexec`, etc.) to limit what it can access if a malformed file triggers a bug. |
| **Rate limiting** | Use IP‑based throttling (e.g., `mod_evasive`, Nginx limit‑req) to stop abuse. |
| **CSRF protection** | Add a hidden token to the `<form>` and verify it server‑side. |
| **HTTPS** | Serve the site over TLS so uploaded video content (which can be sensitive) isn’t sent in clear text. |
| **Logging & monitoring** | Log every upload (IP, timestamp, original name, conversion result) to a secure location. Watch for spikes or repeated failures. |
| **Temporary storage** | Use a **separate filesystem** (or `tmpfs`) for `uploads/` that is cleared on reboot, to avoid persisting raw user content longer than needed. |
| **File permissions** | `chmod 600` on individual uploaded files; directory permissions should be limited to the web‑server user only. |
| **Output filename collisions** | The code already adds a timestamp/uniqid; for added safety store a hash of the original file in the filename. |
| **Timeouts** | Run FFmpeg with a timeout (e.g., `timeout 300 ffmpeg …`) to stop runaway conversions. |
| **Error handling** | In production hide raw FFmpeg output from the user; write it to a server log instead and present a generic “conversion failed” message. |

---

## 6. How to test it locally

```bash
# 1. Clone / copy the files into your web-root
cd /var/www/video-converter
# (Place index.php, download.php, create the two folders as described)

# 2. Give the webserver write permission
chown -R www-data:www-data uploads converted   # adjust for your user

# 3. Make sure ffmpeg works
ffmpeg -version

# 4. Open a browser and go to:
http://localhost/video-converter/index.php
# Select a small .mov, .avi, .webm, etc., click "Convert"…
# You should see the conversion page and then an automatic download prompt.
```

---

### TL;DR

*Upload → FFmpeg → MP4 → auto‑download* can be built in **under 100 lines of PHP** (plus a tiny HTML/JS wrapper).  
The script above gives you a functional prototype **and** highlights the key security measures you need to adopt before using it in any publicly‑exposed environment. Happy (and safe) coding!

