## 2. Full script (cURL version – recommended)

```php
<?php
/**
 * Download a remote video and save it locally.
 *
 * @param string $url           Remote video URL
 * @param string $savePath      Full path (including filename) where the file will be stored
 * @param int    $timeoutSec    Connection timeout in seconds
 * @return bool                  true on success, false on failure
 */
function downloadVideo(string $url, string $savePath, int $timeoutSec = 60): bool
{
    // Open a file handle for writing binary data
    $fp = fopen($savePath, 'wb');
    if ($fp === false) {
        error_log("❌ Cannot open local file for writing: $savePath");
        return false;
    }

    // Initialise cURL
    $ch = curl_init($url);
    curl_setopt_array($ch, [
        CURLOPT_FILE           => $fp,          // write directly to file
        CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION => true,         // follow redirects (important for CDN links)
        CURLOPT_TIMEOUT        => $timeoutSec,  // overall timeout
        CURLOPT_CONNECTTIMEOUT => 15,           // connection timeout
        CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER => true,         // verify SSL certs
        CURLOPT_FAILONERROR    => true,         // treat HTTP >=400 as error
        CURLOPT_USERAGENT      => 'Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; PHP Downloader)', // polite UA
    ]);

    // Execute the request
    $success = curl_exec($ch);

    // Check for cURL errors
    if ($success === false) {
        $err = curl_error($ch);
        error_log("❌ cURL error while downloading $url: $err");
        $success = false;
    }

    // Close everything
    curl_close($ch);
    fclose($fp);

    // Verify that we got a non‑zero file size (optional sanity check)
    if ($success && filesize($savePath) > 0) {
        return true;
    }

    // If something went wrong, delete the possibly incomplete file
    @unlink($savePath);
    return false;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Configuration – edit these values to suit your case
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

// The exact URL you posted (you can also read it from a GET/POST param)
$remoteUrl = 'https://video-cdn.aliexpress-media.com/80ffc57ef0293a3a/ae_sg_item/Bub4DyQ6ex0zl0T8ki7_1100096979227_mp4_264_sd.mp4?auth_key=1691057774-0-0-025e6559104936678796132b513c268d&w=540&h=714&e=sd&t=2141276f16832817742928523ef31f&b=ae_sg&p=ae_sg_ae_sg_vpc_scene&tr=mp4-264-sd&t_u=2141281916848399720631508e566f';

// Where you want to store the file on the local server
$saveDirectory = __DIR__ . '/downloads';               // create sub‑folder "downloads"
if (!is_dir($saveDirectory)) {
    mkdir($saveDirectory, 0755, true);
}

// Derive a sane filename (remove query string, keep extension)
$parsed = parse_url($remoteUrl);
$basename = basename($parsed['path']);                // e.g. "Bub4DyQ6ex0zl0T8ki7_1100096979227_mp4_264_sd.mp4"
$localPath = $saveDirectory . '/' . $basename;

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------

$result = downloadVideo($remoteUrl, $localPath);

if ($result) {
    echo "✅ Video downloaded successfully to: $localPath\n";
} else {
    echo "❌ Failed to download the video.\n";
}
?>
```

### How it works
| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **1️⃣** | The script opens a local file (`fopen`) for binary writing (`wb`). |
| **2️⃣** | `curl_init()` prepares a request to the remote URL. |
| **3️⃣** | `CURLOPT_FILE` points cURL directly to the opened file handle – this streams data straight to disk, avoiding large memory consumption. |
| **4️⃣** | `CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION` follows any 302/301 redirects (common on CDNs). |
| **5️⃣** | After `curl_exec()` finishes, we check for errors, close handles, and optionally validate that the file size is > 0. |
| **6️⃣** | On success you get a local `.mp4` file identical to the source; on failure the partially‑written file is removed. |

---

## 3. Simpler version (using `file_get_contents`)

If your PHP configuration has `allow_url_fopen=On` **and** the video size is modest (a few MB), you can use an even shorter script:

```php
