            Daftar Sekarang untuk <?= htmlspecialchars($brand) ?>
        </a>
    </section>
</body>
</html>
<?php
// End of file
```

### What changed compared to your original script?

| Original behaviour | New, **secure** behaviour |
|--------------------|--------------------------|
| Directly read a *local* text file `brandlist.txt`. | No local file needed – the brand list lives on the remote API and is *whitelisted* there. |
| Hard‑coded HTML placeholders (`path_to_logo`, …). | Dynamically fetched URLs (`$logoUrl`, `$bannerUrl`, `$registerUrl`). |
| No validation of external data → potential XSS/HTML injection. | Every URL must pass `FILTER_VALIDATE_URL` + “HTTPS only” check before being echoed. |
| No guarantee that the brand actually exists (case‑insensitive string compare). | Remote API returns **404** if the brand is unknown; we treat any non‑200 response as “not found”. |
| No HTTPS enforcement on outbound request. | `CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER` + `CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYHOST` are turned on; the API URL uses `https://`. |
| Potential for **Open Redirect** because the user can control `$registerUrl` blindly. | We verify that the URL is well‑formed and **HTTPS**, and we could add a host whitelist (e.g., only `*.example.com`). |

---

## 3️⃣  Security‑Research‑Perspective – Things to Watch Out For

| Issue | Why it matters | How the code mitigates it |
|-------|----------------|---------------------------|
| **Server‑Side Request Forgery (SSRF)** | If an attacker can influence the URL you call, they could make the server request internal services. | The API URL is fully under our control (`https://assets.example.com/...`). No user‑controlled parts are concatenated **except a safe, alphanumeric brand name** that is validated before insertion. |
| **Remote File Inclusion (RFI)** | Including remote PHP files can lead to code execution. | We **never** `include` or `require` a remote file. The remote endpoint returns **JSON**, not PHP code. |
| **Open Redirect / Untrusted Link** | If you echo a link that the attacker controls, they can send victims to malicious sites. | `safeUrl()` enforces `https://` and could be extended to allow only specific domains (`parse_url($url, PHP_URL_HOST) === 'assets.example.com'`). |
| **Cross‑Site Scripting (XSS)** | Injected HTML/JS could run in the visitor’s browser. | All output is escaped with `htmlspecialchars()`. URLs are validated **before** escaping (so they can be safe to use as `src`/`href`). |
| **Content‑Security‑Policy (CSP) Bypass** | Without CSP an attacker could load malicious assets from elsewhere. | A **strict CSP** header is added in the example, limiting images and links to the known assets host. |
| **Denial‑of‑Service (DoS) via Slow Remote API** | If the remote API hangs, your page could hang for users. | `CURLOPT_TIMEOUT` (5 s) limits the total wait time. You can also implement a fallback static asset set. |
| **Information Leakage** | Detailed error messages can give attackers clues. | Errors are logged on the server (`error_log`) but the user only sees a generic 404 page. |
| **Replay / Tampering** | An attacker could intercept the JSON and modify URLs. | Use **HTTPS** for confidentiality and integrity. For extra protection, you could add an **HMAC signature** to the JSON payload that the client verifies. |

